window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("agent-frameworks", {"topics":[{"key":"agent-frameworks/choosing-an-agent-framework","sources":["Official docs: LangGraph","Official docs: LlamaIndex","Official docs: OpenAI Agents SDK","Official docs: Claude Agent SDK","Official docs: CrewAI","Official docs: Pydantic AI"],"why":"- **The loop is small; the rest is not.** A tool-calling loop is about 30 lines. Checkpointing, resume\n  after a crash, human approval, streaming partial output, parallel branches and tracing are each weeks of\n  work to build well.\n- **Frameworks buy that rest, at a price.** You inherit their abstractions, their release cadence and\n  sometimes their prompts. LangChain's early agents parsed model text with hidden prompts, which is why\n  many teams now prefer thinner layers.\n- **Providers now ship their own harnesses.** The OpenAI Agents SDK and the Claude Agent SDK give a\n  supported loop with tools, handoffs or subagents and tracing, tuned for one vendor's models.\n- **The naive choice fails late.** Teams pick the framework with the best tutorial, then discover at\n  month three that it cannot persist a paused run or that its trace format does not fit their stack.","how":"### The decision is about layers, not brands\n| Layer you need | Build it yourself when | Reach for |\n|---|---|---|\n| The loop and tool dispatch | always feasible, 30-100 lines | raw provider SDK, LiteLLM |\n| Typed tools and validated outputs | you want type checking and retries on bad output | Pydantic AI, Instructor |\n| Durable state, pause and resume, branching | runs last minutes to days or need approvals | LangGraph, a durable workflow engine |\n| Retrieval over many documents | data plumbing dominates | LlamaIndex, LangChain retrievers |\n| Role-based multi-agent teams | you are prototyping a crew of roles quickly | CrewAI, AutoGen / AG2 |\n| A full agent harness (files, shell, MCP, subagents) | you want a coding or ops agent out of the box | Claude Agent SDK |\n| Provider-native handoffs, guardrails, tracing | you are committed to one provider | OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK |\n\n### The candidates in one line each\n- **Raw SDK loop.** Full control and the fewest surprises; you build persistence, retries and tracing.\n- **LangGraph.** Agents as a typed state graph with checkpointers, interrupts and streaming; most control\n  over flow, more code for simple cases.\n- **LlamaIndex.** Data-first: loaders, indexes and query engines, plus agents and event-driven workflows.\n- **OpenAI Agents SDK.** `Agent`, `Runner`, function tools, handoffs, guardrails, tool approvals and\n  built-in tracing.\n- **Claude Agent SDK.** The harness behind Claude Code: built-in tools, MCP servers, subagents, hooks and\n  permission modes.\n- **CrewAI.** Agents with a role, goal and backstory, assigned tasks in a crew; Flows for explicit control.\n- **Pydantic AI.** Typed agents with dependency injection and validated structured output, model-agnostic.\n\n### A scoring procedure that holds up\n1. **Write the hard requirements first:** longest run time, need for human approval, multi-agent or not,\n   languages (Python, TypeScript), hosting limits (VPC, no SaaS tracing).\n2. **Shortlist two candidates** that cover them natively, plus the raw-SDK baseline.\n3. **Build the same thin slice** in each: one tool, one approval, one resume after a killed process.\n4. **Run your eval set** (20-50 cases) on each and compare pass rate, tokens per task and p95 latency.\n5. **Check the exit cost:** can tools, prompts and evals move without rewrites?","example":"### The baseline every framework wraps\nRuns as-is; `call_model` stands in for one provider SDK call with tools attached. If a framework cannot\nbeat this on your requirements, you do not need it yet.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nTOOLS = {\"order_status\": lambda order_id: {\"A-1001\": \"shipped, arrives Friday\"}.get(order_id, \"not found\")}\n\ndef call_model(messages):               # stand-in for one provider SDK call with tools=[...] attached\n    if messages[-1][\"role\"] == \"user\":\n        return {\"tool_calls\": [{\"id\": \"c1\", \"name\": \"order_status\", \"args\": {\"order_id\": \"A-1001\"}}]}\n    return {\"content\": \"Order A-1001 has shipped and arrives Friday.\", \"tool_calls\": []}\n\ndef run_agent(question, max_steps=8):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Support agent. Use tools for order data.\"},\n                {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}]\n    for step in range(max_steps):                        # the step budget a framework would hide\n        reply = call_model(messages)\n        messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", **reply})\n        if not reply[\"tool_calls\"]:\n            return reply[\"content\"], step + 1\n        for call in reply[\"tool_calls\"]:                 # execute, then feed results back\n            try:\n                result = TOOLS[call[\"name\"]](**call[\"args\"])\n            except Exception as e:                       # errors go back to the model, not up the stack\n                result = f\"error: {e}\"\n            messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"tool_call_id\": call[\"id\"], \"content\": json.dumps(result)})\n    raise RuntimeError(\"step budget exhausted\")\n\nprint(run_agent(\"Where is order A-1001?\"))   # ('Order A-1001 has shipped and arrives Friday.', 2)\n```\n\n### The same agent in a framework (Pydantic AI)\n`TestModel` calls every tool with generated arguments, so this runs offline; swap in a provider string\nsuch as `\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\"` for real use. The schema comes from the type hints and docstring.\n\n```python\n# pip install pydantic-ai-slim\nfrom pydantic_ai import Agent\nfrom pydantic_ai.models.test import TestModel\n\nagent = Agent(TestModel(), instructions=\"Support agent. Use tools for order data.\")\n\n@agent.tool_plain\ndef order_status(order_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Shipping status for an order id.\"\"\"\n    return \"shipped\"\n\nresult = agent.run_sync(\"Where is A-1001?\")\nprint(result.output)          # TestModel echoes tool results: {\"order_status\":\"shipped\"}\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: a refund agent for a fintech\nRequirements: refunds over 100 need a human approval that may take a day; the team is Python; traces must\nstay in their VPC; one provider today, maybe two next year.\n\n| Option | Approval that survives a restart | Multi-provider | Self-hosted tracing | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Raw SDK loop | build it (state table, resume logic) | via LiteLLM | any OTel backend | viable, most code |\n| LangGraph | `interrupt()` plus a Postgres checkpointer | yes | LangSmith self-hosted, Langfuse | best fit |\n| OpenAI Agents SDK | `needs_approval` tools; you store and reload the serialized `RunState` | partial | custom trace processors | workable, more glue |\n| CrewAI | human input exists; durable resume weaker | yes | yes | prototype only |\n\nThe team picked LangGraph because the durable pause is built in, not bolted on, and kept tools as plain\nfunctions, so the exit cost stays low.","patterns":["**Keep tools framework-free.** Write tools as plain typed functions in your own package and adapt them with a one-line decorator per framework. Monitor nothing new; the payoff is a migration measured in days.","**Put a gateway under the framework.** Route model calls through LiteLLM or your own gateway for keys, rate limits, fallbacks and cost logs, so swapping frameworks does not lose them. Monitor cost per task.","**Pin versions and read changelogs.** Agent frameworks ship breaking changes often; pin exact versions, upgrade on a branch, and gate the upgrade on your eval set passing.","**Run a bake-off on your eval set.** Build the same thin slice in two candidates and compare pass rate, tokens per task and p95 latency on 20-50 real cases before committing.","**Own the trace format.** Prefer frameworks that export OpenTelemetry or have a pluggable trace sink, so traces land in your observability stack even if you change frameworks."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The framework decides how state is persisted, how a human pauses a run,\n  how traces look and how hard it is to leave. Those are expensive to change after launch.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Between your provider SDK (or an LLM gateway such as LiteLLM) and\n  your app: it owns the loop, tool dispatch, memory, streaming and often tracing.\n- **What you control.** Whether you take a framework at all, which layer it owns, and an exit plan: your\n  tools, prompts and eval set should not depend on it.\n- **What you can skip.** Ranking frameworks by GitHub stars or demo count. Run the same 20-case eval on two\n  candidates instead.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Greenfield agent kickoff.** Teams run a one-week bake-off of two frameworks plus a raw loop on the same\n  thin slice before committing.\n- **Approval-heavy workflows.** Refunds, account changes and procurement agents pick a framework for\n  durable pause and resume first and everything else second.\n- **Retrieval-first assistants.** Knowledge-base agents choose data tooling (LlamaIndex, LangChain\n  retrievers) and keep orchestration minimal.\n- **Vendor-committed products.** Teams on one provider adopt its agent SDK for faster access to new model\n  features, with tools kept portable.","mini_project":"**Framework bake-off on one thin slice (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** choose between a raw SDK loop and one framework with evidence, not opinion.\n- **Steps:** write two plain tools (`order_status`, `issue_refund`) and 10 eval cases; build the agent as a\n  raw loop and in one framework (LangGraph or Pydantic AI); add an approval before `issue_refund`; kill the\n  process mid-approval and try to resume; log pass rate, tokens and latency per case.\n- **Done when:** you have a table comparing both on pass rate, tokens per task, lines of glue code and\n  whether resume worked, plus a one-paragraph recommendation with its exit plan."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q1[\"Runs pause, resume or need approval?\"] -->|\"yes\"| LG[\"LangGraph or durable workflow\"]\n    Q1 -->|\"no\"| Q2[\"Mostly retrieval over documents?\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"yes\"| LI[\"LlamaIndex or LangChain retrievers\"]\n    Q2 -->|\"no\"| Q3[\"Committed to one provider?\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"yes\"| VS[\"Vendor SDK: OpenAI or Claude\"]\n    Q3 -->|\"no\"| Q4[\"Need typed outputs and DI?\"]\n    Q4 -->|\"yes\"| PA[\"Pydantic AI\"]\n    Q4 -->|\"no\"| RAW[\"Raw SDK loop plus LiteLLM\"]","caption":"Read it top-down: each question removes options, and the raw SDK stays a valid answer at every level."},"interview":[{"q":"What does an agent framework give you that a raw provider SDK does not?","a":"- **The same loop, plus the hard parts.** Persistence after every step, pause and resume for humans,\n  streaming of tokens and steps, parallel branches, retries and tracing.\n- **What it does not give.** Better reasoning. The model and your tools decide quality; a framework only\n  changes how much infrastructure you write around them."},{"q":"Your team is split between LangGraph and the OpenAI Agents SDK. How do you decide?","a":"- **Write the hard requirements:** durable approvals, provider neutrality, hosting and trace residency.\n- **Build a thin slice in both** (one tool, one approval, one resume after a killed process) and run the\n  same 20-50 case eval.\n- **Decide on evidence:** pass rate, tokens and p95 latency per task, plus how much glue code each needed."},{"q":"When would you not use a framework at all?","a":"- **Short, stateless agents.** One loop, a few tools, answers in seconds: a 50-line loop is easier to read,\n  test and tune than any abstraction.\n- **Heavy prompt control or unusual providers.** When you must see every token sent, or the framework\n  lags a model feature you need, the raw SDK wins."},{"q":"How do you keep a framework choice reversible?","a":"- **Tools and prompts live outside it** as plain typed functions and versioned files, adapted by thin\n  wrappers.\n- **Model calls go through a gateway** so keys, fallbacks and cost logs survive a switch.\n- **The eval set is framework-neutral**, so you can prove a migration did not regress quality."},{"q":"A CrewAI prototype works in demos but costs 4x more tokens than expected in production. What do you check?","a":"- **Count agents and turns per task.** Role-based crews often pass full context between several agents;\n  each hop re-sends the transcript.\n- **Then try fewer roles.** Collapse to one agent with the same tools, rerun the eval, and keep the crew\n  only if pass rate drops without it."}],"tradeoffs":["**Control vs speed to first demo.** A raw loop shows every prompt and retry; a framework gives approval, streaming and persistence in days. Flips when requirements are simple and stable.","**Provider SDK vs neutral framework.** Vendor SDKs track new model features first and are tuned for them; neutral frameworks let you switch providers. Flips once you need a second provider for cost or outage cover.","**Batteries vs debuggability.** Prebuilt agents and crews hide prompts; when output is wrong you debug someone else's prompt. Explicit graphs cost more code but keep every step visible.","**Hosted platform vs self-run.** Managed deployment (LangSmith Deployment, formerly LangGraph Platform, or vendor runtimes) removes ops work but adds a bill and a data-residency question."],"pitfalls":["**Choosing by tutorial.** Symptom: month-three rewrite when you need resume-after-crash. Fix: list hard requirements first and test a killed-process resume in the bake-off.","**Letting the framework own your prompts.** Symptom: quality changes after a minor upgrade. Fix: keep system prompts in your repo or prompt registry and pass them in explicitly.","**Mixing two orchestration frameworks.** Symptom: two state models, two trace trees, retries at both levels. Fix: one orchestrator; other libraries only as components (a retriever, a validator).","**Multi-agent by default.** Symptom: three agents doing one agent's job at triple the tokens. Fix: start with one agent and add roles only when an eval shows a single agent failing."],"when":"- **Use a raw SDK loop when** the agent is one loop with a few tools, runs in seconds, and you want full\n  control of prompts and cost.\n- **Use a framework when** you need durable pause and resume, parallel branches, multi-agent routing or a\n  harness with files, shell and MCP that you would otherwise build.\n- **Avoid deciding without** a thin-slice bake-off on your own eval set and a written exit plan.\n- **Measure:** pass rate, tokens and latency per task, and lines of glue code you own.\n- **Interviews:** expect \"why this framework and not a raw loop?\"; answer with requirements, not features.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the thin slice in two candidates, keeps tools and prompts framework-free, and reports pass rate, tokens and latency per task from the same eval set.","architect":"Writes the hard requirements (durability, approvals, hosting, languages) and the exit plan, and decides which layer the framework is allowed to own.","fde":"Maps a customer's constraints, such as VPC-only tracing or an existing provider contract, to a framework choice they can operate after the engagement ends."},"check":{"q":"A team wants an internal agent that answers HR questions from 2,000 policy PDFs, with no actions\nand answers in under 5 seconds. Another wants an agent that issues refunds over 100 only after a manager\napproves, which can take a day. Would you pick the same framework for both?","a":"No. The HR agent is retrieval-heavy and stateless: a raw SDK loop or a single LlamaIndex or\nLangChain retrieval pipeline is enough, and a heavy orchestration layer adds latency and code without\nbenefit. The refund agent needs a durable pause that survives restarts, an approval payload, and resume on\nthe same thread, which LangGraph gives with `interrupt()` and a Postgres checkpointer, or a durable\nworkflow engine gives around a raw loop. In both cases keep tools as plain functions and run the same eval\nset before committing, so the choice can be revisited."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the strongest reason to adopt LangGraph instead of a raw SDK loop?","options":["It makes the model reason better on multi-step tasks","You need runs to pause for human approval and resume from persisted state after a restart","It removes the need for tool schemas","It is the only framework that supports tool calling"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LangGraph's checkpointers and interrupts give durable pause and resume. Reasoning quality comes from the model and prompts, and every framework, including a raw loop, supports tool calling."},{"q":"Which practice most reduces the cost of switching agent frameworks later?","options":["Using the framework's prebuilt agent for every workflow","Storing prompts inside framework components","Keeping tools as plain typed functions and the eval set framework-neutral","Combining two orchestration frameworks so either can be dropped"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Plain tools adapt to any framework with a thin wrapper, and a neutral eval set proves a migration did not regress. Prompts inside components and mixed orchestrators both increase lock-in."},{"q":"A team needs an agent with built-in file editing, shell access, MCP servers and subagents for an internal coding workflow. Which is the most direct fit?","options":["CrewAI","LlamaIndex","Claude Agent SDK","A LangChain LCEL chain"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Claude Agent SDK packages the agent harness behind Claude Code, with built-in tools, MCP support, subagents and permission controls. The others would need that harness built on top."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/langchain","sources":["Official docs: LangChain","Official docs: LangChain Core (Runnable interface)","Official docs: LangGraph"],"why":"- **Every provider speaks a slightly different dialect.** Message roles, tool-call formats and streaming\n  events differ. LangChain normalizes them into `AIMessage.tool_calls`, so swapping a model is ideally a\n  one-line change.\n- **RAG needs many small adapters.** Loaders, splitters, embedding models and vector stores each have\n  their own SDK; LangChain wraps hundreds of them behind `Document`, `Embeddings`, `VectorStore` and\n  retriever interfaces.\n- **Composition needs uniform plumbing.** Streaming, batching, async and retries should work the same on a\n  prompt, a model and a parser. The Runnable interface gives all of them `invoke`, `batch`, `stream` and\n  async twins.\n- **The early agent API was too magic.** Text-parsed ReAct agents with hidden prompts were hard to debug,\n  which pushed agents onto LangGraph; `create_agent` is the thin, tool-calling successor.","how":"### Packages\n| Package | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `langchain-core` | messages, Runnables, prompts, tools, `Document`, base interfaces |\n| `langchain` | `create_agent`, middleware, `init_chat_model` |\n| `langchain-openai`, `langchain-anthropic`, ... | one package per provider integration |\n| `langchain-community` | long tail of community integrations |\n\n### Chat models and messages\n- **One interface.** `init_chat_model(\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\")` (or a provider class) returns a chat model\n  with `invoke`, `stream`, `batch` and `bind_tools`.\n- **Typed messages.** `SystemMessage`, `HumanMessage`, `AIMessage` (with `.tool_calls` and\n  `.usage_metadata`) and `ToolMessage` (with `tool_call_id`).\n- **Structured output.** `model.with_structured_output(MyPydanticModel)` uses the provider's native\n  mechanism where available and returns a validated object.\n\n### Tools\n- **`@tool` turns a function into a schema.** Name from the function, description from the docstring,\n  arguments from the type hints (`args_schema` is a Pydantic model).\n- **`model.bind_tools([...])`** attaches the schemas to every call; the model replies with `tool_calls`\n  that you, `ToolNode` or `create_agent` execute.\n\n### LCEL: the pipe syntax\n- **`a | b | c` builds a `RunnableSequence`.** Each step's output is the next step's input.\n- **A dict is a parallel step.** `{\"context\": retriever, \"question\": RunnablePassthrough()}` runs both\n  branches on the same input and returns a dict.\n- **Every Runnable has** `with_retry`, `with_fallbacks`, `with_config` (tags, run name) and\n  `astream_events` for fine-grained streaming.\n- **Use LCEL for linear pipelines.** Loops, branches with state and pauses belong in LangGraph.\n\n### Retrievers\n- **A retriever is a Runnable from a query string to `list[Document]`.** `vector_store.as_retriever(\n  search_kwargs={\"k\": 4})` is the common one; BM25, ensembles and rerankers fit the same interface.\n- **Agents use them two ways:** always retrieve before answering (a chain), or expose retrieval as a tool\n  the model decides to call (agentic RAG).\n\n### `create_agent`\n- **The loop as a function.** `create_agent(model, tools, system_prompt=...)` returns a compiled LangGraph\n  graph: model, tools, repeat until no tool calls.\n- **Middleware hooks the loop.** LangChain 1.x ships middleware such as `HumanInTheLoopMiddleware`,\n  `SummarizationMiddleware` and `ModelCallLimitMiddleware`; check the current docs for their arguments.\n- **It inherits LangGraph features:** pass `checkpointer=` for memory and resume, stream with\n  `stream_mode`, and trace in LangSmith.","example":"### LCEL retrieval chain (runs offline)\nA toy embedding and a scripted model let this run without keys; the pipeline is the same with real ones.\n\n```python\n# pip install langchain-core numpy ; runs offline (toy embeddings, scripted model)\nfrom langchain_core.documents import Document\nfrom langchain_core.embeddings import Embeddings\nfrom langchain_core.vectorstores import InMemoryVectorStore\nfrom langchain_core.prompts import ChatPromptTemplate\nfrom langchain_core.output_parsers import StrOutputParser\nfrom langchain_core.runnables import RunnablePassthrough\nfrom langchain_core.language_models.fake_chat_models import GenericFakeChatModel\nfrom langchain_core.messages import AIMessage\n\nclass ToyEmbeddings(Embeddings):          # bag of words; use a real embedding model in practice\n    VOCAB = [\"refund\", \"return\", \"days\", \"shipping\", \"eu\", \"delivery\"]\n    def embed_query(self, text):\n        return [float(w in text.lower()) for w in self.VOCAB]\n    def embed_documents(self, texts):\n        return [self.embed_query(t) for t in texts]\n\nstore = InMemoryVectorStore(embedding=ToyEmbeddings())\nstore.add_documents([\n    Document(\"Refunds: return items within 30 days of delivery.\", metadata={\"src\": \"policy.md\"}),\n    Document(\"Shipping to the EU takes 5-7 business days.\", metadata={\"src\": \"shipping.md\"}),\n])\nretriever = store.as_retriever(search_kwargs={\"k\": 1})      # a Runnable: str -> list[Document]\n\ndef format_docs(docs):\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"[{d.metadata['src']}] {d.page_content}\" for d in docs)\n\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_messages([\n    (\"system\", \"Answer only from the context and cite the [source].\\n\\nContext:\\n{context}\"),\n    (\"human\", \"{question}\"),\n])\nllm = GenericFakeChatModel(messages=iter([AIMessage(\"Within 30 days of delivery [policy.md].\")]))\n# real model: from langchain.chat_models import init_chat_model; llm = init_chat_model(\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\")\n\nchain = (\n    {\"context\": retriever | format_docs, \"question\": RunnablePassthrough()}   # dict runs both branches\n    | prompt | llm | StrOutputParser()\n)\nprint(retriever.invoke(\"Can I return it after 20 days?\")[0].metadata)   # {'src': 'policy.md'}\nprint(chain.invoke(\"Can I return it after 20 days?\"))\n```\n\n### A tool-calling agent with `create_agent`\nThe scripted model replays a tool call and then an answer, so the loop runs offline. Pass a provider\nstring instead for real use.\n\n```python\n# pip install langchain ; offline: a scripted model stands in for a real one\nfrom langchain.agents import create_agent\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom langchain_core.messages import AIMessage\nfrom langchain_core.language_models.fake_chat_models import GenericFakeChatModel\n\n@tool\ndef order_status(order_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Look up the shipping status of an order by its id, e.g. 'A-1001'.\"\"\"\n    return {\"A-1001\": \"shipped, arrives Friday\"}.get(order_id, \"not found\")\n\nclass ScriptedModel(GenericFakeChatModel):          # replays canned replies; ignores bound tools\n    def bind_tools(self, tools, **kwargs):\n        return self\n\nmodel = ScriptedModel(messages=iter([\n    AIMessage(\"\", tool_calls=[{\"name\": \"order_status\", \"args\": {\"order_id\": \"A-1001\"}, \"id\": \"c1\"}]),\n    AIMessage(\"Order A-1001 has shipped and arrives Friday.\"),\n]))\n# real model: model = \"openai:gpt-4o-mini\" (any provider string init_chat_model accepts)\n\nagent = create_agent(model, tools=[order_status],\n                     system_prompt=\"You are a support agent. Use tools; never guess order data.\")\nresult = agent.invoke({\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Where is order A-1001?\"}]})\nfor m in result[\"messages\"]:\n    print(f\"{m.type:>5}: {m.content or m.tool_calls}\")\nprint(order_status.args_schema.model_json_schema()[\"properties\"])\n```\n\n- **Output:** `human`, `ai` (a tool call), `tool` (the result), `ai` (the answer): the loop in four\n  messages.\n- **The last line** shows the JSON schema the model sees, built from the type hint.\n\n### Worked scenario: which LangChain layer for which job\n| Job in a support agent | LangChain piece | Why not something heavier |\n|---|---|---|\n| Answer FAQs from 3,000 help articles | LCEL chain: retriever, prompt, model | no loop needed, one pass is cheaper |\n| Look up orders and open tickets | `create_agent` with 2-4 tools | loop is standard, no custom branching |\n| Refunds needing approval | `create_agent` plus HITL middleware, or a LangGraph graph | needs durable pause and resume |\n| Swap providers for cost | `init_chat_model` string per environment | same message format everywhere |","patterns":["**Install only the integrations you use.** Depend on `langchain-core`, `langchain` and the specific provider packages, pin exact versions, and gate upgrades on your eval set, because minor releases have changed behavior before.","**Wrap model calls in retries and fallbacks.** `llm.with_retry(stop_after_attempt=3).with_fallbacks( [backup_llm])` covers rate limits and outages; monitor fallback rate and cost per request.","**Tag runs for tracing.** `chain.with_config({\"run_name\": \"faq_answer\", \"tags\": [\"faq\", \"v3\"]})` makes LangSmith or Langfuse traces filterable by feature and prompt version.","**Batch offline work.** Use `chain.batch(inputs, config={\"max_concurrency\": 8})` for backfills and eval runs instead of a Python loop, and watch provider rate-limit errors.","**Read usage from messages.** `AIMessage.usage_metadata` carries input and output token counts; log them per step to track agent cost and cache hits."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** It gives one message and tool-call format across providers, so the same\n  agent code can run on several models, and it plugs into LangGraph and LangSmith without glue.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `init_chat_model` for models, `@tool` for tool schemas, vector\n  stores and retrievers for RAG, `create_agent` for the loop (built on LangGraph in LangChain 1.x).\n- **What you control.** Which integration packages you install, the prompts you pass in, middleware on the\n  agent loop, and retries and fallbacks on each Runnable.\n- **What you can skip.** Legacy chains and the old `AgentExecutor`, which LangChain 1.0 moved to the\n  `langchain-classic` package. New agent code uses `create_agent` or LangGraph directly.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Support assistants.** An LCEL retrieval chain answers FAQs while a `create_agent` loop handles order\n  lookups and ticket creation.\n- **Provider portability.** Teams switch models per environment or tenant with `init_chat_model` strings\n  and keep the same tools and prompts.\n- **Agentic RAG.** Retrieval exposed as a tool lets the agent search again with a refined query when the\n  first results are thin.\n- **Bulk enrichment jobs.** `batch` with `max_concurrency` classifies or summarizes thousands of records\n  through the same chain used online.","mini_project":"**Support agent with retrieval and one action (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** combine a retriever and a tool in one LangChain agent and see which one the model picks.\n- **Steps:** load 20 help articles into an in-memory vector store; wrap the retriever as a `search_help`\n  tool; add an `order_status` tool; build the agent with `create_agent` and a real model; write 10 test\n  questions (5 FAQ, 5 order); log tool calls and tokens per question.\n- **Done when:** you have a table showing which tool each question triggered, the answer, and tokens per\n  question, with at least 9 of 10 routed correctly and a note on the one that was not."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    Q[\"User question\"] --> R[\"Retriever: top-k Documents\"]\n    Q --> P[\"ChatPromptTemplate\"]\n    R -->|\"context\"| P\n    P --> M[\"Chat model with bound tools\"]\n    M -->|\"tool_calls\"| T[\"Execute tools\"]\n    T -->|\"ToolMessage\"| M\n    M -->|\"no tool calls\"| O[\"Final AIMessage\"]","caption":"Follow one request: LCEL feeds retrieved context into the prompt, and the agent loop repeats model and\ntool steps until the model stops calling tools."},"interview":[{"q":"What is LCEL and when would you not use it?","a":"- **Definition.** The LangChain Expression Language composes Runnables with `|` into sequences, and with\n  dicts into parallel steps, giving uniform `invoke`, `batch`, `stream` and async.\n- **When not.** Loops, conditional branches with shared state, pauses and multi-agent routing; those are\n  LangGraph's job, and plain Python is clearer for anything branched."},{"q":"How does a LangChain tool become something the model can call?","a":"- **`@tool` builds a schema** from the function name, docstring and type hints into a Pydantic\n  `args_schema`.\n- **`bind_tools` sends the schemas** with each model call in the provider's format; the reply's\n  `tool_calls` are normalized into a list of name, args and id.\n- **Something must execute them:** your loop, `ToolNode` or `create_agent`, returning `ToolMessage`s."},{"q":"Your agent works on one provider but breaks after switching models. What do you check?","a":"- **Tool schema support.** Some models handle nested or optional arguments worse; simplify the schema.\n- **Prompt assumptions.** System prompts tuned for one model may need rework; rerun the eval set.\n- **Provider-specific features** (parallel tool calls, structured-output mode) may differ; check the\n  integration's docs and the raw trace."},{"q":"How do `create_agent`, LangGraph and LangSmith relate?","a":"- **`create_agent`** is a prebuilt LangGraph graph implementing the tool-calling loop, with middleware.\n- **LangGraph** is the runtime: state, checkpointers, interrupts and streaming that `create_agent` uses.\n- **LangSmith** traces and evaluates runs from both, with no code change beyond environment variables."},{"q":"How would you control cost in a LangChain RAG agent serving 100k queries a day?","a":"- **Retrieve less.** Tune `k`, filter by metadata and rerank, so fewer chunks reach the prompt.\n- **Route by difficulty.** A cheap model for easy queries via `init_chat_model`, a stronger one as\n  fallback or for hard cases.\n- **Measure per step** from `usage_metadata` and cache stable prompt prefixes at the provider."}],"tradeoffs":["**Breadth of integrations vs dependency weight.** Hundreds of adapters save glue code, but each package adds transitive dependencies and upgrade risk. Flips when you use one provider and one vector store.","**Uniform interface vs provider features.** The common format lags new provider features (new content block types, special tool types); you sometimes drop to the raw SDK.","**LCEL concision vs readability.** Pipes are short for linear flows but hard to step through when branched; plain Python functions or LangGraph read better past three or four steps.","**`create_agent` speed vs control.** You get a working loop in a few lines; custom routing, parallel branches or several agents need a hand-written LangGraph graph."],"pitfalls":["**Mixing legacy and current APIs.** Symptom: tutorials using `AgentExecutor` or `initialize_agent` fail to import on 1.x or behave oddly. Fix: use `create_agent` or LangGraph; pin `langchain-classic` only while migrating old code.","**Vague tool docstrings.** Symptom: the model calls the wrong tool or invents arguments. Fix: the docstring is the tool description; state when to use it and give an example argument.","**Hidden prompt drift.** Symptom: answers change after an upgrade with no code change. Fix: keep your prompts explicit in your repo and diff traces before and after upgrades.","**Retrieving too much.** Symptom: high token cost and answers citing irrelevant chunks. Fix: tune `k`, add metadata filters and a reranker, and measure retrieval recall separately."],"when":"- **Use when** you want provider-neutral chat models and tools, ready-made RAG integrations, and a quick\n  tool-calling agent that can later grow into LangGraph.\n- **Avoid when** you use one provider and one store and want minimal dependencies; the raw SDK plus a few\n  helpers may be simpler.\n- **Measure:** tokens and latency per step, tool-call accuracy, retrieval recall, and fallback rate.\n- **Interviews:** explain LCEL, `bind_tools` and how `create_agent` relates to LangGraph.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds chains and agents from `init_chat_model`, `@tool` and retrievers, wraps calls in retries and fallbacks, and tags runs so traces map to features and prompt versions.","architect":"Decides which layer LangChain owns (integrations, the agent loop, or both), pins the integration packages, and sets the upgrade policy gated on evals.","fde":"Plugs a customer's existing vector store and model provider into a working agent quickly by choosing the matching integration packages, then hands over a pinned, traced setup."},"check":{"q":"A teammate's LCEL chain retrieves documents, formats a prompt and calls a model. Now they need the\nmodel to decide whether to search again, call an order lookup, or answer, possibly several times. Should\nthey extend the LCEL chain?","a":"No. LCEL is for linear or fan-out pipelines with no loop. Deciding repeatedly among tools is an\nagent loop: expose retrieval and order lookup as `@tool` functions and use `create_agent`, which runs model\nand tools until the model stops calling tools. If they later need custom branching, a durable pause for\napproval or several agents, they should write the graph directly in LangGraph, since `create_agent` is\nitself a LangGraph graph. Either way they keep the same retriever and prompts, add a step limit, and trace\nthe loop in LangSmith."},"quiz":[{"q":"In LCEL, what does a dict such as {\"context\": retriever, \"question\": RunnablePassthrough()} do in a pipeline?","options":["Defines the model's output schema","Runs each value on the same input and returns a dict of their outputs","Stores intermediate results in a checkpointer","Registers two tools with the model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A dict literal is coerced to a parallel Runnable: every branch receives the same input, and the outputs are collected into a dict that the next step, usually a prompt template, consumes."},{"q":"Where does a LangChain `@tool` get the description the model sees?","options":["From the variable name it is assigned to","From a separate YAML file","From the function's docstring, with arguments taken from its type hints","From the first line of the function body"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The decorator builds the schema from the name, docstring and type hints, so a vague docstring directly causes wrong tool selection and invented arguments."},{"q":"What is the recommended way to build a new tool-calling agent in LangChain 1.x?","options":["`initialize_agent` with a ReAct text parser","An LCEL chain with a while loop around it","`create_agent`, which builds a LangGraph graph running model and tools until no tool calls remain","A retriever with `search_kwargs` set to the tool list"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`create_agent` is the current agent API and returns a compiled LangGraph graph. The older text-parsing agents are legacy, and retrievers are not tools by themselves."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/langgraph-fundamentals","sources":["Official docs: LangGraph","Official docs: LangGraph Graph API","Official docs: LangChain"],"why":"- **Agents are state machines with an LLM inside.** Real agents need \"classify, then branch\", \"retry this\n  tool\", \"search three sources in parallel\" and \"stop after N steps\". A while loop gets tangled fast.\n- **Chains cannot loop.** LCEL pipelines run once from left to right; an agent must revisit the model\n  after every tool result.\n- **Shared state needs merge rules.** When two parallel branches both append findings, something must\n  define how writes combine. Reducers make that explicit instead of last-write-wins by accident.\n- **Explicit graphs are inspectable.** You can render the graph, unit-test a node with a fake state, and\n  trace exactly which edge fired, which matters when a run goes wrong in production.","how":"### State and reducers\n- **The state schema is a `TypedDict`, dataclass or Pydantic model.** Every node reads the full state and\n  returns a dict of only the keys it changes.\n- **Default merge is overwrite.** Annotate a key to change that: `Annotated[list[str], operator.add]`\n  concatenates, and `add_messages` appends messages and replaces ones with the same id.\n- **`MessagesState`** is a ready schema with `messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]`, the usual base for\n  chat agents.\n\n### Nodes and edges\n1. **`add_node(name, fn)`** registers a function (sync or async) that takes state and returns an update.\n2. **`add_edge(a, b)`** always goes from `a` to `b`; `START` and `END` mark entry and exit.\n3. **`add_conditional_edges(src, router, path_map)`** calls `router(state)` after `src` and goes to the\n   returned node name (or names). A `Literal` return type documents the targets.\n4. **`compile()`** validates the graph and returns a Runnable with `invoke`, `stream` and `batch`;\n   pass `checkpointer=` here for persistence.\n\n### Routing tools: Command and Send\n- **`Command(goto=..., update=...)`** returned from a node updates state and routes in one step, useful\n  when the node itself decides the next hop.\n- **`Send(node, payload)`** returned from a conditional edge launches one task per payload in parallel\n  (map-reduce); a reducer on the target key collects results.\n\n### Execution model\n- **Supersteps.** All nodes triggered in one step run, then their updates merge through reducers, then the\n  next set runs. Parallel branches fan in at a node with several incoming edges.\n- **`recursion_limit`** (in the run config) caps supersteps; exceeding it raises `GraphRecursionError`.\n  Set it per run as your step budget: older releases defaulted to 25, but current 1.x releases default to\n  roughly 10,000, so the default no longer stops a runaway loop.\n- **Retries per node.** `add_node(..., retry_policy=RetryPolicy(max_attempts=3))` (import from\n  `langgraph.types`) retries flaky tool or model nodes with backoff.\n\n### Prebuilt pieces\n| Piece | Does |\n|---|---|\n| `ToolNode(tools)` | runs every tool call in the last AI message and returns `ToolMessage`s |\n| `tools_condition` | router: to `\"tools\"` if the last message has tool calls, else `END` |\n| `create_agent` (LangChain) | the whole model-tools loop as one call |","example":"### The agent loop as an explicit graph (runs offline)\nA scripted model replays a tool call and an answer; replace it with a real chat model and `bind_tools`.\n\n```python\n# pip install langgraph langchain-core ; offline: a scripted model stands in for a real one\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, MessagesState, START, END\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import ToolNode, tools_condition\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom langchain_core.messages import AIMessage\nfrom langchain_core.language_models.fake_chat_models import GenericFakeChatModel\n\n@tool\ndef get_weather(city: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Current weather for a city.\"\"\"\n    return f\"{city}: 18C, light rain\"\n\nclass ScriptedModel(GenericFakeChatModel):\n    def bind_tools(self, tools, **kwargs):\n        return self\n\nllm = ScriptedModel(messages=iter([\n    AIMessage(\"\", tool_calls=[{\"name\": \"get_weather\", \"args\": {\"city\": \"Oslo\"}, \"id\": \"t1\"}]),\n    AIMessage(\"Oslo is 18C with light rain; take an umbrella.\"),\n])).bind_tools([get_weather])           # real: init_chat_model(\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\").bind_tools([...])\n\ndef call_model(state: MessagesState) -> dict:\n    return {\"messages\": [llm.invoke(state[\"messages\"])]}   # add_messages reducer appends\n\nbuilder = StateGraph(MessagesState)\nbuilder.add_node(\"agent\", call_model)\nbuilder.add_node(\"tools\", ToolNode([get_weather]))      # runs every tool call in the last AI message\nbuilder.add_edge(START, \"agent\")\nbuilder.add_conditional_edges(\"agent\", tools_condition)  # -> \"tools\" if tool calls, else END\nbuilder.add_edge(\"tools\", \"agent\")                       # the loop\ngraph = builder.compile()\n\nout = graph.invoke({\"messages\": [(\"user\", \"Do I need an umbrella in Oslo?\")]},\n                   {\"recursion_limit\": 10})              # hard cap on supersteps\nprint([m.type for m in out[\"messages\"]])                 # ['human', 'ai', 'tool', 'ai']\nprint(out[\"messages\"][-1].content)\n```\n\n### Conditional routing plus parallel fan-out\nA triage agent classifies a ticket, routes it, and for bugs searches three sources in parallel with\n`Send`; the `operator.add` reducer collects the findings.\n\n```python\nimport operator\nfrom typing import Annotated, Literal, TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\nfrom langgraph.types import Send\n\nclass Triage(TypedDict):\n    ticket: str\n    category: str\n    findings: Annotated[list[str], operator.add]   # reducer: parallel writes are concatenated\n    reply: str\n\ndef classify(state: Triage) -> dict:                # a model call with structured output in practice\n    t = state[\"ticket\"].lower()\n    return {\"category\": \"billing\" if \"charge\" in t else \"bug\" if \"error\" in t else \"other\"}\n\ndef route(state: Triage) -> Literal[\"billing\", \"investigate\", \"escalate\"]:\n    return {\"billing\": \"billing\", \"bug\": \"investigate\"}.get(state[\"category\"], \"escalate\")\n\ndef fan_out(state: Triage):                         # map step: one task per source, run in parallel\n    return [Send(\"search\", {\"source\": s, \"ticket\": state[\"ticket\"]}) for s in (\"logs\", \"docs\", \"issues\")]\n\ndef search(task: dict) -> dict:\n    return {\"findings\": [f\"{task['source']}: match for '{task['ticket'][:12]}'\"]}\n\ndef billing(state: Triage) -> dict:\n    return {\"reply\": \"Refund queued.\"}\n\ndef summarize(state: Triage) -> dict:\n    return {\"reply\": f\"{len(state['findings'])} findings: \" + \"; \".join(sorted(state[\"findings\"]))}\n\ng = StateGraph(Triage)\nfor name, fn in [(\"classify\", classify), (\"billing\", billing), (\"search\", search), (\"summarize\", summarize)]:\n    g.add_node(name, fn)\ng.add_node(\"investigate\", lambda s: {})            # no-op hub that fans out\ng.add_node(\"escalate\", lambda s: {\"reply\": \"Sent to a human.\"})\ng.add_edge(START, \"classify\")\ng.add_conditional_edges(\"classify\", route)          # Literal return type documents the targets\ng.add_conditional_edges(\"investigate\", fan_out, [\"search\"])\ng.add_edge(\"search\", \"summarize\")                   # fan-in: waits for all three branches\nfor end in (\"billing\", \"summarize\", \"escalate\"):\n    g.add_edge(end, END)\napp = g.compile()\nprint(app.invoke({\"ticket\": \"Error 500 on checkout\", \"findings\": []})[\"reply\"])   # 3 findings: ...\nprint(app.invoke({\"ticket\": \"Double charge on my card\", \"findings\": []})[\"reply\"])  # Refund queued.\n```\n\n- **Without the reducer** on `findings`, three parallel writes to one key in the same superstep raise an\n  `InvalidUpdateError` instead of merging.\n- **The router is plain Python** here; in production `classify` is a model call returning a Pydantic\n  category, and the router stays deterministic.","patterns":["**Keep routers deterministic.** Let a model node write a typed decision into state (via structured output), and let a plain function route on it. Edge choices then show up clearly in traces and are easy to unit-test.","**Set a recursion limit per run.** Pass `{\"recursion_limit\": 25}` (or your budget) in every config, since the current default is far too high to act as a budget, and catch `GraphRecursionError` to return a graceful \"could not finish\" with the partial state; alert on its rate.","**Retry at the node, not the graph.** Attach `RetryPolicy(max_attempts=3)` to nodes that call flaky tools or models, so one transient failure does not restart the whole run. Monitor retries per node.","**Unit-test nodes as functions.** Nodes are plain functions from state to update; test each with a handcrafted state and a fake model before testing the compiled graph end to end.","**Render the graph in review.** `graph.get_graph().draw_mermaid()` outputs the topology as Mermaid; commit it with the PR so reviewers see routing changes."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** The tool-calling loop becomes two nodes and a conditional edge you can\n  see, test and extend, instead of a black box with hidden retries.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `langgraph` in Python (and `@langchain/langgraph` in TypeScript);\n  `create_agent` in LangChain is a prebuilt graph on top of it.\n- **What you control.** The state schema and its reducers, which node runs next, the `recursion_limit`,\n  and where models, tools and humans sit in the flow.\n- **What you can skip.** The Pregel internals. Know that execution runs in supersteps and that parallel\n  writes to one key need a reducer.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Triage and routing agents.** A classifier node writes a typed category and conditional edges route to\n  billing, technical or human queues.\n- **Research agents with fan-out.** `Send` searches several sources in parallel and a reducer merges the\n  findings before synthesis.\n- **Tool-calling assistants with limits.** The explicit loop gets per-node retries and a recursion limit\n  instead of a hidden while loop.\n- **Process automation.** Existing SOPs (check, decide, act, notify) map one-to-one onto nodes and edges.","mini_project":"**Ticket triage graph (2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** build a LangGraph agent that classifies, routes and investigates support tickets.\n- **Steps:** define a `TypedDict` state with a reducer on `findings`; add a model node that classifies into\n  a `Literal` category with structured output; route with `add_conditional_edges`; for bugs, fan out with\n  `Send` to three search nodes; add a `RetryPolicy` and a `recursion_limit`; unit-test each node.\n- **Done when:** 10 sample tickets route correctly, bug tickets show three merged findings, node tests\n  pass, and the rendered Mermaid graph is committed alongside the code."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"START\"] --> A[\"agent node: call model\"]\n    A --> C[\"tools_condition\"]\n    C -->|\"has tool calls\"| T[\"ToolNode: run tools\"]\n    C -->|\"no tool calls\"| E[\"END\"]\n    T -->|\"ToolMessages appended\"| A","caption":"The tool-calling agent as a graph: one conditional edge decides between running tools and finishing,\nand the edge back to the agent node is the loop."},"interview":[{"q":"What are state, nodes, edges and reducers in LangGraph?","a":"- **State** is a typed schema shared by all nodes; **nodes** are functions returning partial updates.\n- **Edges** decide what runs next: fixed with `add_edge`, or computed with `add_conditional_edges`.\n- **Reducers** define how updates merge into a key, such as `add_messages` appending messages; without\n  one, the update overwrites."},{"q":"How would you implement a tool-calling agent in LangGraph without prebuilt helpers?","a":"- **Two nodes:** one calls the model with bound tools and appends its message; one executes each tool\n  call and appends `ToolMessage`s.\n- **One conditional edge** after the model: tools if the last message has tool calls, else `END`.\n- **One edge back** from tools to the model, plus a recursion limit as the step budget."},{"q":"When would you use `Command` instead of a conditional edge?","a":"- **Use `Command`** when the node that does the work also decides the next hop, such as a handoff, and\n  you want the update and the routing to be atomic.\n- **Use conditional edges** when routing is a separate, deterministic decision on state, which keeps it\n  visible in the graph structure and easy to test."},{"q":"A production agent sometimes loops between two nodes until it hits the recursion limit. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Read the trace** to find which edge keeps firing and what state the router saw each time.\n- **Usual causes:** a router reading a stale key, a tool error the model keeps retrying, or a missing exit\n  condition.\n- **Fix:** add a counter in state, exit after N attempts, return tool errors as structured messages, and\n  alert on recursion-limit hits."},{"q":"How does LangGraph run parallel branches, and what can go wrong?","a":"- **Supersteps:** all triggered nodes run, then updates merge through reducers, then the next step runs.\n- **Risks:** missing reducers cause update errors, and order-dependent logic breaks because writes merge\n  together at the boundary.\n- **Cost:** parallel model calls multiply concurrent tokens, so watch rate limits."}],"tradeoffs":["**Explicit control vs boilerplate.** You see every edge and can add branches anywhere, but a simple agent takes 20 lines instead of one `create_agent` call. Flips when the flow is just the standard loop.","**Typed shared state vs coupling.** One schema makes data flow obvious, yet every node can read everything; large teams split state with subgraphs and narrower input schemas.","**Parallel supersteps vs ordering surprises.** `Send` and fan-out speed up independent work, but all writes merge at the step boundary, so order-dependent logic needs explicit sequencing.","**Graph runtime vs plain code.** You gain checkpoints, streaming and interrupts for free, and pay with a framework dependency and a learning curve around reducers."],"pitfalls":["**Returning the full state from a node.** Symptom: duplicated messages or lists growing unexpectedly. Fix: return only changed keys; reducers like `add_messages` append whatever you return.","**Parallel writes without a reducer.** Symptom: `InvalidUpdateError` when two branches write one key in the same step. Fix: annotate the key with a reducer such as `operator.add`.","**Unbounded loops.** Symptom: runs end in `GraphRecursionError` or burn tokens. Fix: a step counter in state, a router that exits on it, and a sensible `recursion_limit`.","**LLM-driven routing without validation.** Symptom: router returns a node name that does not exist. Fix: structured output with a `Literal` of valid targets, and a default branch."],"when":"- **Use when** the agent needs branches, loops with limits, parallel sub-tasks, or will later need\n  persistence, streaming and human approval.\n- **Avoid when** a single `create_agent` call or a linear LCEL chain does the job; add the graph when the\n  flow outgrows it.\n- **Measure:** steps per run, recursion-limit hits, retries per node, and tokens per node.\n- **Interviews:** draw the agent loop as nodes and a conditional edge, and explain reducers.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Designs the state schema and reducers, writes nodes as testable functions, and sets recursion limits and retry policies per node.","architect":"Decides where determinism ends and model judgment begins, which steps run in parallel, and how graphs split into subgraphs owned by different teams.","fde":"Sketches a customer's process as a graph in a working session, then turns each box into a node so the agent mirrors the workflow they already trust."},"check":{"q":"You add a step where an agent searches three knowledge bases in parallel and each branch writes to\n`state[\"results\"]`. The run crashes with an update error. What is wrong and how do you fix it, and what\nelse should you set before shipping?","a":"In one superstep the three branches each write `results`, and the default merge is overwrite, so\nLangGraph refuses conflicting writes. Annotate the key with a reducer, for example `Annotated[list,\noperator.add]`, so writes are concatenated, and have each branch return only its own list. Launch the\nbranches with `Send` from a conditional edge, and fan in at a node that runs after all of them. Before\nshipping, add a `recursion_limit` per run, a `RetryPolicy` on the search nodes for transient failures, and\nunit tests for each node with a fake state."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when two parallel nodes write to the same state key that has no reducer?","options":["The later node's value silently wins","The values are concatenated automatically","LangGraph raises an invalid update error for conflicting writes in one step","The graph waits and runs the nodes sequentially instead"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Without a reducer a key accepts one write per superstep. Annotating it, for example with `operator.add`, tells LangGraph how to combine parallel writes."},{"q":"In the standard LangGraph agent, what does `tools_condition` decide?","options":["Which tool the model should call","Whether to route to the tool node or end, based on tool calls in the last AI message","How many tools can run in parallel","Whether a tool result is valid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The model chooses tools; `tools_condition` only checks whether the latest AI message contains tool calls and routes to the tools node if so, otherwise to `END`."},{"q":"What does `recursion_limit` in the run config protect against?","options":["Deeply nested Python recursion inside a node","Runaway loops, by capping the number of supersteps in a run","Too many tools bound to the model","Oversized state objects"],"answer":1,"explanation":"It caps supersteps per run and raises `GraphRecursionError` when exceeded, acting as a hard step budget against agents looping between model and tools."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/langgraph-persistence-streaming-and-human-in-the-loop","sources":["Official docs: LangGraph (persistence, durable execution, streaming, interrupts)","Official docs: LangGraph checkpointer libraries","Official docs: LangChain"],"why":"- **Approvals take hours, processes do not wait.** A refund agent cannot hold a Python frame open until a\n  manager clicks approve; the run must be saved and resumed, possibly on another worker.\n- **Long runs crash.** A 40-step research agent that dies at step 35 should resume at step 35, not\n  re-spend every token. Checkpoints after each superstep make that the default.\n- **Conversations are threads.** Multi-turn memory is just \"load the last checkpoint for this\n  `thread_id`\", so the same mechanism powers chat history.\n- **Users abandon silent agents.** Streaming steps and tokens gives feedback within a second instead of a\n  spinner for 30 seconds.\n- **Debugging needs history.** Replaying from an earlier checkpoint, or forking with edited state, turns\n  \"why did it do that\" into a reproducible experiment.","how":"### Checkpointers and threads\n- **A checkpoint is a snapshot of state** plus metadata and the next nodes to run, written after every\n  superstep.\n- **A thread is a sequence of checkpoints** keyed by `config[\"configurable\"][\"thread_id\"]`. Same id\n  continues the conversation; a new id starts fresh.\n- **Stores by environment:**\n\n| Saver | Package | Use for |\n|---|---|---|\n| `InMemorySaver` | `langgraph` | tests and notebooks; lost on restart |\n| `SqliteSaver` | `langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite` | local tools, single-process apps |\n| `PostgresSaver` | `langgraph-checkpoint-postgres` | production, many workers; call `setup()` once |\n\n- **Pending writes.** If one node in a superstep fails, writes from the nodes that succeeded are kept, so\n  a resume does not re-run them.\n- **Durability modes.** The `durability` argument to `invoke` or `stream` takes `\"exit\"`, `\"async\"` (the\n  default in current releases) or `\"sync\"`, trading write latency against how much can be lost on a crash.\n\n### Reading and editing history\n- **`get_state(cfg)`** returns a `StateSnapshot`: `values`, `next`, `tasks` (with any interrupts) and\n  `config` with the checkpoint id.\n- **`get_state_history(cfg)`** lists checkpoints newest first.\n- **`update_state(cfg, values)`** writes a new checkpoint as if a node produced it; called on an old\n  checkpoint's config, it forks the thread.\n- **`invoke(None, cfg)`** continues from the checkpoint in `cfg`: replay or resume.\n\n### Interrupts (human-in-the-loop)\n1. **A node calls `interrupt(payload)`.** The run stops, the payload is saved with the checkpoint, and the\n   stream yields an `__interrupt__` update.\n2. **Your app shows the payload** to a reviewer: approve, edit arguments, or reject.\n3. **Resume with `invoke(Command(resume=value), cfg)`.** The interrupted node **re-runs from its first\n   line**, and this time `interrupt()` returns `value`.\n4. **Consequence:** code before `interrupt()` in that node runs twice. Keep side effects in a later node,\n   or make them idempotent.\n- **Static breakpoints** (`interrupt_before=[\"tools\"]` at compile or run time) pause before a node without\n  code changes; useful for debugging, less so for product approvals.\n\n### Long-term memory: the store\n- **Checkpoints are per thread.** Facts that should outlive a thread (user preferences) go in a store:\n  `compile(store=InMemoryStore())`, with `put(namespace, key, value)`, `get` and `search`.\n- **Production stores** are Postgres-backed and can add semantic search; see the agent-memory lessons.\n\n### Streaming\n| `stream_mode` | Yields | Typical consumer |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"values\"` | full state after each step | debugging, simple UIs |\n| `\"updates\"` | each node's returned update | step-by-step progress |\n| `\"messages\"` | `(token_chunk, metadata)` from model calls | typing effect in chat |\n| `\"custom\"` | anything a node sends via `get_stream_writer()` | tool progress bars |\n| `\"debug\"` | detailed events | development only |\n\n- **Combine modes** with a list: `stream_mode=[\"updates\", \"custom\"]` yields `(mode, chunk)` pairs.\n- **`subgraphs=True`** adds a namespace so you can tell which subgraph emitted an event.\n- **`metadata[\"langgraph_node\"]`** on message chunks tells you which node produced a token, so you can\n  stream only the final answer node.","example":"### Refund approval that survives a restart\nRuns locally with SQLite; the second `invoke` could happen hours later in another process pointing at the\nsame database.\n\n```python\n# pip install langgraph langgraph-checkpoint-sqlite\nimport sqlite3\nfrom typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.sqlite import SqliteSaver\nfrom langgraph.config import get_stream_writer\nfrom langgraph.types import interrupt, Command\n\nclass Refund(TypedDict, total=False):\n    order_id: str\n    amount: float\n    decision: str\n    receipt: str\n\ndef propose(state: Refund) -> dict:\n    get_stream_writer()({\"progress\": \"looked up order\"})      # 'custom' stream event\n    return {\"amount\": 120.0}                                 # a model + tool call in practice\n\ndef approve(state: Refund) -> dict:\n    if state[\"amount\"] < 50:\n        return {\"decision\": \"auto-approved\"}\n    answer = interrupt({\"question\": f\"Refund {state['amount']} for {state['order_id']}?\"})\n    return {\"decision\": answer}                              # node re-runs from the top on resume\n\ndef execute(state: Refund) -> dict:                          # side effect lives AFTER the approval\n    if state[\"decision\"] != \"approve\":\n        return {\"receipt\": \"cancelled\"}\n    return {\"receipt\": f\"refund-{state['order_id']}\"}        # pass an idempotency key to the payment API\n\ng = StateGraph(Refund)\ng.add_node(\"propose\", propose); g.add_node(\"approve\", approve); g.add_node(\"execute\", execute)\ng.add_edge(START, \"propose\"); g.add_edge(\"propose\", \"approve\")\ng.add_edge(\"approve\", \"execute\"); g.add_edge(\"execute\", END)\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\"checkpoints.db\", check_same_thread=False)\napp = g.compile(checkpointer=SqliteSaver(conn))              # PostgresSaver in production\ncfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"refund-A1001\"}}\n\nfor mode, chunk in app.stream({\"order_id\": \"A1001\"}, cfg, stream_mode=[\"updates\", \"custom\"]):\n    print(mode, chunk)                                       # last update carries '__interrupt__'\n\nsnap = app.get_state(cfg)\nprint(\"paused at:\", snap.next, \"| payload:\", snap.tasks[0].interrupts[0].value)\n\n# ...hours later, any process with the same DB and thread_id:\nprint(app.invoke(Command(resume=\"approve\"), cfg)[\"receipt\"])\nprint(\"checkpoints:\", len(list(app.get_state_history(cfg))))\n```\n\n- **Output:** a `custom` progress event, the `propose` update, an `__interrupt__` update, then\n  `paused at: ('approve',)`, the receipt `refund-A1001`, and `checkpoints: 5` after the resume.\n\n### Time travel: replay and fork a run\nFind the checkpoint before a bad step, edit state, and continue from there. The thread head moves to the\nfork, and the original history stays available.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, START, END\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.memory import InMemorySaver\n\nclass S(TypedDict, total=False):\n    query: str\n    plan: str\n    answer: str\n\ndef plan(s: S) -> dict:   return {\"plan\": f\"search for {s['query']}\"}\ndef act(s: S) -> dict:    return {\"answer\": f\"result of '{s['plan']}'\"}\n\ng = StateGraph(S)\ng.add_node(\"plan\", plan); g.add_node(\"act\", act)\ng.add_edge(START, \"plan\"); g.add_edge(\"plan\", \"act\"); g.add_edge(\"act\", END)\napp = g.compile(checkpointer=InMemorySaver())\ncfg = {\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": \"t1\"}}\napp.invoke({\"query\": \"q3 revenue\"}, cfg)\n\nhistory = list(app.get_state_history(cfg))            # newest first\nbefore_act = next(h for h in history if h.next == (\"act\",))\nprint(\"replay from:\", before_act.config[\"configurable\"][\"checkpoint_id\"][:8], before_act.values[\"plan\"])\n\nfork = app.update_state(before_act.config, {\"plan\": \"search finance DB for q3 revenue\"})  # new branch\nprint(app.invoke(None, fork)[\"answer\"])              # None = continue from that checkpoint\nprint(app.get_state(cfg).values[\"answer\"])           # thread head is now the fork\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: duplicate emails after adding approval\n| Observation | Cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Customers get 2 \"refund pending\" emails | email sent in `approve` before `interrupt()` | move the email to its own node before `approve` |\n| Retried resumes double-refund | payment call not idempotent | idempotency key = thread id + order id |\n| Paused runs vanish after deploy | `InMemorySaver` in production | `PostgresSaver` with `setup()` at startup |\n| UI shows nothing for 20 s | `invoke` instead of `stream` | stream `updates` and `messages` over SSE |","patterns":["**Postgres checkpointer with a retention job.** Use `PostgresSaver` (or the async variant), run `setup()` in a migration step, and delete or archive checkpoints of closed threads on a schedule. Monitor checkpoint table size and write latency per step.","**Deterministic thread ids.** Derive `thread_id` from the business object (`refund-{order_id}`, `chat-{user}-{session}`) so a retry or webhook resumes the same run instead of starting a duplicate.","**Interrupt payloads as a contract.** Send a typed payload (action, arguments, risk, deadline) to an approvals queue, and resume with a typed decision (approve, edit with new args, reject). Alert on approvals pending past their SLA.","**Side effects after the pause, with idempotency keys.** Put tool calls with external effects in a node after the approval, keyed by thread and action id, so re-runs and retries cannot double-execute.","**Stream over SSE with filtered modes.** Forward `updates` for step progress, `messages` filtered by `langgraph_node` for tokens, and `custom` for tool progress; drop `values` to keep payloads small.","**Encrypt sensitive state.** Checkpoints store full state, including PII and tool results; restrict database access and use an encrypted serializer (`EncryptedSerializer`) if policy requires it."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Real agents wait on people, crash mid-run and need to show progress.\n  Checkpoints turn those into resumable runs instead of lost work.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `compile(checkpointer=...)` with `InMemorySaver`, `SqliteSaver` or\n  `PostgresSaver`; `interrupt()` and `Command(resume=...)`; `stream(..., stream_mode=...)` behind your\n  SSE or WebSocket endpoint.\n- **What you control.** The thread id scheme, the checkpoint store and its retention, which nodes pause,\n  what payload a reviewer sees, and which stream modes reach the UI.\n- **What you can skip.** Checkpoint serialization internals. Know that state must be serializable and\n  that the node with the interrupt re-runs from its top on resume.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Approval gates.** Refunds, account changes, outbound emails and code merges pause for a human and\n  resume on the same thread.\n- **Long research and coding agents.** Checkpoints let a 50-step run resume after a worker crash without\n  re-spending tokens.\n- **Chat assistants with memory.** One thread per conversation; a store holds cross-thread preferences.\n- **Live progress UIs.** `updates`, `messages` and `custom` streams drive step lists, typing effects and\n  tool progress bars.","mini_project":"**Durable approval agent with a streaming UI (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent that proposes an action, pauses for approval, survives a restart and streams progress.\n- **Steps:** build a three-node graph (propose, approve with `interrupt()`, execute); compile with\n  `SqliteSaver` or `PostgresSaver`; expose `/start` and `/resume` endpoints that stream `updates` and\n  `custom` events over SSE; kill the server while paused, restart it and resume by `thread_id`; add an\n  idempotency key to execute.\n- **Done when:** a paused run resumes after a restart, the side effect runs exactly once across three\n  resume retries, and the browser shows step progress as it happens."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as App\n    participant G as Graph\n    participant C as Checkpointer\n    participant R as Reviewer\n    U->>G: stream(input, thread_id)\n    G->>C: save checkpoint per step\n    G-->>U: updates, then __interrupt__ payload\n    U->>R: show refund for approval\n    R->>U: approve\n    U->>G: invoke(Command resume approve, thread_id)\n    G->>C: load latest checkpoint\n    Note over G: approve node re-runs, interrupt returns approve\n    G-->>U: final state with receipt","caption":"One approval round trip: the first call stops at the interrupt and saves a checkpoint, and a later call\nfrom any worker resumes the same thread."},"interview":[{"q":"What does a LangGraph checkpointer store, and what does it enable?","a":"- **State after every superstep** under a thread id, plus metadata, next nodes, pending writes and any\n  interrupts.\n- **Enables** multi-turn memory, resume after crashes, human-in-the-loop pauses, and time travel for\n  debugging and forking."},{"q":"Design an approval step for an agent that can issue refunds.","a":"- **Interrupt before the refund tool** with a typed payload: amount, order, reason, model rationale.\n- **Resume with a typed decision** (approve, edit amount, reject) via `Command(resume=...)` on the same\n  thread.\n- **Execute in a separate node** with an idempotency key; durable Postgres checkpointer; alert on stale\n  approvals."},{"q":"How would you stream an agent's progress to a web UI?","a":"- **Use `stream` with several modes:** `updates` for steps, `messages` for tokens, `custom` for tool\n  progress from `get_stream_writer()`.\n- **Forward over SSE,** filtering tokens by `langgraph_node` so only the answer node types into the chat,\n  and send the `thread_id` and latest checkpoint id so a reconnecting client can call `get_state` and\n  catch up."},{"q":"When would you fork a thread with `update_state`?","a":"- **Debugging and what-if.** Rewind to the checkpoint before a bad tool call, correct the state, and\n  continue to see whether the rest of the run recovers.\n- **Human correction.** A reviewer edits a draft or plan in state and the agent continues from there.\n- **Caveat:** forks create new history; keep the original for audit."},{"q":"Your checkpoint table grew to hundreds of GB in a month. What do you do?","a":"- **Measure first:** rows per thread and bytes per checkpoint; large tool outputs in state are the usual\n  culprit.\n- **Then reduce:** store big artifacts by reference, prune or archive checkpoints of closed threads, and\n  keep only the latest checkpoint for threads that do not need time travel."}],"tradeoffs":["**Durability vs latency.** Writing a checkpoint every superstep adds a database round trip per step; `\"sync\"` is safest, `\"exit\"` is fastest but loses intermediate progress on a crash.","**Full state snapshots vs storage.** Time travel and pending writes need history, but long agents with big tool outputs fill tables fast. Store large artifacts by reference and prune old threads.","**Dynamic `interrupt()` vs static breakpoints.** Dynamic interrupts carry a payload and fire on conditions; static `interrupt_before` needs no code but pauses every time.","**Rich streaming vs client complexity.** More modes give better UX, but clients must handle interleaved event types and reconnection."],"pitfalls":["**Side effects before `interrupt()`.** Symptom: duplicate emails, tickets or charges after approval. Fix: the node re-runs on resume, so move effects after the pause or make them idempotent.","**Missing `thread_id`.** Symptom: an error that a checkpointer requires a thread id, or every message starting a new conversation. Fix: always pass `{\"configurable\": {\"thread_id\": ...}}`.","**`InMemorySaver` in production.** Symptom: paused approvals and chat history disappear on deploy or scale-out. Fix: a shared Postgres checkpointer for every replica.","**Unserializable state.** Symptom: checkpoint write errors when state holds clients, file handles or sockets. Fix: keep only data in state and inject clients through config or context.","**Several interrupts resumed with one value.** Symptom: parallel branches paused together resume wrongly. Fix: resume with a mapping of interrupt ids to values; check current docs for the format."],"when":"- **Use when** runs pause for people, last longer than a request, must survive crashes, or need\n  multi-turn memory and visible progress.\n- **Avoid when** the agent is a short stateless call; a checkpointer only adds writes and storage.\n- **Measure:** checkpoint write latency, table growth, time paused per approval, resume success rate and\n  time to first streamed token.\n- **Interviews:** explain why the interrupted node re-runs and how you prevent duplicate side effects.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires a Postgres checkpointer, puts `interrupt()` before consequential tools, moves side effects after approvals with idempotency keys, and streams updates and tokens to the UI.","architect":"Chooses the checkpoint store, thread id scheme, retention and encryption policy, and defines the approval payload contract shared with the review UI.","fde":"Maps a customer's approval chain (who approves what, within what SLA) onto interrupt points and shows them a run paused, edited and resumed live."},"check":{"q":"An agent drafts a contract change, sends a Slack notification, then calls `interrupt()` for legal\napproval. After approval, the Slack message is sent a second time. Why, and how do you fix it? What else\nmust be true for the approval to survive a deploy?","a":"On resume LangGraph does not restore a Python frame; it re-runs the interrupted node from its first\nline, and `interrupt()` returns the resume value only on that second pass. Everything before it, including\nthe Slack call, runs again. Move the notification into its own node that runs before the approval node, or\nmake it idempotent with a key derived from the thread and action. To survive a deploy, compile with a\nshared durable checkpointer such as `PostgresSaver`, not `InMemorySaver`, and resume with the same\n`thread_id` using `Command(resume=...)` from whichever worker handles the approval callback."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to code that runs before `interrupt()` in a node when the run is resumed?","options":["It is skipped because the frame is restored after the interrupt","It runs again, because the whole node re-executes from the top on resume","It runs only if the resume value is falsy","It is moved to a separate node automatically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LangGraph resumes by re-running the interrupted node; `interrupt()` then returns the resume value. Side effects before it repeat unless moved to another node or made idempotent."},{"q":"Which checkpointer setup lets an approval paused on one server be resumed by another after a deploy?","options":["`InMemorySaver` on each server","No checkpointer, passing the state in the resume request","A shared `PostgresSaver` with the same `thread_id` used on resume","`interrupt_before` without a checkpointer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Only a shared durable store keeps the checkpoint across processes. In-memory savers lose it on restart, and interrupts require a checkpointer to save the paused state."},{"q":"Which stream mode gives token-by-token output from model calls inside the graph?","options":["`\"values\"`","`\"updates\"`","`\"messages\"`","`\"debug\"`"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`\"messages\"` yields message chunks with metadata such as the producing node, while `\"values\"` and `\"updates\"` yield state after each step and `\"debug\"` yields detailed execution events."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/langgraph-multi-agent-patterns","sources":["Official docs: LangGraph (multi-agent systems, subgraphs, Command)","Official docs: langgraph-supervisor","Official docs: langgraph-swarm","Official docs: LangChain (multi-agent)"],"why":"- **One agent with 40 tools picks the wrong one.** Tool-selection accuracy drops as the tool list and\n  prompt grow; splitting tools across focused agents keeps each prompt short.\n- **Context pollution.** A research agent's 30 search results do not belong in the writer's context;\n  separate agents (or subgraphs with private state) keep transcripts small.\n- **Team boundaries.** Billing and logistics teams can own their agents as separate graphs with their own\n  tests, composed by a router.\n- **The naive version fails quietly.** Free-form agents chatting with each other loop, repeat work and\n  multiply tokens. Explicit routing and budgets keep the system debuggable.","how":"### Pattern 1: supervisor\n- **One router, many workers.** The supervisor node (an LLM with structured output, or tool calls named\n  `transfer_to_<agent>`) picks the next worker; each worker reports back to the supervisor.\n- **Strengths:** central control, easy budgets and logging. **Cost:** an extra model call per hop, and the\n  supervisor's context grows with every report.\n- **Prebuilt:** `create_supervisor([agents], model=..., prompt=...)` from `langgraph-supervisor` builds\n  it from named agent graphs.\n- **Now recommended: subagents as tools.** The `langgraph-supervisor` README itself recommends a main\n  `create_agent` that calls each worker through a tool for most new work, because you choose exactly what\n  context each worker receives and returns.\n\n### Pattern 2: handoffs (swarm-style)\n- **Agents pass control directly.** A tool returns `Command(goto=\"billing\", graph=Command.PARENT,\n  update=...)`, so the parent graph jumps to another agent node.\n- **Include the history you hand over.** The handoff must carry the AI message with the tool call and a\n  matching `ToolMessage`; providers reject a tool result without its call.\n- **Prebuilt:** `create_swarm([agents], default_active_agent=...)` with `create_handoff_tool(\n  agent_name=...)` from `langgraph-swarm`, which also remembers the active agent per thread.\n- **Strengths:** no extra router call per turn. **Cost:** routing logic is spread across agents.\n\n### Pattern 3: subgraphs\n- **A compiled graph is a node.** `parent.add_node(\"researcher\", research_graph)` runs it when routed to.\n- **Shared keys flow automatically.** Keys with the same name (usually `messages`) pass between parent\n  and child; keys only in the child schema stay private.\n- **Different schemas?** Call the subgraph inside a wrapper node that maps parent state in and out.\n- **Persistence:** give the checkpointer to the parent; subgraphs inherit it. Interrupts inside a\n  subgraph surface to the parent caller.\n\n### Choosing\n| Situation | Pattern |\n|---|---|\n| Tasks need planning and several specialists in sequence | supervisor |\n| Conversation moves between departments (triage, billing, tech) | handoffs |\n| A team owns a self-contained capability with private state | subgraph |\n| Specialists only answer narrow questions for a main agent | subagents as tools |","example":"### Supervisor with a subgraph worker (runs offline)\nDeterministic stand-ins replace the model decisions so the routing is visible; `subgraphs=True` shows\nwhich graph emitted each update.\n\n```python\n# pip install langgraph ; deterministic stand-ins for model calls\nfrom typing import Annotated, Literal, TypedDict\nfrom langgraph.graph.message import add_messages\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, MessagesState, START, END\nfrom langgraph.types import Command\n\n# --- a worker built as its own graph (a subgraph) with private state ---\nclass ResearchState(TypedDict, total=False):\n    messages: Annotated[list, add_messages]   # same key and reducer as the parent\n    queries: list[str]          # private to the subgraph: never reaches the parent\n\ndef plan_queries(s: ResearchState) -> dict:\n    return {\"queries\": [\"eu vat rules 2025\", \"vat digital services\"]}\n\ndef run_queries(s: ResearchState) -> dict:\n    notes = f\"research: found {len(s['queries'])} sources on EU VAT\"\n    return {\"messages\": [(\"ai\", notes)]}\n\nr = StateGraph(ResearchState)\nr.add_node(\"plan_queries\", plan_queries); r.add_node(\"run_queries\", run_queries)\nr.add_edge(START, \"plan_queries\"); r.add_edge(\"plan_queries\", \"run_queries\"); r.add_edge(\"run_queries\", END)\nresearcher = r.compile()\n\n# --- supervisor: decides the next worker and routes with Command ---\ndef supervisor(s: MessagesState) -> Command[Literal[\"researcher\", \"writer\", \"__end__\"]]:\n    said = \" \".join(m.content for m in s[\"messages\"] if m.type == \"ai\")\n    if \"research:\" not in said:\n        return Command(goto=\"researcher\")            # an LLM with structured output picks this in practice\n    if \"draft:\" not in said:\n        return Command(goto=\"writer\")\n    return Command(goto=END)\n\ndef writer(s: MessagesState) -> Command[Literal[\"supervisor\"]]:\n    return Command(goto=\"supervisor\", update={\"messages\": [(\"ai\", \"draft: VAT applies at the buyer's rate.\")]})\n\ng = StateGraph(MessagesState)\ng.add_node(\"supervisor\", supervisor)\ng.add_node(\"researcher\", researcher)                  # compiled graph as a node: shares 'messages'\ng.add_node(\"writer\", writer)\ng.add_edge(START, \"supervisor\")\ng.add_edge(\"researcher\", \"supervisor\")                # workers always report back\napp = g.compile()\n\nfor update in app.stream({\"messages\": [(\"user\", \"Write a note on EU VAT for SaaS\")]},\n                         stream_mode=\"updates\", subgraphs=True):\n    print(update[0] or \"root\", list(update[1]))         # namespace shows which graph emitted it\n```\n\n- **Output order:** `supervisor`, then `plan_queries` and `run_queries` under a `researcher:<id>`\n  namespace, then `researcher`, `supervisor`, `writer`, `supervisor`.\n- **`queries` never appears** in the parent's state: it is private to the subgraph schema.\n\n### A handoff tool between two agents\nTriage hands the conversation to billing with `Command.PARENT`. Scripted models make it run offline;\n`InjectedState` passes the full history so the tool call and its result travel together.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool, InjectedToolCallId\nfrom langchain_core.messages import AIMessage, ToolMessage\nfrom langchain_core.language_models.fake_chat_models import GenericFakeChatModel\nfrom langchain.agents import create_agent\nfrom langgraph.graph import StateGraph, MessagesState, START\nfrom langgraph.types import Command\nfrom langgraph.prebuilt import InjectedState\n\nclass ScriptedModel(GenericFakeChatModel):\n    def bind_tools(self, tools, **kwargs):\n        return self\n\n@tool\ndef transfer_to_billing(reason: str, state: Annotated[dict, InjectedState],\n                        tool_call_id: Annotated[str, InjectedToolCallId]) -> Command:\n    \"\"\"Hand the conversation to the billing agent. Give a one-line reason.\"\"\"\n    msg = ToolMessage(f\"Transferred to billing: {reason}\", tool_call_id=tool_call_id)\n    return Command(goto=\"billing\", graph=Command.PARENT, update={\"messages\": state[\"messages\"] + [msg]})\n\ntriage = create_agent(ScriptedModel(messages=iter([AIMessage(\"\", tool_calls=[\n    {\"name\": \"transfer_to_billing\", \"args\": {\"reason\": \"double charge\"}, \"id\": \"h1\"}])])),\n    tools=[transfer_to_billing], name=\"triage\")\nbilling = create_agent(ScriptedModel(messages=iter([AIMessage(\"I've refunded the duplicate charge.\")])),\n                       tools=[], name=\"billing\")\n\ng = StateGraph(MessagesState)\ng.add_node(\"triage\", triage, destinations=(\"billing\",))   # declares the handoff edge for rendering\ng.add_node(\"billing\", billing)\ng.add_edge(START, \"triage\")\napp = g.compile()\nout = app.invoke({\"messages\": [(\"user\", \"I was charged twice\")]})\nprint([(m.type, m.content[:40]) for m in out[\"messages\"]])\n# [('human', ...), ('ai', ''), ('tool', 'Transferred to billing: double charge'), ('ai', \"I've refunded...\")]\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: collapsing a three-agent crew\nA team ran planner, researcher and writer agents for market briefs (illustrative numbers from an eval of\n50 briefs).\n\n| Setup | Model calls per brief | Tokens per brief | Eval pass rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Supervisor plus 3 workers, full history to each | ~14 | ~95k | 82% |\n| Supervisor plus 3 workers, workers see only their task | ~14 | ~40k | 84% |\n| One agent with the same tools | ~8 | ~30k | 78% |\n\nThey kept the supervisor but scoped each worker's context, because the single agent lost 6 points on\nbriefs needing many searches.","patterns":["**Start single, split on evidence.** Ship one agent first; split into roles only when an eval shows tool-selection or context errors, and keep the eval to prove the split helped.","**Scope context per worker.** Pass a worker its task and the minimum history (subgraph with its own schema, or `output_mode=\"last_message\"` in `create_supervisor`), so tokens do not grow with every hop. Monitor tokens per agent per run.","**One budget for the whole system.** Set `recursion_limit` explicitly on the parent run (the current default is roughly 10,000 supersteps) and count handoffs in state, so agents cannot ping-pong forever. Alert on runs near the limit.","**Name every agent in traces.** Give each agent a `name` and stream with `subgraphs=True`, so LangSmith traces and UI events show which agent spent which tokens.","**Persist at the parent.** Compile only the parent with a checkpointer; subgraphs inherit it, and interrupts in a worker surface to the parent for approval UIs."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Splitting work across agents can isolate context, tools and prompts per\n  role, but every hop costs tokens and latency and adds a routing decision that can go wrong.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Hand-written supervisor graphs, `Command(goto=..., graph=\n  Command.PARENT)` handoff tools, compiled graphs added as nodes, and the `langgraph-supervisor` and\n  `langgraph-swarm` helper packages.\n- **What you control.** Who decides the next agent, what history each agent sees, which tools each one\n  holds, and the global step and cost budget.\n- **What you can skip.** Agent \"personalities\". Roles matter only through the tools, context and\n  instructions each agent gets.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Customer support with departments.** Triage hands off to billing, logistics or tech agents, each\n  holding only its own tools.\n- **Research and report pipelines.** A supervisor sends a researcher subgraph to gather sources and a\n  writer to draft, with private scratch state.\n- **Team-owned capabilities.** A platform team composes graphs owned by other teams as subgraphs behind\n  one entry point.\n- **Coding agents with reviewers.** A coder agent proposes a patch and a reviewer agent with test tools\n  approves or sends it back, under a handoff budget.","mini_project":"**Supervisor versus single agent (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** find out whether a multi-agent design actually beats one agent on your task.\n- **Steps:** build a single `create_agent` with search and calculator tools; build a supervisor with a\n  researcher subgraph and an analyst agent using the same tools; write 15 eval questions; run both with\n  tracing on; record pass rate, model calls, tokens and handoffs per question.\n- **Done when:** you have a comparison table for both designs, a recursion limit and handoff counter in\n  the multi-agent version, and a written decision on which to ship and why."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User request\"] --> S[\"Supervisor: pick next agent\"]\n    S -->|\"goto researcher\"| R[\"Researcher subgraph\"]\n    R --> R1[\"plan queries (private)\"]\n    R1 --> R2[\"run queries\"]\n    R2 -->|\"report\"| S\n    S -->|\"goto writer\"| W[\"Writer agent\"]\n    W -->|\"report\"| S\n    S -->|\"done\"| E[\"END\"]","caption":"A supervisor routes to two workers; the researcher is a subgraph with private steps, and every worker\nreports back before the supervisor decides again or ends."},"interview":[{"q":"Compare the supervisor and handoff patterns in LangGraph.","a":"- **Supervisor:** one router picks the next worker and workers report back; central control and easy\n  budgets, but an extra model call per hop.\n- **Handoffs:** agents transfer control directly via `Command(goto=..., graph=Command.PARENT)`; fewer\n  calls, but routing logic is distributed and loops are harder to see."},{"q":"How do subgraphs share state with the parent graph?","a":"- **Same-named keys** in both schemas (usually `messages`) flow in and out automatically, using the\n  parent's reducer on return.\n- **Child-only keys stay private,** which keeps scratch work out of the parent.\n- **Different schemas** need a wrapper node that maps state in and out before and after invoking the\n  subgraph."},{"q":"When would you not build a multi-agent system?","a":"- **When one agent passes the eval.** A single agent with fewer than about ten clear tools is cheaper,\n  faster and easier to debug.\n- **When roles share all tools and context,** splitting only adds hops; better tool descriptions or\n  retrieval over tools usually fix selection errors."},{"q":"A multi-agent research system costs 3x the single-agent version. How do you cut it?","a":"- **Measure tokens per agent** in traces to find who re-reads what.\n- **Scope context:** workers get the task and a summary, return a short result, not the transcript.\n- **Remove hops:** merge roles that always run together, and use a cheaper model for routing."},{"q":"How do you handle human approval inside a worker subgraph?","a":"- **Call `interrupt()` in the worker node;** it surfaces to the parent caller with the payload.\n- **Compile the parent with a durable checkpointer;** subgraphs inherit it, so the pause survives\n  restarts.\n- **Resume the parent thread** with `Command(resume=...)`; the worker node re-runs from its top."}],"tradeoffs":["**Specialization vs coordination cost.** Focused agents pick tools better, but every supervisor hop is an extra model call. Flips when tasks are short and need one or two tools.","**Supervisor vs handoffs.** Central routing is easy to budget and audit; handoffs save router calls but spread routing across agents and make loops harder to spot.","**Shared history vs private state.** Shared `messages` lets agents build on each other, but floods each context. Private subgraph state saves tokens at the risk of losing needed facts.","**Prebuilt helpers vs hand-written graphs.** `create_supervisor` and `create_swarm` get you running quickly; hand-written graphs or subagents-as-tools are easier to customize, debug and scope context in."],"pitfalls":["**Multi-agent by default.** Symptom: triple the tokens with no quality gain. Fix: compare against one agent with the same tools on your eval before splitting.","**Handoffs without the tool-call message.** Symptom: provider errors about a tool result without a matching call. Fix: pass the AI message and its `ToolMessage` together in the handoff update.","**Agents ping-ponging.** Symptom: runs hit the recursion limit with alternating handoffs. Fix: a handoff counter in state, clear handoff rules in prompts, and a fallback to a human.","**Supervisor context bloat.** Symptom: later routing decisions get slower and worse. Fix: workers return short summaries, not full transcripts."],"when":"- **Use when** distinct roles need different tools or instructions, tasks need planning across\n  specialists, or teams must own separate agents.\n- **Avoid when** one agent with fewer than about ten well-described tools passes your eval.\n- **Measure:** pass rate versus a single-agent baseline, model calls and tokens per agent, handoffs per\n  run and recursion-limit hits.\n- **Interviews:** compare supervisor, handoffs and subgraphs and argue when not to go multi-agent.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements supervisors and handoff tools with `Command`, scopes worker state with subgraphs, and traces tokens per agent.","architect":"Decides whether the system needs multiple agents at all, which pattern fits, and how context, budgets and ownership are split across teams.","fde":"Maps a customer's departments to agents only where tools and data really differ, and shows the single-agent baseline before proposing a multi-agent design."},"check":{"q":"A customer-service system has a triage agent that hands off to billing or tech agents. Users report\nthat after a handoff the billing agent sometimes errors, and some conversations bounce between billing and\ntech for 20 turns. What is likely wrong and what do you change?","a":"The errors suggest the handoff update carries a `ToolMessage` without the AI message that made the\ntool call, which providers reject; the handoff tool should pass the full history or at least both\nmessages. The bouncing means no agent owns the decision to stop: add a handoff counter in state and route\nto a human after a small number of transfers, tighten each agent's handoff instructions, and set a\n`recursion_limit` on the parent run. Then trace with agent names to confirm which handoff rule fires, and\ncompare against a single agent holding both tool sets."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does `graph=Command.PARENT` do in a handoff tool's returned Command?","options":["Ends the whole multi-agent run","Applies the goto and update in the parent graph, so control jumps to a sibling agent node","Restarts the current agent from its first node","Sends the message to the supervisor's system prompt"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A tool runs inside an agent's own graph. `Command.PARENT` tells LangGraph to route in the enclosing graph, which is how one agent node hands control to another."},{"q":"In a subgraph added as a node, what happens to a key that exists only in the subgraph's state schema?","options":["It is copied into the parent state after the subgraph finishes","It stays private to the subgraph and does not appear in the parent state","It raises a schema validation error at compile time","It overwrites the parent's messages key"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only keys shared by name pass between parent and child. Private keys let a worker keep scratch state, such as search queries, out of the parent's context."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to split one agent into several in LangGraph?","options":["Multi-agent systems are always more accurate","It removes the need for a recursion limit","An eval shows one agent failing because of too many tools or polluted context that focused agents fix","Each agent can use a different system font in the UI"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Splitting adds hops, tokens and routing risk, so it is justified by measured failures of a single agent, typically tool-selection errors or context overload."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/langsmith-for-agents","sources":["Official docs: LangSmith (observability, evaluation, prompt engineering)","Official docs: agentevals","Official docs: openevals","Official docs: LangGraph"],"why":"- **Final-answer checks miss agent bugs.** An agent can reach a right answer by a wrong path (refunding\n  before checking delivery) or a wrong answer by a right path. You need step-level visibility and\n  trajectory scoring.\n- **Agent quality regresses silently.** A prompt tweak, a model swap or a framework upgrade changes tool\n  choices. Without a dataset and experiments, you find out from customers.\n- **Production traces are the best test cases.** Failures in real traffic should become dataset examples\n  in one click, not a manual copy job.\n- **Prompts change faster than code.** Teams want to edit, compare and roll back the system prompt without\n  redeploying, while keeping each version traceable to the runs it produced.","how":"### Tracing an agent\n- **Turn it on with env vars:** `LANGSMITH_TRACING=true`, `LANGSMITH_API_KEY`, and `LANGSMITH_PROJECT`\n  to pick the project. LangChain and LangGraph runs are traced with no code change.\n- **The trace tree mirrors the graph.** One root run per invocation; child runs per node, model call\n  (with tokens and cost) and tool call (with arguments and result).\n- **Your own code:** decorate functions with `@traceable(run_type=\"tool\")` or wrap provider clients\n  (`wrap_openai`), so non-LangChain steps join the same tree.\n- **Threads.** Runs that share a `thread_id` or `session_id` metadata key appear as one conversation;\n  LangGraph's `thread_id` makes this natural.\n- **Tags and metadata.** Pass `config={\"tags\": [...], \"metadata\": {...}}` (prompt version, tenant,\n  model) so you can filter and compare later.\n\n### Datasets\n- **Examples are inputs plus reference outputs.** For agents, the reference often includes the expected\n  tool sequence as well as the answer.\n- **Sources:** hand-written golden cases, production traces added from the UI or by rules, and synthetic\n  cases. Split them (for example `smoke`, `regression`, `hard`) so CI runs a fast subset.\n\n### Evaluators for agents\n| Level | Question | Typical evaluator |\n|---|---|---|\n| Final response | is the answer correct and grounded? | LLM judge (`openevals`) or exact match |\n| Single step | did it pick the right tool with valid args? | code check on one model call |\n| Trajectory | did it take an acceptable path? | `agentevals` trajectory match or trajectory judge |\n| Cost and speed | tokens, latency, steps | summary evaluators from run stats |\n\n- **Trajectory match modes** in `agentevals`: `strict`, `unordered`, `subset`, `superset`, with\n  `tool_args_match_mode` to compare or ignore arguments.\n- **`evaluate(target, data=..., evaluators=[...])`** runs the agent on every example, traces each run and\n  records scores as feedback; `num_repetitions` reruns examples to measure variance and\n  `max_concurrency` parallelizes.\n- **Pairwise comparison** of two experiments and human review through annotation queues cover what\n  automated scores miss.\n\n### Online evaluation\n- **Rules on a tracing project** run evaluators on a sample of production traces, add matching traces to\n  a dataset, or send them to an annotation queue for humans.\n- **User feedback** (thumbs up or down) is logged with `create_feedback` against the run id and appears\n  next to automated scores.\n\n### Prompt hub\n- **`client.push_prompt(\"refund-agent-system\", object=prompt)`** stores a new commit of a prompt template;\n  commit tags (such as a production tag) mark which version is live.\n- **`client.pull_prompt(\"refund-agent-system:<commit-or-tag>\")`** loads a pinned version at startup;\n  check the current docs for tag syntax and caching options.\n- **Every run can record the prompt version** in metadata, so experiments compare prompt commits\n  directly.","example":"### Trajectory evaluators, offline\n`agentevals` scores the tool path; this runs without a LangSmith account and is the same evaluator you\npass to `evaluate`.\n\n```python\n# pip install langsmith agentevals ; the evaluator part runs offline\nfrom agentevals.trajectory.match import create_trajectory_match_evaluator\n\ndef tool_calls(*calls):                     # helper: OpenAI-style assistant message with tool calls\n    return {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"\", \"tool_calls\": [\n        {\"function\": {\"name\": n, \"arguments\": a}} for n, a in calls]}\n\nreference = [\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Refund order A-1001\"},\n    tool_calls((\"order_status\", '{\"order_id\": \"A-1001\"}')),\n    {\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": \"delivered\"},\n    tool_calls((\"issue_refund\", '{\"order_id\": \"A-1001\"}')),\n    {\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": \"refund-77\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Refunded, reference refund-77.\"},\n]\nskipped_check = [reference[0], reference[3], reference[4], reference[5]]   # refunded without looking up\n\nstrict = create_trajectory_match_evaluator(trajectory_match_mode=\"strict\")\nsuperset = create_trajectory_match_evaluator(trajectory_match_mode=\"superset\", tool_args_match_mode=\"ignore\")\nprint(strict(outputs=reference, reference_outputs=reference))       # score True\nprint(strict(outputs=skipped_check, reference_outputs=reference))   # score False: lookup missing\nprint(superset(outputs=reference, reference_outputs=skipped_check)) # extra calls allowed: True\n```\n\n### An experiment over a real agent\nNeeds LangSmith and OpenAI keys and a dataset named `refund-agent-golden` whose examples hold a\n`question` input and reference `messages` and `answer` outputs.\n\n```python\n# pip install langsmith langchain langchain-openai agentevals openevals\n# env: LANGSMITH_TRACING=true LANGSMITH_API_KEY=... LANGSMITH_PROJECT=refund-agent OPENAI_API_KEY=...\nfrom langsmith import evaluate\nfrom langchain.agents import create_agent\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom agentevals.trajectory.match import create_trajectory_match_evaluator\nfrom openevals.llm import create_llm_as_judge\nfrom openevals.prompts import CORRECTNESS_PROMPT\n\n@tool\ndef order_status(order_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Delivery status for an order id.\"\"\"\n    return \"delivered\"\n\n@tool\ndef issue_refund(order_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Refund a delivered order. Always check order_status first.\"\"\"\n    return \"refund-77\"\n\nagent = create_agent(\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\", tools=[order_status, issue_refund],\n                     system_prompt=\"Refund agent. Verify delivery before refunding.\")\n\ndef target(inputs: dict) -> dict:                      # one traced agent run per example\n    result = agent.invoke({\"messages\": [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": inputs[\"question\"]}]})\n    return {\"messages\": result[\"messages\"], \"answer\": result[\"messages\"][-1].content}\n\nmatch = create_trajectory_match_evaluator(trajectory_match_mode=\"superset\", tool_args_match_mode=\"ignore\")\njudge = create_llm_as_judge(prompt=CORRECTNESS_PROMPT, model=\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\", feedback_key=\"correct\")\n\ndef tool_path(outputs: dict, reference_outputs: dict):  # did it call at least the reference tools?\n    return match(outputs=outputs[\"messages\"], reference_outputs=reference_outputs[\"messages\"])\n\ndef correct(inputs: dict, outputs: dict, reference_outputs: dict):\n    return judge(inputs=inputs[\"question\"], outputs=outputs[\"answer\"],\n                 reference_outputs=reference_outputs[\"answer\"])\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    evaluate(target, data=\"refund-agent-golden\", evaluators=[tool_path, correct],\n             experiment_prefix=\"gpt-4o-mini-prompt-v3\", max_concurrency=4, num_repetitions=3)\n```\n\n- **In the UI:** one experiment row per example and repetition, each linked to its full agent trace, with\n  `trajectory_superset_match` and `correct` scores.\n- **`num_repetitions=3`** shows how stable each score is; agents with temperature above zero vary.\n\n### Worked scenario: a model swap that looked fine\nIllustrative numbers from a refund agent's regression dataset.\n\n| Metric on 120 examples | Old model | New model |\n|---|---|---|\n| Final answer correct (judge) | 91% | 92% |\n| Trajectory superset match | 96% | 81% |\n| Median tool calls per run | 3 | 2 |\n| Cost per run | baseline | 40% lower |\n\n- **The answer score hid the problem:** the new model often skipped `order_status` and refunded directly.\n- **The decision:** keep the old model for refunds, add a guard node that requires the status check, and\n  re-run the experiment before trying the new model again.","patterns":["**Gate releases on experiments in CI.** Run `evaluate` on a smoke dataset for every prompt, model or framework change and fail the build if trajectory or correctness drops below the baseline experiment.","**Turn failures into examples.** Add rules that send traces with negative user feedback, errors or long step counts to an annotation queue, and promote reviewed ones into the regression dataset weekly.","**Sample and redact in production.** Set `LANGSMITH_TRACING_SAMPLING_RATE` (for example 0.1) for high-volume agents and `LANGSMITH_HIDE_INPUTS` or `LANGSMITH_HIDE_OUTPUTS` where data is sensitive; consider self-hosting for residency.","**Tag every run with versions.** Put prompt commit, model and app version in metadata, so dashboards and experiments compare like with like and a regression points at one change.","**Pin prompts by commit or tag.** Pull the production prompt by tag at startup, cache it, and keep a fallback copy in the repo so a hub outage cannot take the agent down.","**Watch online scores per agent node.** Online evaluators on sampled traces track judge scores, tool errors and cost per run; alert on drops after deploys."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Agent failures hide in the middle: a wrong tool, a bad argument, a loop.\n  A trace tree per run and trajectory evals are how you see and catch them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Environment variables turn on tracing for LangChain and LangGraph;\n  `@traceable` covers your own code; `evaluate()` runs experiments; `push_prompt` and `pull_prompt`\n  manage prompts.\n- **What you control.** What gets traced (sampling, hidden inputs), how runs are tagged and grouped into\n  threads, which evaluators gate a release, and which prompt version production pulls.\n- **What you can skip.** Re-learning tracing basics; the observability LangSmith lesson covers runs,\n  transport and feedback. This lesson is the agent workflow on top.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Debugging agent loops.** Trace trees show which node, tool call or argument caused a wrong answer or\n  a recursion-limit hit.\n- **Model and prompt migrations.** Experiments on a golden dataset compare correctness, trajectory and\n  cost before switching.\n- **Continuous quality monitoring.** Online evaluators and feedback on sampled production traces catch\n  regressions after deploys.\n- **Prompt iteration with non-engineers.** Product and support staff edit prompts in the hub, and pinned\n  commits keep production stable.","mini_project":"**Agent regression suite in LangSmith (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** catch a wrong tool path that a final-answer score would miss.\n- **Steps:** build a two-tool refund agent with `create_agent`; create a 15-example dataset with reference\n  answers and tool sequences; write a trajectory evaluator and an LLM-judge evaluator; run a baseline\n  experiment with `num_repetitions=3`; change the system prompt to drop the \"verify first\" rule and rerun;\n  compare both experiments.\n- **Done when:** the comparison view shows the trajectory score dropping while correctness barely moves,\n  and a CI script fails when trajectory falls below the baseline."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    A[\"Agent in production\"] -->|\"traces\"| P[(\"Tracing project\")]\n    P -->|\"rules: sample, failures\"| D[(\"Dataset\")]\n    P -->|\"online evaluators\"| M[\"Live quality dashboard\"]\n    H[\"Prompt hub commit\"] --> X[\"Experiment: evaluate on dataset\"]\n    D --> X\n    X -->|\"scores beat baseline\"| A\n    X -->|\"regression\"| F[\"Fix prompt, tools or model\"]\n    F --> H","caption":"The loop teams run: production traces feed datasets, experiments gate changes, and online evaluators\nwatch live traffic for regressions."},"interview":[{"q":"How would you evaluate a tool-calling agent beyond the final answer?","a":"- **Three levels:** final response (judge or exact match), single step (right tool and valid args for a\n  given state), and trajectory (acceptable sequence of tool calls).\n- **Tools:** `agentevals` trajectory match modes or a trajectory judge, run through `evaluate` on a\n  dataset with reference tool sequences."},{"q":"How do production traces become regression tests?","a":"- **Rules on the tracing project** send failures, low feedback or long runs to an annotation queue.\n- **Reviewers fix the reference output** and add the example to a dataset split.\n- **CI runs `evaluate`** on that dataset for every change, so the same failure cannot ship twice."},{"q":"Your experiment scores change between two runs of the same code. Why, and what do you do?","a":"- **Agents are stochastic:** sampling, tool timing and judge variance all move scores.\n- **Use `num_repetitions`** of 3 or more, compare averages and spread, and fix temperature for judges.\n- **Look at per-example diffs** in the comparison view instead of the aggregate alone."},{"q":"How do you keep traces safe and affordable at high volume?","a":"- **Sample** with `LANGSMITH_TRACING_SAMPLING_RATE`, keeping full traces for errors and flagged runs.\n- **Redact** with hide inputs or outputs settings and masking in code before tracing.\n- **Retention and hosting:** shorter retention for raw traces, self-hosting where residency requires it."},{"q":"When would you choose Langfuse or Phoenix over LangSmith for an agent?","a":"- **Mixed or non-LangChain stacks** that already emit OpenTelemetry fit OTel-first tools more naturally.\n- **Open-source self-hosting** is a hard requirement and the team wants to run the whole stack itself.\n- **LangSmith wins** when the agent is LangGraph-based and you want graph-aware traces with no code change."}],"tradeoffs":["**Native LangGraph integration vs lock-in.** Zero-code tracing and graph-aware views are strongest with LangChain and LangGraph; OpenTelemetry-first tools like Langfuse or Phoenix fit mixed stacks better.","**Full traces vs cost and privacy.** Tracing every step of every run is the best debugging aid, but storage, ingestion pricing and PII exposure grow with volume; sample and redact.","**LLM judges vs code checks.** Judges score open-ended answers, but cost tokens and drift; trajectory and schema checks are cheap and deterministic but narrow.","**Hosted prompt hub vs prompts in git.** Editing in the hub is fast for non-engineers, but adds a runtime dependency and a second source of truth unless versions are pinned."],"pitfalls":["**Evaluating only the final answer.** Symptom: unsafe or wasteful paths pass. Fix: add trajectory or single-step evaluators for consequential tools.","**Strict trajectory match everywhere.** Symptom: correct runs fail because the agent took a valid extra step. Fix: use `superset` or `unordered` modes, or a trajectory judge, where order is flexible.","**Datasets that never change.** Symptom: experiments pass while production quality drops. Fix: feed production failures into the dataset on a schedule.","**Tracing secrets.** Symptom: API keys or PII visible in tool inputs. Fix: hide inputs or outputs, mask fields in your code before tracing, and restrict project access.","**Single runs of a stochastic agent.** Symptom: scores swing between identical experiments. Fix: `num_repetitions` of 3 or more and compare averages."],"when":"- **Use when** you build on LangChain or LangGraph, or want one tool for agent traces, datasets,\n  experiments and prompt versions.\n- **Avoid when** policy forbids sending traces to a SaaS and self-hosting is not an option, or your stack\n  is standardized on OpenTelemetry tooling.\n- **Measure:** trajectory and correctness scores per experiment, online judge scores, tool error rate,\n  steps and cost per run.\n- **Interviews:** explain final-response, single-step and trajectory evals and how a trace becomes a test.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Turns on tracing, tags runs with versions, writes trajectory and judge evaluators, and wires `evaluate` into CI so prompt and model changes must beat the baseline.","architect":"Sets the evaluation policy (which datasets gate which releases), sampling and redaction rules, and whether LangSmith is hosted or self-hosted.","fde":"Builds a customer's golden dataset from their real conversations, shows failures in trace trees, and proves improvements with side-by-side experiments."},"check":{"q":"After switching models, a refund agent's final-answer correctness stays at 92%, but support sees\nrefunds issued for undelivered orders. How would LangSmith have caught this, and what do you set up so it\ncannot slip through again?","a":"The answer judge only scored the final message, so a path that skipped the delivery check still\nlooked correct. The traces would show `issue_refund` without a preceding `order_status`. Add a trajectory\nevaluator (superset match against a reference that includes `order_status`, or a trajectory judge) to the\nregression dataset, add the failing production traces as examples via an annotation queue, and gate model\nand prompt changes in CI on both correctness and trajectory scores with several repetitions. Add an online\nevaluator or rule on production traces that flags refunds without a status check."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does a trajectory evaluator check that a final-answer judge does not?","options":["Whether the answer is grammatically correct","Whether the agent called an acceptable sequence of tools on the way to its answer","Whether the prompt was pulled from the hub","Whether the trace was sampled"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Trajectory evaluators compare the tool calls in a run with a reference path, catching runs that reach a plausible answer by skipping a required step or calling a wrong tool."},{"q":"Why set `num_repetitions` above 1 in an agent experiment?","options":["To bypass rate limits","To create more dataset examples automatically","To measure how much scores vary across runs of a stochastic agent before trusting a difference","To force the agent to use a different model each time"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Agents and judges are nondeterministic. Repeating each example shows the spread, so a small score difference between experiments is not mistaken for a real improvement."},{"q":"How do LangGraph agent runs get traced in LangSmith?","options":["Only after wrapping every node with `@traceable`","Automatically once tracing environment variables such as `LANGSMITH_TRACING` and an API key are set","Only when deployed on LangGraph Platform","By exporting checkpoints to LangSmith after the run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"LangChain and LangGraph emit runs to LangSmith when tracing is enabled through environment variables; `@traceable` is for your own non-LangChain code."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/llamaindex","sources":["LlamaIndex documentation (framework, Python)","LlamaIndex documentation — Workflows","LlamaIndex documentation — Agents (FunctionAgent, AgentWorkflow)"],"why":"- **Retrieval is where RAG agents fail.** In practice wrong answers trace back to bad parsing, bad\n  chunking or a missed document far more often than to the model. A framework that makes those steps\n  explicit and swappable pays off quickly.\n- **Hand-rolled ingestion does not scale.** Loading PDFs, slides and HTML, splitting them, embedding,\n  deduplicating re-runs and writing to a vector store is the same 500 lines in every project, with the\n  same bugs.\n- **Indexes need to become tools.** An agent should choose between \"search the HR handbook\" and \"search\n  the contract archive\". LlamaIndex wraps each index as a described tool, so routing is a tool-selection\n  problem the model already handles.\n- **Agents outgrew linear chains.** Retrieve, grade, maybe re-query, then answer is a loop with branches.\n  Workflows model that as steps reacting to events, which stays readable as branches multiply.","how":"### The data layer\n| Abstraction | What it is | Agent-builder note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Document` | a loaded source file plus metadata | keep source, owner and ACL fields in `metadata`; you filter on them later |\n| `Node` | a chunk of a document with links to neighbours | the unit that is embedded, retrieved and cited |\n| Node parser | splitter such as `SentenceSplitter(chunk_size=512, chunk_overlap=50)` | chunking is the first thing to tune when answers miss |\n| `IngestionPipeline` | ordered transformations plus optional docstore and cache | re-runs skip unchanged documents, so nightly syncs stay cheap |\n| `VectorStoreIndex` | nodes plus embeddings in a vector store | defaults to in-memory; point it at a real store for production |\n| Retriever | `index.as_retriever(similarity_top_k=k)` returns scored nodes | use directly when the agent needs raw chunks |\n| Query engine | `index.as_query_engine()`: retrieve, then synthesize an answer | the thing you wrap as a tool |\n\n### Global settings\n- **`Settings`** holds defaults such as `Settings.llm` and `Settings.embed_model`, because most apps use\n  one LLM and one embedder everywhere. Override per component when an agent needs a cheaper model for\n  synthesis than for planning.\n- **Integrations are separate packages,** for example `llama-index-llms-openai` or a vector-store\n  package, imported as `llama_index.llms.openai`. Pin them together with `llama-index-core`.\n\n### From index to tool to agent\n1. **Wrap retrieval as a tool.** `QueryEngineTool.from_defaults(query_engine=..., name=..., description=...)`.\n   The description is a prompt: say what the corpus covers and what input to send.\n2. **Wrap plain functions too.** `FunctionAgent(tools=[fn])` accepts Python functions; the name, type\n   hints and docstring become the tool schema (or use `FunctionTool.from_defaults(fn)` explicitly).\n3. **Build the agent.** `FunctionAgent` uses the model's native tool calling; `ReActAgent` uses a\n   text-based reason-act prompt for models without it.\n4. **Run it.** `await agent.run(user_msg=...)`. Pass a `Context` (`ctx = Context(agent)`) to keep\n   conversation state across runs.\n5. **Go multi-agent if needed.** `AgentWorkflow` holds several agents that hand off to each other by\n   name, with a root agent receiving the first message.\n\n### Workflows: the runtime underneath\n- **Steps are async methods decorated with `@step`.** Each declares the event type it accepts and the\n  event types it may return, via type hints.\n- **Events are typed messages.** `StartEvent` carries the `run(...)` keyword arguments, `StopEvent`\n  carries the result, and you subclass `Event` for everything in between.\n- **Routing comes from types.** Returning `Retrieved` triggers whichever step accepts `Retrieved`, so\n  branches and loops need no explicit graph. The framework validates the step graph before running.\n- **State lives in a `Context`,** shared by steps in a run and serializable, which is how you persist\n  and resume. The exact store API changed across versions (`ctx.store` in recent releases); check the\n  current docs.\n- **A `timeout`** on the workflow bounds a run, which is your first line of defence against loops.","example":"### A policy agent with a retrieval tool and a function tool\nRequires `llama-index-core` and `llama-index-llms-openai` with an OpenAI key; any tool-calling model\nintegration works the same way.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio, os\nfrom datetime import date\nfrom llama_index.core import SimpleDirectoryReader, StorageContext, VectorStoreIndex, load_index_from_storage\nfrom llama_index.core.agent.workflow import FunctionAgent\nfrom llama_index.core.tools import QueryEngineTool\nfrom llama_index.llms.openai import OpenAI\n\nPERSIST = \"./storage\"\nif os.path.exists(PERSIST):                       # reuse embeddings instead of paying again\n    index = load_index_from_storage(StorageContext.from_defaults(persist_dir=PERSIST))\nelse:\n    docs = SimpleDirectoryReader(\"./policies\").load_data()\n    index = VectorStoreIndex.from_documents(docs)\n    index.storage_context.persist(persist_dir=PERSIST)\n\npolicy_tool = QueryEngineTool.from_defaults(\n    query_engine=index.as_query_engine(similarity_top_k=4),\n    name=\"policy_search\",\n    description=\"Answers questions about refund, travel and expense policies. Input: one full question.\",\n)\n\ndef days_between(start: str, end: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Number of days between two ISO dates (YYYY-MM-DD).\"\"\"\n    return (date.fromisoformat(end) - date.fromisoformat(start)).days\n\nagent = FunctionAgent(\n    tools=[policy_tool, days_between],\n    llm=OpenAI(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\"),\n    system_prompt=\"Answer only from policy_search results, name the policy, and say 'not covered' if unsure.\",\n)\n\nasync def main():\n    resp = await agent.run(user_msg=\"Bought 2025-03-01, asking for a refund on 2025-04-10. Eligible?\")\n    print(str(resp))\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **What to notice.** The agent must call `days_between` (40 days) and `policy_search`, then combine\n  them. Arithmetic goes to code, not to the model.\n- **Persisting** the index means the second run embeds nothing; in production the vector store does this.\n\n### A corrective-RAG workflow that refuses on weak evidence\nA workflow makes the \"is the evidence good enough\" branch explicit instead of hoping the agent decides.\n\n```python\nfrom llama_index.core.workflow import Event, StartEvent, StopEvent, Workflow, step\n\nclass Retrieved(Event):\n    question: str\n    texts: list[str]\n\nclass GuardedRAG(Workflow):\n    def __init__(self, index, llm, min_score: float = 0.75, **kw):\n        super().__init__(**kw)\n        self.retriever = index.as_retriever(similarity_top_k=6)\n        self.llm, self.min_score = llm, min_score\n\n    @step\n    async def retrieve(self, ev: StartEvent) -> Retrieved | StopEvent:\n        nodes = await self.retriever.aretrieve(ev.question)\n        good = [n.get_content() for n in nodes if (n.score or 0) >= self.min_score]\n        if not good:                                   # branch by returning a different event\n            return StopEvent(result=\"I could not find a supporting document.\")\n        return Retrieved(question=ev.question, texts=good)\n\n    @step\n    async def answer(self, ev: Retrieved) -> StopEvent:\n        ctx = \"\\n\\n---\\n\\n\".join(ev.texts)\n        out = await self.llm.acomplete(f\"Use only this context:\\n{ctx}\\n\\nQ: {ev.question}\\nA:\")\n        return StopEvent(result=str(out))\n\n# result = await GuardedRAG(index=index, llm=OpenAI(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\"), timeout=60).run(question=\"...\")\n```\n\n- **The score threshold is illustrative.** Similarity scales differ by embedding model and metric;\n  calibrate `min_score` on a labelled set before trusting it.\n- **Scenario numbers.** On an internal eval of 200 questions, a team might find 30 have no answer in\n  the corpus. Without the guard, those become confident hallucinations; with it, they become explicit\n  refusals you can count.","patterns":["**Separate ingestion from serving.** Run `IngestionPipeline` as a scheduled job with a docstore (for deduplication by document hash) and a persistent vector store; the agent service only loads the index. Monitor documents added, skipped and failed per run, because a silent parser failure empties a corpus.","**One tool per corpus, described precisely.** Give each index its own `QueryEngineTool` with a description listing scope and exclusions (\"HR policies; not payroll\"). Measure tool-selection accuracy on a labelled set; vague descriptions are the usual cause of wrong-corpus answers.","**Filter by metadata for tenancy and ACLs.** Store tenant and permission fields on every node and pass metadata filters to the retriever (`MetadataFilters` in `llama_index.core.vector_stores`) per request. Never rely on the prompt to hide another tenant's chunks; test it with a cross-tenant query.","**Trace every retrieval.** Use the instrumentation integrations (for example Langfuse, Arize Phoenix or OpenTelemetry handlers; check the current docs for the hook names) so each trace shows the query, retrieved node IDs, scores and the final prompt. Retrieval bugs are invisible in answer-only logs.","**Evaluate retrieval and answers separately.** Track hit rate and MRR on question-to-document labels, plus faithfulness on answers; LlamaIndex ships evaluation modules, or use your eval tool. Re-run on every chunking or embedding change, because both silently shift quality.","**Bound runs and costs.** Set a workflow `timeout`, cap `similarity_top_k`, use a cheaper LLM for synthesis than for planning, and log tokens per run. Embedding re-runs are the hidden cost; caching in the pipeline avoids paying for unchanged documents."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most enterprise agents are retrieval agents in disguise: the hard part\n  is getting the right chunk from a 400-page PDF, not the loop. LlamaIndex gives you that data layer and\n  turns any index into a tool with one call.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Ingestion jobs (`IngestionPipeline`), the retrieval tool your\n  agent calls (`QueryEngineTool`), the agent itself (`FunctionAgent`, `ReActAgent`, `AgentWorkflow`) and\n  multi-step control flow (`Workflow` with typed events).\n- **What you control.** Parsing, chunk size and overlap, embedding model, top-k, metadata filters,\n  reranking, the vector store behind the index, and the tool descriptions the agent reads.\n- **What you can skip.** The long tail of legacy query-engine variants and older agent classes. Learn\n  indexes, retrievers, query engines, tools, `FunctionAgent` and `Workflow`; the rest is built from them.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Policy and handbook assistants.** HR, IT and finance agents answer from thousands of pages with\n  citations, refusing when no document supports an answer.\n- **Multi-corpus research agents.** One tool per corpus (contracts, tickets, wiki) lets the agent route\n  questions and combine evidence across sources.\n- **Document-heavy back-office workflows.** Invoice, claim or contract intake where parsing and\n  extraction quality decide the outcome more than the agent loop.\n- **Retrieval layer for another framework.** Teams on LangGraph or Pydantic AI use LlamaIndex only for\n  ingestion and retrievers, called from their own tools.","mini_project":"**Build a cited policy agent with a refusal path (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent over 20-50 policy documents that answers with the policy name and refuses when\n  evidence is weak.\n- **Steps:** ingest with `IngestionPipeline` and a persisted index; expose it as a `QueryEngineTool`;\n  add one function tool for date or amount arithmetic; write 25 labelled questions, including five with\n  no answer in the corpus; run them, log retrieved node IDs and scores, then try two chunk sizes.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of hit rate, correct refusals and tokens per answer for both chunk\n  sizes, and every answer in the run cites a real document."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User question\"] --> AG[\"FunctionAgent\"]\n    AG -->|\"tool call\"| QT[\"QueryEngineTool\"]\n    QT --> QE[\"Query engine\"]\n    QE --> R[\"Retriever top-k\"]\n    R --> VS[(\"Vector store index\")]\n    QE -->|\"answer plus source nodes\"| AG\n    AG -->|\"tool call\"| FT[\"Function tools\"]\n    AG --> A[\"Final answer with citations\"]\n    SRC[\"Files and APIs\"] --> IP[\"Ingestion pipeline\"]\n    IP -->|\"parse, chunk, embed\"| VS","caption":"Follow a question from the agent through the retrieval tool to the index and back; ingestion runs\noffline and feeds the same index."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a retriever, a query engine and a QueryEngineTool in LlamaIndex?","a":"- **A retriever** returns scored nodes for a query and nothing else; it is what you evaluate with hit\n  rate and MRR.\n- **A query engine** wraps a retriever plus response synthesis, so it returns an answer with source\n  nodes.\n- **A `QueryEngineTool`** gives that engine a name and description so an agent can choose to call it,\n  which turns corpus routing into ordinary tool selection."},{"q":"You have three corpora: HR policy, contracts and engineering docs. How do you structure the agent?","a":"- **One tool per corpus** with mutually exclusive descriptions, so the model's tool selection does the\n  routing and traces show which corpus was used.\n- **Metadata filters** for anything that is a filter rather than a topic, such as tenant, region or date,\n  because those must be enforced in code.\n- **Evaluate routing** with labelled questions per corpus and add a router step if selection accuracy is\n  low."},{"q":"When would you use a LlamaIndex Workflow instead of a FunctionAgent?","a":"- **Use a Workflow** when the control flow is known: retrieve, grade, re-query or refuse, answer. Typed\n  events make each branch explicit and testable.\n- **Use a FunctionAgent** when the model must decide which tools to call and in what order.\n- **Combine them:** agents run on workflows, so an agent can be one step inside a larger workflow."},{"q":"How do you keep a production LlamaIndex agent from leaking documents across tenants?","a":"- **Tag every node** with tenant and permission metadata at ingestion, because filters can only use\n  fields that exist.\n- **Apply filters in the retriever per request** from the authenticated identity, never from the prompt\n  or the model's arguments.\n- **Test it:** a red-team query from tenant A asking for tenant B's terms must return nothing, and that\n  test runs in CI."},{"q":"What does LlamaIndex cost you compared with writing retrieval by hand?","a":"- **Dependency surface:** many integration packages with their own versions; you must pin and upgrade\n  them together.\n- **Hidden defaults:** prompts and synthesis modes you did not choose until you override them.\n- **What you save:** loaders, parsers, splitters, deduplicating ingestion and store adapters, which are\n  weeks of work and a common source of subtle bugs."}],"tradeoffs":["**Fast retrieval scaffolding vs abstraction depth.** You get working RAG in 20 lines, but defaults (prompt templates, chunk sizes, response synthesis mode) hide decisions. Once quality matters, read and override them, or drop to the retriever and write your own synthesis.","**Broad integrations vs dependency churn.** Hundreds of readers and stores exist as separate packages with independent versions. Pin everything and upgrade deliberately, because minor releases have renamed modules before.","**Workflows vs a graph framework.** Event-typed steps are lighter than LangGraph's explicit graph and fine for retrieval pipelines. Workflows do support human-input events and a serializable `Context`, but for approvals that pause for days with managed checkpoints, LangGraph or a durable-execution engine is the more established choice.","**Using its agents vs only its data layer.** Many teams use LlamaIndex purely for ingestion and retrieval and call it from a LangGraph node or Pydantic AI tool. That flips the choice when the orchestration requirements are heavier than the retrieval ones."],"pitfalls":["**Re-embedding the corpus on every start.** Symptom: slow cold starts and a growing embedding bill. Fix: persist the index or use an external vector store, and load it in the serving process.","**Trusting default chunking for structured documents.** Symptom: tables and clauses split mid-row, answers cite half a rule. Fix: parse with a layout-aware parser and tune chunk size and overlap per document type, then re-run the retrieval eval.","**Copying code across API generations.** Symptom: imports like `from llama_index import ...` or old agent classes failing. Fix: since 0.10 the core lives in `llama_index.core`; use current agent classes from `llama_index.core.agent.workflow` and check the docs version matching your pin.","**Tool descriptions that overlap.** Symptom: the agent queries the wrong index or both, doubling cost. Fix: make descriptions mutually exclusive, or add a router step that picks one corpus first."],"when":"- **Use when** the hard part of your agent is documents: many sources, messy formats, multiple corpora,\n  metadata filters, and you want indexes as tools with little glue.\n- **Use its data layer only when** orchestration lives elsewhere (LangGraph, Pydantic AI, a vendor SDK)\n  but you still want its readers, parsers, splitters and retrievers.\n- **Avoid when** the agent mostly calls APIs with little unstructured data; a vendor agent SDK or\n  Pydantic AI is less to learn and less to pin.\n- **Measure** retrieval hit rate, faithfulness, refusal rate on unanswerable questions, and tokens per\n  answer before and after every ingestion change.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the ingestion pipeline and the retrieval tools, tunes chunking, top-k and filters against a retrieval eval, and wires tracing so every answer shows its source nodes.","architect":"Decides whether LlamaIndex owns only the data layer or the agent runtime too, picks the vector store, and sets the tenancy, ACL and re-indexing strategy.","fde":"Connects a customer's document sources (SharePoint, Confluence, PDFs), proves retrieval quality on their own questions, and explains why some answers are refused."},"check":{"q":"Your LlamaIndex support agent answers well in testing but, after a nightly sync, starts saying\n\"not covered\" for questions it used to answer. What do you check, in order?","a":"First the ingestion run: did the pipeline report documents skipped or failed, did a parser\nchange, did the docstore treat changed files as new and duplicate them, or did the sync point at an\nempty folder? Second the index: is the serving process loading the new vector store collection, and\ndoes it use the same embedding model as ingestion? A mismatch makes every similarity score low, which\ntrips a score threshold or returns irrelevant chunks. Third the retrieval eval: re-run the\nquestion-to-document set and compare hit rate with the last good run. Only then look at the agent's\nprompt or tool descriptions, because they did not change."},"quiz":[{"q":"In a LlamaIndex Workflow, how does control pass from one step to the next?","options":["Each step names the next step explicitly in a graph definition","A step returns a typed event, and the step whose signature accepts that event type runs next","Steps run in the order they are defined in the class","The LLM chooses the next step on every transition"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Steps declare the event types they accept and return through type hints. Returning `Retrieved` or `StopEvent` is the branch; no separate edge list is needed."},{"q":"Why should ingestion run as a separate job rather than inside the agent's serving process?","options":["Because LlamaIndex agents cannot read from a persisted index","Because embeddings can only be created on a GPU worker","Because re-parsing and re-embedding on every start is slow and costly, and a scheduled pipeline with a docstore skips unchanged documents","Because query engines require the index to be rebuilt per request"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The serving process should load an existing index. A scheduled pipeline with deduplication keeps the index fresh while paying only for documents that changed."},{"q":"Where should tenant isolation for retrieval be enforced?","options":["In the system prompt, by telling the agent which tenant it serves","In the tool description of each QueryEngineTool","In metadata filters applied by the retriever from the authenticated identity on each request","By using a different LLM per tenant"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Prompts and descriptions can be ignored or injected against. Filters applied in code from the caller's identity decide which nodes are even candidates."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/openai-agents-sdk","sources":["OpenAI Agents SDK documentation (Python)","OpenAI API documentation — Responses API and function calling"],"why":"- **Every team re-wrote the same loop.** Call the model, parse tool calls, run them, append results,\n  repeat, stop on a final answer. The SDK packages that loop with sane defaults so teams stop owning it.\n- **Multi-agent needed a simple primitive.** A triage agent routing to billing or shipping specialists\n  is common. Handoffs make \"transfer to another agent\" a tool call instead of custom orchestration.\n- **Safety checks were bolted on ad hoc.** Guardrails run validation in parallel with the agent and\n  abort with an exception when a tripwire fires, so cheap checks can block expensive or risky runs.\n- **Agents are undebuggable without traces.** Tracing is on by default and records model calls, tool\n  calls, handoffs and guardrails as spans, so the first bug report already has a timeline.","how":"### The primitives\n| Primitive | What it is | Key fields or calls |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Agent` | an LLM plus instructions and capabilities | `name`, `instructions`, `tools`, `handoffs`, `output_type`, `model`, `model_settings` |\n| Function tool | a Python function exposed as a tool | `@function_tool`; schema from type hints and docstring |\n| Handoff | delegation to another agent, offered to the model as a tool | `handoffs=[agent]`, `handoff_description`, `handoff()` for options |\n| Agent as tool | call another agent and get its result back, without transferring control | `agent.as_tool(tool_name=..., tool_description=...)` |\n| Guardrail | a check on input or final output that can trip and abort | `@input_guardrail`, `@output_guardrail`, `GuardrailFunctionOutput` |\n| Session | stored conversation history across runs | for example `SQLiteSession(session_id)` passed as `session=` (check the docs for current backends) |\n| Context | your typed dependencies, never sent to the model | `context=` on run, `RunContextWrapper[T]` in tools |\n| Trace | a span tree of one workflow | automatic; `with trace(\"name\"):` groups several runs |\n\n### The run loop\n1. **`Runner.run(agent, input)`** (async), `Runner.run_sync` or `Runner.run_streamed` starts a run.\n2. **Input guardrails** of the first agent run concurrently with its first model call.\n3. **The model is called** with instructions, history, tool schemas and handoff tools.\n4. **If it returns a final output** (plain text, or a valid `output_type` object), output guardrails run\n   and the loop ends with `result.final_output`.\n5. **If it calls a handoff,** the current agent changes and the loop continues; `result.last_agent`\n   tells you who finished.\n6. **If it calls tools,** the SDK runs them, appends results and loops.\n7. **If the loop passes `max_turns`,** it raises `MaxTurnsExceeded`, your hard stop for runaway agents.\n\n### Handoffs vs agents-as-tools\n- **Handoff:** the specialist takes over the conversation and answers the user directly. Use it for\n  routing (triage to billing), because the specialist sees the history and owns the reply.\n- **Agent as tool:** a manager agent calls a sub-agent, gets its output back, and stays in charge. Use it\n  when one agent must combine several specialists' results.\n\n### Models and providers\n- **Default is OpenAI's Responses API,** with the Chat Completions API also supported.\n- **Other providers** plug in through a model provider interface and a LiteLLM extension; check the\n  current docs for the import path, because it has moved between releases.\n- **MCP servers** can be attached (`mcp_servers=[...]` on an `Agent`, with stdio and Streamable HTTP\n  server classes in `agents.mcp`), so tools served over MCP look like ordinary tools to the agent.","example":"### Triage with handoffs, a tool and an input guardrail\nRequires `pip install openai-agents` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`. The guardrail is itself a tiny agent with a\nstructured output.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom agents import (Agent, GuardrailFunctionOutput, InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered,\n                    Runner, function_tool, input_guardrail, trace)\n\n@function_tool\ndef get_order_status(order_id: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the shipping status of an order, given its ID such as A100.\"\"\"\n    return {\"A100\": \"shipped 2025-05-02\", \"A200\": \"processing\"}.get(order_id, \"unknown order\")\n\nclass TopicCheck(BaseModel):\n    off_topic: bool\n    reason: str\n\nchecker = Agent(name=\"Topic checker\", model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", output_type=TopicCheck,\n                instructions=\"Flag messages unrelated to orders, billing or refunds.\")\n\n@input_guardrail\nasync def on_topic(ctx, agent, user_input):\n    res = await Runner.run(checker, user_input, context=ctx.context)\n    return GuardrailFunctionOutput(output_info=res.final_output,\n                                   tripwire_triggered=res.final_output.off_topic)\n\norders = Agent(name=\"Orders agent\", handoff_description=\"Order and shipping status questions\",\n               instructions=\"Look up orders with get_order_status. Never guess a status.\",\n               tools=[get_order_status])\nbilling = Agent(name=\"Billing agent\", handoff_description=\"Charges, invoices and refunds\",\n                instructions=\"Explain charges. Refunds need a human: say a ticket was opened.\")\ntriage = Agent(name=\"Triage agent\", instructions=\"Hand off to the right specialist.\",\n               handoffs=[orders, billing], input_guardrails=[on_topic])\n\nasync def main():\n    with trace(\"support-triage\"):\n        try:\n            res = await Runner.run(triage, \"Where is my order A100?\", max_turns=8)\n            print(res.last_agent.name, \"->\", res.final_output)\n        except InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered:\n            print(\"Refused: off-topic request\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **Expected:** `Orders agent -> ...shipped 2025-05-02...`. Ask for a poem instead and the guardrail\n  trips before any specialist runs.\n- **Cost note:** the guardrail adds one small-model call per request, which is cheap insurance when the\n  guarded agent uses a large model and several tools.\n\n### Production shape: typed context, user-scoped tools and a turn budget\nContext carries identity into tools without exposing it to the model, so the model cannot ask for\nanother user's data.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom agents import Agent, MaxTurnsExceeded, RunContextWrapper, Runner, function_tool\n\n@dataclass\nclass UserCtx:\n    user_id: str\n    plan: str\n\nINVOICES = {\"u1\": [\"INV-1\", \"INV-7\"], \"u2\": [\"INV-3\"]}\n\n@function_tool\ndef list_my_invoices(ctx: RunContextWrapper[UserCtx]) -> list[str]:\n    \"\"\"List the signed-in user's invoice IDs.\"\"\"\n    return INVOICES.get(ctx.context.user_id, [])    # identity from code, not from the model\n\nagent = Agent[UserCtx](name=\"Account agent\", tools=[list_my_invoices],\n                       instructions=\"Answer account questions using tools only.\")\n\nasync def handle(user_id: str, message: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        res = await Runner.run(agent, message, context=UserCtx(user_id, \"pro\"), max_turns=6)\n        return res.final_output\n    except MaxTurnsExceeded:\n        return \"This needs a human; I have opened a ticket.\"   # fail closed, log the trace ID\n```\n\n| Decision | Setting in this example | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Identity | `context=UserCtx(...)` | tools read it; the model never sees or sets it |\n| Budget | `max_turns=6` | a looping agent raises instead of burning tokens |\n| Failure | catch `MaxTurnsExceeded` | the user gets a handoff message, ops get a trace |\n| History | add `session=` with a session store | multi-turn chat without hand-managing `to_input_list()` |","patterns":["**Route traces to your own backend too.** Tracing exports to the OpenAI dashboard by default; add a processor with `add_trace_processor` (or use an integration such as Langfuse or Logfire) so traces live next to your other telemetry. Use `set_tracing_disabled(True)` or the documented environment variable when data policy forbids export. Monitor turns, tool errors and handoffs per trace.","**Guard expensive agents with cheap checks.** Put a small-model input guardrail in front of the agent that uses the large model, and an output guardrail on anything user-facing (PII, policy). Count tripwire rate per guardrail; a sudden rise usually means a prompt regression, not an attack.","**Typed final outputs for downstream code.** Set `output_type` to a Pydantic model whenever a program, not a person, consumes the result, so invalid shapes fail inside the run. Track validation failures.","**Keep identity and secrets in context.** Pass user, tenant and clients through `context`, read them via `RunContextWrapper` in tools, and never accept IDs for authorization from tool arguments.","**Persist conversations explicitly.** Use a session store, or save `result.to_input_list()` yourself, keyed by conversation ID. For long or approval-gated runs, wrap runs in a durable workflow engine (the docs describe integrations; check which are current) because the SDK itself keeps no checkpoints.","**Budget and fail closed.** Set `max_turns`, catch `MaxTurnsExceeded` and guardrail exceptions, and record token usage per run from the result's usage data (check the attribute path in your version) so cost per conversation is a dashboard, not a guess."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** It is the thinnest production-grade loop you can adopt: you write\n  agents and Python functions, and the SDK handles tool-call parsing, the loop, handoffs between agents\n  and a trace of every step.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `Agent` definitions, `@function_tool` wrappers around your\n  services, `Runner.run` in your API handler, guardrails in front of expensive agents, and traces in the\n  OpenAI dashboard or your own processor.\n- **What you control.** Instructions, tools, `output_type`, which agents can hand off to which,\n  guardrail logic, `max_turns`, model settings and where traces go.\n- **What you can skip.** Graph DSLs and state machines: the SDK deliberately has none. When you need\n  them, you write plain Python around `Runner.run`, or choose a graph framework.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Customer-support triage.** A triage agent hands off to billing, shipping and account specialists,\n  each with its own tools and instructions.\n- **Internal ops assistants.** Function tools wrap internal APIs (tickets, deploys, on-call), with\n  guardrails blocking out-of-scope requests and traces for audit.\n- **Research managers.** A manager agent calls search and summarise agents as tools and merges their\n  outputs into a typed report.\n- **Voice and realtime front-ends.** The same agents and tools back a voice interface; check the\n  current docs for the realtime and voice features.","mini_project":"**Triage agent with routing eval and a turn budget (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a three-specialist support agent whose routing accuracy you can measure.\n- **Steps:** build triage, orders and billing agents with two mock function tools; add an input\n  guardrail; set `max_turns` and handle `MaxTurnsExceeded`; write 30 labelled user messages; run them\n  and record `last_agent`, turns and tokens; tighten `handoff_description`s and re-run.\n- **Done when:** a before/after table shows routing accuracy and average turns, and you can open the\n  trace of one misrouted message and explain the cause."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant U as App\n    participant R as Runner\n    participant G as Input guardrail\n    participant T as Triage agent\n    participant S as Specialist agent\n    participant F as Function tool\n    U->>R: Runner.run(triage, input)\n    R->>G: check input\n    R->>T: model call with tools and handoffs\n    T-->>R: handoff to specialist\n    R->>S: model call with history\n    S-->>R: tool call get_order_status\n    R->>F: run tool\n    F-->>R: result\n    S-->>R: final output\n    R-->>U: final_output, last_agent, trace","caption":"Trace one run: guardrail and model call start together, then the loop either runs tools, hands off, or\nreturns a final output."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a handoff and an agent used as a tool?","a":"- **Handoff:** control transfers; the receiving agent sees the conversation and produces the final reply,\n  which suits triage and routing.\n- **Agent as tool:** the caller stays in charge, gets the sub-agent's output as a tool result and\n  continues, which suits a manager combining several specialists.\n- **Choose by ownership of the reply:** who should talk to the user at the end decides it."},{"q":"How do guardrails execute, and what are they not good for?","a":"- **Execution:** input guardrails run alongside the first agent's model call; output guardrails run on\n  the final output. A tripped guardrail raises an exception that aborts the run.\n- **Good for:** cheap topic, policy and PII checks that block expensive runs or unsafe replies.\n- **Not good for:** authorization or protecting side-effecting tools mid-run; enforce those in tool code."},{"q":"A run occasionally loops for 40 turns. How do you find and fix the cause?","a":"- **Find it in the trace:** look for repeated identical tool calls, tools returning errors the model keeps\n  retrying, or handoffs bouncing between two agents.\n- **Fix the cause:** clearer tool errors, idempotent tools, exclusive handoff descriptions.\n- **Bound it anyway:** `max_turns` plus a handled `MaxTurnsExceeded` path and an alert."},{"q":"How would you run this SDK in a regulated environment?","a":"- **Traces:** disable default export or add a custom processor that sends spans to an internal backend,\n  with redaction of inputs and outputs.\n- **Models:** pin the model and provider; if a non-OpenAI or private endpoint is required, verify tool\n  calling and structured output support for that exact model.\n- **Data:** keep identity and secrets in context, store sessions in your database, and log tool calls\n  for audit."},{"q":"When would you choose the OpenAI Agents SDK over LangGraph?","a":"- **Choose the SDK** for a straightforward tool loop with a few specialist agents, where speed of\n  delivery and built-in tracing matter most.\n- **Choose LangGraph** when you need explicit state, checkpointers, human interrupts that survive\n  restarts, or complex branching you want to see as a graph.\n- **Either way,** keep tools as plain typed functions so switching costs one module."}],"tradeoffs":["**Small API vs no orchestration model.** A handful of primitives is quick to learn, but there is no graph, checkpointing or interrupt primitive. Flips to LangGraph or a durable engine when runs pause for days.","**OpenAI-first defaults vs portability.** Responses API features and hosted tools work best on OpenAI models; other providers work through adapters with uneven feature support. Test your exact model.","**Handoffs vs explicit routing code.** Handoffs let the model route, which is flexible but probabilistic. When routing rules are fixed (by product or region), a Python `if` before `Runner.run` is cheaper and deterministic.","**Default tracing vs data residency.** Traces with inputs and outputs leave your infrastructure by default. Flips to disabling export or a custom processor for regulated data."],"pitfalls":["**No turn limit in production.** Symptom: a few conversations with huge token bills and 30-step traces. Fix: set `max_turns`, handle `MaxTurnsExceeded`, alert on runs near the limit.","**Letting the model pass the user ID.** Symptom: a prompt-injected request reads another account. Fix: identity comes from `context`, set by your auth layer.","**Vague `handoff_description`s.** Symptom: triage bounces between specialists or picks the wrong one. Fix: make descriptions mutually exclusive and evaluate routing on labelled messages.","**Treating guardrails as complete security.** Symptom: injected tool output still triggers a write. Fix: guardrails are checks on input and final output; enforce permissions in the tools themselves."],"when":"- **Use when** you want a thin, well-traced tool loop with handoffs, mostly on OpenAI models, and your\n  control flow fits \"agent plus tools plus a few specialists\".\n- **Avoid when** you need durable pauses, explicit state machines or heavy multi-provider parity; reach\n  for LangGraph, a durable-execution engine, or Pydantic AI.\n- **Compare with** the Claude Agent SDK, which ships a full computer-access harness (files, shell, MCP)\n  rather than a bare loop.\n- **Measure** turns per run, handoff accuracy, guardrail tripwire rate, tool error rate and cost per run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Defines agents, function tools and guardrails, sets `max_turns` and `output_type`, and debugs runs from the trace timeline rather than from logs.","architect":"Decides where handoffs are acceptable versus coded routing, where traces are exported, and what wraps the SDK for persistence and durable execution.","fde":"Stands up a triage-plus-specialists agent on the customer's systems quickly and shows them the traces as evidence of what the agent did and why."},"check":{"q":"A support agent built with the OpenAI Agents SDK sometimes answers refund questions itself instead\nof handing off to the billing agent, and once issued a refund tool call for the wrong customer. What do\nyou change?","a":"Two separate problems. Routing: make the triage instructions and the billing agent's\n`handoff_description` explicit and mutually exclusive, add a labelled routing eval, and if refund routing\nmust be guaranteed, route in code before calling the runner instead of trusting a handoff. Authorization:\nthe refund tool must take the customer from `context`, which your auth layer sets, not from a model\nargument, and it should require an approval step for any write. Guardrails can add an output check, but\nthe fix for the wrong-customer refund is in the tool's contract, not in the prompt."},"quiz":[{"q":"What happens when an input guardrail's tripwire is triggered?","options":["The agent receives a warning message and continues","The run raises an exception such as InputGuardrailTripwireTriggered, which your code handles","The Runner retries the request with a different model","The guardrail rewrites the input and restarts the run"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Guardrails return `GuardrailFunctionOutput(tripwire_triggered=...)`. When it is true the SDK aborts the run with an exception, so the application decides how to respond."},{"q":"Why pass the user's identity through the run context instead of the prompt?","options":["Context is cheaper because it uses fewer tokens","Context is available to tools in code but is never sent to the model, so the model cannot change or forge it","The model cannot read the prompt during tool calls","Sessions only work when the identity is in the context"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Tools read `ctx.context` through `RunContextWrapper`. Anything in the prompt or tool arguments can be influenced by the model or by injected text."},{"q":"Which mechanism stops an agent that keeps calling tools without finishing?","options":["An output guardrail","A handoff back to the triage agent","The max_turns limit on the run, which raises MaxTurnsExceeded","Disabling tracing for the run"],"answer":2,"explanation":"`max_turns` bounds the loop; handling `MaxTurnsExceeded` lets you fail closed with a clear message and a trace to investigate."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/claude-agent-sdk","sources":["Claude Agent SDK documentation (Python and TypeScript)","Claude Code documentation — hooks, subagents, permissions","Model Context Protocol specification"],"why":"- **Agents that act on computers need many tools done well.** Reading a file range, editing with exact\n  replacements, globbing, running a command with a timeout: each is easy to write badly. The SDK reuses\n  the tools Claude Code has hardened.\n- **Long tasks overflow context.** A 50-step debugging run produces more tool output than fits. The\n  harness compacts and manages context automatically, which a hand-rolled loop must reinvent.\n- **Tool integration should be a protocol, not glue.** Custom tools are MCP tools, in process or\n  external, so the same server serves this agent, Claude Code and any other MCP host.\n- **Autonomy needs policy hooks.** Teams must block `rm -rf`, require approval for writes and log every\n  tool call. Hooks and permission callbacks give deterministic code a veto over the model.","how":"### Two ways to drive it\n| Entry point | Use it for | Shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| `query(prompt=..., options=...)` | one-shot tasks, jobs, CI | async iterator of messages; new session each call |\n| `ClaudeSDKClient(options=...)` | multi-turn conversations, interrupts | `async with` client; `client.query(...)` then `client.receive_response()` |\n\n- **Messages stream as typed objects:** `AssistantMessage` (with `TextBlock` and tool-use blocks),\n  `UserMessage` (tool results), `SystemMessage`, and a final `ResultMessage`.\n- **`ResultMessage`** carries the outcome: `subtype`, `is_error`, `num_turns`, `session_id`, usage and\n  `total_cost_usd`, which is what you log per run.\n\n### Configuration: `ClaudeAgentOptions`\n- **Prompt and model:** `system_prompt`, `model`. Recent versions do not apply the Claude Code system\n  prompt or load filesystem settings by default; opt in (for example `setting_sources=[\"project\"]` to\n  read `CLAUDE.md`). This changed at the rename from the Claude Code SDK, so check the migration notes.\n- **Tools:** `allowed_tools` lists tools pre-approved to run; `disallowed_tools` removes tools.\n  Built-ins include `Read`, `Write`, `Edit`, `Bash`, `Glob`, `Grep`, `WebSearch` and `WebFetch`.\n- **Permissions:** `permission_mode` (`default`, `acceptEdits`, `plan`, `bypassPermissions`) plus a\n  `can_use_tool` callback for decisions in code. In Python the callback needs streaming input (use\n  `ClaudeSDKClient` or an async-iterable prompt), not a plain string prompt.\n- **Scope and limits:** `cwd` for the working directory, `max_turns` for the loop budget.\n- **Sessions:** `resume=<session_id>` continues a conversation; `fork_session` branches from it.\n\n### Custom tools are MCP tools\n1. **Define a tool** with the `@tool(name, description, input_schema)` decorator on an async function\n   that returns MCP-style content (`{\"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": ...}]}`).\n2. **Bundle tools** with `create_sdk_mcp_server(name=..., version=..., tools=[...])`. This runs in your\n   process, with no subprocess or network hop.\n3. **Register** it in `mcp_servers={\"helpdesk\": server}` and allow it as `mcp__helpdesk__get_ticket`\n   (the `mcp__<server>__<tool>` naming).\n4. **External servers** use the same map with a config: a stdio command (`\"type\": \"stdio\"`, `command`,\n   `args`) or a remote URL (`\"type\": \"http\"` for Streamable HTTP, `\"type\": \"sse\"` for the older\n   transport). Streamable HTTP replaced HTTP+SSE in the 2025-03-26 MCP spec; remote servers use the\n   spec's OAuth-based authorization.\n\n### Subagents\n- **Defined with `AgentDefinition`** (description, prompt, tools, optional model) in `options.agents`.\n- **The main agent delegates** through its subagent tool, and each subagent runs with its own context\n  window and tool list, returning only a summary. That keeps the main context small and lets you give a\n  reviewer read-only tools.\n- **The description is the routing signal,** so say when to use it (\"use after any code edit\").\n\n### Hooks\n- **Events** include `PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop` and others; register them\n  as `hooks={\"PreToolUse\": [HookMatcher(matcher=\"Bash\", hooks=[fn])]}`.\n- **A `PreToolUse` hook can deny** a call with a reason the model sees, which is how deterministic\n  policy overrides the model. Return shapes are version-sensitive; check the hooks reference.","example":"### A read-only bug-triage agent with one custom MCP tool\nRequires `pip install claude-agent-sdk` and an Anthropic API key (Bedrock and Vertex are supported via\nenvironment settings). The agent reads a ticket, searches the repo and proposes a fix.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom claude_agent_sdk import (AssistantMessage, ClaudeAgentOptions, ResultMessage, TextBlock,\n                              create_sdk_mcp_server, query, tool)\n\n@tool(\"get_ticket\", \"Fetch a support ticket's text by ID, e.g. T-1\", {\"ticket_id\": str})\nasync def get_ticket(args):\n    tickets = {\"T-1\": \"CSV export times out after 30 seconds for accounts with 50k rows.\"}\n    return {\"content\": [{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": tickets.get(args[\"ticket_id\"], \"not found\")}]}\n\nhelpdesk = create_sdk_mcp_server(name=\"helpdesk\", version=\"1.0.0\", tools=[get_ticket])\n\noptions = ClaudeAgentOptions(\n    system_prompt=\"Triage bugs: read the ticket, find the responsible code, propose a fix. Never edit.\",\n    cwd=\"./repo\",\n    mcp_servers={\"helpdesk\": helpdesk},\n    allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\", \"mcp__helpdesk__get_ticket\"],\n    disallowed_tools=[\"Write\", \"Edit\", \"Bash\"],\n    max_turns=15,\n)\n\nasync def main():\n    async for msg in query(prompt=\"Investigate ticket T-1\", options=options):\n        if isinstance(msg, AssistantMessage):\n            for block in msg.content:\n                if isinstance(block, TextBlock):\n                    print(block.text)\n        elif isinstance(msg, ResultMessage):\n            print(f\"[{msg.subtype}] turns={msg.num_turns} cost={msg.total_cost_usd} session={msg.session_id}\")\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **Why `disallowed_tools` as well:** pre-approving tools and removing tools are different settings;\n  removing writes makes \"read-only\" a property of the configuration, not of the prompt.\n- **Log the `session_id`:** passing it as `resume=` later continues the investigation with full context.\n\n### A fixing agent with a test-runner subagent and a policy hook\nThe main agent may edit; a subagent runs tests with a narrower tool list; a hook vetoes dangerous\ncommands no matter what the model decides.\n\n```python\nfrom claude_agent_sdk import AgentDefinition, ClaudeAgentOptions, HookMatcher\n\nBLOCKED = (\"rm -rf\", \"git push\", \"curl \", \"wget \")\n\nasync def block_dangerous_bash(input_data, tool_use_id, context):\n    cmd = input_data.get(\"tool_input\", {}).get(\"command\", \"\")\n    if any(b in cmd for b in BLOCKED):\n        return {\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n            \"hookEventName\": \"PreToolUse\",\n            \"permissionDecision\": \"deny\",\n            \"permissionDecisionReason\": f\"Blocked by policy: {cmd[:60]}\",\n        }}\n    return {}                                              # no opinion: normal permission flow\n\noptions = ClaudeAgentOptions(\n    cwd=\"./repo\",\n    permission_mode=\"acceptEdits\",                         # file edits auto-approved inside cwd\n    allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\", \"Edit\", \"Bash\", \"Task\"],  # subagent tool; its name varies by version\n    agents={\"test-runner\": AgentDefinition(\n        description=\"Runs the test suite and reports failures. Use after every code edit.\",\n        prompt=\"Run pytest -q, then summarise failing tests and their errors. Never edit files.\",\n        tools=[\"Bash\", \"Read\"],\n    )},\n    hooks={\"PreToolUse\": [HookMatcher(matcher=\"Bash\", hooks=[block_dangerous_bash])]},\n    max_turns=30,\n)\n```\n\n| Layer | Protects against | Deterministic? |\n|---|---|---|\n| System prompt \"never push\" | honest mistakes | no |\n| `disallowed_tools` | whole tool classes | yes |\n| `PreToolUse` hook | specific dangerous arguments | yes |\n| Container with no credentials or egress | everything the hook missed | yes |","patterns":["**Run each agent session in a sandbox.** The harness executes real commands, so host it in a container or VM with only the repository mounted, no production credentials and restricted network egress. The docs discuss ephemeral versus long-running containers; pick per task length. Monitor container CPU, wall time and blocked egress attempts.","**Policy in hooks, not prompts.** Put deny rules for commands and paths in `PreToolUse` hooks and approval logic in `can_use_tool`, and log every decision with the tool input. Count denials per rule; a spike means a prompt or task change is pushing the model toward unsafe actions.","**Keep tools on MCP and reuse them.** Build domain tools as MCP servers (in-process for latency, external stdio or Streamable HTTP for sharing), so the same tools serve this agent, Claude Code and other MCP hosts. Remote servers should use OAuth-scoped tokens per user, as the MCP authorization spec describes, not a shared admin key.","**Track cost and turns from `ResultMessage`.** Store `total_cost_usd`, `num_turns`, usage and `subtype` per run, alert on `error_max_turns`-style subtypes, and set `max_turns` per task class. Cost per successful task is the metric that tells you if a cheaper model works.","**Resume instead of restarting.** Persist `session_id` with the job; on a crash or a follow-up question, start with `resume=` so the agent keeps its context. Use `fork_session` to try two fixes from the same investigated state.","**Evaluate on task outcomes.** For coding agents, the eval is \"tests pass and diff is reviewed\" on a fixed set of issues, run in fresh containers; record success rate, turns and cost per issue."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Instead of a bare tool loop, you get a harness that already reads and\n  edits files, runs commands, searches, compacts long contexts and delegates to subagents. You add your\n  tools and policies, which suits coding, ops and research agents that work on a computer.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `query()` or `ClaudeSDKClient` in your service or job,\n  `ClaudeAgentOptions` as the agent's configuration, in-process MCP servers for your own tools, external\n  MCP servers for SaaS systems, and hooks as your policy layer.\n- **What you control.** System prompt, allowed and disallowed tools, permission mode, working directory,\n  MCP servers, subagent definitions, hooks, turn limits, model, and session resume or fork.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own file-edit, grep or bash tools and context compaction; the\n  harness ships them. Build only the domain tools.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Coding agents in CI.** An agent picks up a labelled issue, reproduces it, edits code, runs tests in a\n  container and opens a pull request for review.\n- **SRE and on-call assistants.** Read logs and metrics through MCP tools, run allowlisted diagnostics,\n  and propose remediations behind an approval step.\n- **Research and analysis agents.** Web search and fetch plus file tools to gather sources, write notes\n  and produce a report, with subagents per sub-question.\n- **Internal workflow automation.** MCP servers for ticketing, CRM and docs let one agent run multi-step\n  back-office tasks with hooks enforcing policy.","mini_project":"**Read-only repo investigator with a policy hook (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent that answers \"where and why does X happen\" in a repository without being able to\n  change anything.\n- **Steps:** write one in-process MCP tool that returns a mock ticket; configure `Read`, `Grep`, `Glob`\n  and a `Bash` hook that allows only `git log` and `ls`; run five investigation prompts; log\n  `ResultMessage` cost, turns and subtype; try to make it delete a file and confirm the hook denies it.\n- **Done when:** five runs have logged cost and turns, every answer cites file paths, and your denial\n  test shows the hook's reason in the transcript."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    APP[\"Your code: query or client\"] --> LOOP[\"Agent loop\"]\n    LOOP --> M[\"Claude model\"]\n    M -->|\"tool call\"| H[\"PreToolUse hooks\"]\n    H --> P{\"Permission check\"}\n    P -->|\"deny\"| LOOP\n    P -->|\"allow\"| BT[\"Built-in tools\"]\n    P -->|\"allow\"| MCP[\"MCP servers\"]\n    P -->|\"allow\"| SA[\"Subagent\"]\n    BT -->|\"result\"| LOOP\n    MCP -->|\"result\"| LOOP\n    SA -->|\"summary\"| LOOP\n    M -->|\"final text\"| R[\"ResultMessage: cost, turns, session\"]","caption":"Follow one turn: the model proposes a tool call, hooks and permissions decide, the tool runs locally or\nover MCP, and the result returns to the loop until a result message ends the run."},"interview":[{"q":"What does the Claude Agent SDK give you that calling the Messages API with tools does not?","a":"- **A harness:** the agent loop, hardened file, shell and search tools, and automatic context\n  management for long runs.\n- **Integration points:** MCP servers for tools, subagents with their own contexts, hooks and\n  permission callbacks for policy, and session resume and fork.\n- **The cost:** a heavier runtime with a subprocess boundary, and coupling to Claude models."},{"q":"How do you add a custom tool, and why is it an MCP server?","a":"- **Define** an async function with `@tool`, bundle it with `create_sdk_mcp_server`, register it in\n  `mcp_servers`, and allow it as `mcp__server__tool`.\n- **Why MCP:** one protocol for in-process and external tools, so the same server can be reused by other\n  MCP hosts and moved out of process (stdio or Streamable HTTP) without changing the agent."},{"q":"Design the safety layers for a coding agent that can run shell commands.","a":"- **Configuration:** remove tools it does not need, set `permission_mode` deliberately, and cap turns.\n- **Hooks:** `PreToolUse` allowlists or denies commands and paths, with reasons the model sees.\n- **Environment:** a disposable container with no secrets and restricted egress, which catches what the\n  hooks miss, plus human review of the diff before merge."},{"q":"When would you use subagents, and what can go wrong?","a":"- **Use them** to isolate noisy work (running tests, searching a large codebase) in a separate context\n  and to give that work narrower tools.\n- **What goes wrong:** the summary drops a detail the main agent needed, or a vague description means the\n  subagent is never invoked. Fix with explicit descriptions and outputs that include file paths and errors."},{"q":"How do you measure and control the cost of these agents in production?","a":"- **Record** `total_cost_usd`, usage, `num_turns` and `subtype` from every `ResultMessage`, keyed by task\n  class.\n- **Control** with `max_turns`, subagents to keep contexts small, a cheaper model for subagents where\n  quality holds, and cost per successful task as the comparison metric."}],"tradeoffs":["**Rich harness vs bare loop.** You inherit strong file, shell and context-management behaviour for free, but also a larger runtime and a subprocess boundary. Flips to a bare API loop or the OpenAI Agents SDK when the agent only calls a few HTTP tools.","**Claude-specific vs portable.** The SDK targets Claude models (directly or through Bedrock and Vertex). Teams needing many providers behind one interface choose Pydantic AI, LangGraph or LiteLLM.","**Autonomy vs blast radius.** Bash and edit tools make agents capable and dangerous in equal measure; `bypassPermissions` is only acceptable inside a disposable sandbox.","**Subagents vs one long context.** Subagents keep the main context small and allow least-privilege tools, but add latency and can lose detail in their summaries. Flips when a task needs the full detail."],"pitfalls":["**Assuming the prompt makes it read-only.** Symptom: an \"analysis\" agent edits files. Fix: remove write tools with `disallowed_tools` and verify in a test.","**Expecting `CLAUDE.md` or project settings to load.** Symptom: project conventions ignored after an upgrade. Fix: opt in with `setting_sources` in recent versions; check the migration guide.","**Running on a developer laptop or CI runner with secrets.** Symptom: the agent can read `.env` files or cloud credentials. Fix: dedicated container, scoped tokens, no ambient credentials.","**Wrong MCP tool names in `allowed_tools`.** Symptom: the tool never runs or is denied. Fix: use `mcp__<server>__<tool>` exactly, with the server key from `mcp_servers`."],"when":"- **Use when** the agent works on a computer: code, files, logs, shell commands, browsing, or long\n  investigations where context management matters, and Claude is your model.\n- **Use when** you already invest in MCP servers and want one tool layer across agents and IDEs.\n- **Avoid when** you need multi-provider parity, or the agent only does short API tool calls where a\n  bare loop is simpler to host.\n- **Measure** success rate per task class, turns, cost per run, hook denials and sandbox violations.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Configures `ClaudeAgentOptions`, writes MCP tools and hooks, sets turn budgets, and debugs runs from the streamed messages and the session transcript.","architect":"Designs the sandbox, credential and network boundary, decides which tools are in process versus shared MCP servers, and owns the permission model.","fde":"Connects the customer's systems as MCP servers with scoped auth, tunes subagents to their workflow, and demonstrates the policy hooks to their security team."},"check":{"q":"You deploy a Claude Agent SDK agent that triages on-call alerts. It must read logs and run\nread-only diagnostic commands, but must never restart services without a human. How do you configure\nit?","a":"Give it `Read`, `Grep` and `Bash`, and a custom MCP tool for the log store. Add a `PreToolUse` hook\non `Bash` that allows only an explicit list of diagnostic commands and denies anything else with a\nreason, rather than trying to list every dangerous command. Expose restarts only as a separate MCP tool\nthat is not pre-approved; route it through `can_use_tool` (or your own approval service) so a human\napproves each call with its arguments. Run the agent in a container with read-only credentials and no\naccess to production write paths, set `max_turns`, and log every hook decision and the `ResultMessage`."},"quiz":[{"q":"How is a custom Python function exposed as a tool to a Claude Agent SDK agent?","options":["By adding it to the system prompt as a JSON schema","By decorating it with @tool, bundling it with create_sdk_mcp_server and registering the server in mcp_servers","By subclassing the built-in Bash tool","By writing it to a file the agent can Read"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Custom tools are MCP tools. The in-process server avoids a subprocess, and the tool is referenced as `mcp__<server>__<tool>` in `allowed_tools`."},{"q":"Which mechanism lets deterministic code block a specific dangerous command even if the model chooses it?","options":["A sentence in the system prompt","Setting max_turns lower","A PreToolUse hook that returns a deny decision for matching tool inputs","Moving the command into a subagent"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Hooks run before the tool executes and can deny the call with a reason. Prompts only influence the model; hooks enforce policy."},{"q":"Why give a test-running subagent its own definition instead of letting the main agent run tests?","options":["Subagents run on faster hardware","It runs in its own context with narrower tools and returns a summary, keeping the main context small","The main agent cannot use the Bash tool","Subagents do not count toward cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Test output is long and noisy. A subagent absorbs it in a separate context, can be restricted to `Bash` and `Read`, and returns only the failures."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/pydantic-ai","sources":["Pydantic AI documentation","Pydantic Evals documentation","Pydantic Logfire documentation"],"why":"- **Structured output alone is not enough.** Libraries such as Instructor validate one call's output;\n  agents also need tools, dependencies, retries across a loop and history. Pydantic AI covers the loop\n  with the same validation discipline.\n- **Globals make agents untestable.** Tools that import a DB client cannot be tested or scoped per user.\n  Dependency injection through `RunContext[Deps]` makes every tool a function of its inputs.\n- **Validation errors are signal.** When the model returns a refund amount of -5, a `ValidationError`\n  or `ModelRetry` is sent back as a retry prompt, so the model fixes it instead of your code crashing.\n- **Teams switch providers.** One `provider:model` string (for example `openai:...`, `anthropic:...`)\n  and a common interface reduce provider lock-in, while still exposing provider-specific settings.","how":"### The core objects\n| Object | Role | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Agent(model, deps_type=..., output_type=..., instructions=...)` | the agent definition, reusable across runs | generic over deps and output types, so type checkers follow it |\n| `RunContext[Deps]` | first parameter of context-aware tools and validators | `ctx.deps` holds your clients and identity; `ctx.usage` the running usage |\n| `@agent.tool` / `@agent.tool_plain` | register tools with or without context | schema from type hints; docstring becomes the description |\n| `output_type` | a Pydantic model, dataclass, scalar or union | validated; on failure the model is asked to retry |\n| `@agent.output_validator` | extra checks that need deps or I/O | raise `ModelRetry(\"why\")` to ask for a corrected output |\n| `ModelRetry` | exception from a tool or validator | becomes a retry message to the model, bounded by `retries` |\n| `UsageLimits` | caps on requests and tokens per run | exceeding raises `UsageLimitExceeded` |\n\n### A run, step by step\n1. **`agent.run(prompt, deps=...)`** (or `run_sync`, `run_stream`, `iter`) starts the loop.\n2. **The model is called** with instructions, history and tool schemas generated from type hints.\n3. **Tool calls are validated** against the schema; invalid arguments go back to the model as a retry.\n4. **Tools run** with `RunContext`; a `ModelRetry` they raise also goes back as a retry message.\n5. **The final output is validated** against `output_type`, then by output validators.\n6. **The run returns** `result.output` (typed), `result.usage()`, and messages via `result.new_messages()`\n   or `result.all_messages()`. Passing `message_history=` continues a conversation.\n- **Retries are bounded.** `retries` on the agent or a tool caps attempts; past that, the run raises\n  `UnexpectedModelBehavior`, which you catch and fail closed on.\n- **Parameter names follow 1.x** (`output_type`, `result.output`); older examples use `result_type` and\n  `result.data`.\n\n### Around the agent\n- **Testing:** `pydantic_ai.models.test.TestModel` and `FunctionModel`, swapped in with\n  `agent.override(model=...)`, plus `models.ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False` in tests.\n- **Observability:** OpenTelemetry instrumentation, with first-class Logfire support\n  (`logfire.instrument_pydantic_ai()`); any OTel backend works.\n- **Evals:** the separate `pydantic_evals` package defines `Case` and `Dataset` objects with evaluators.\n- **Integrations:** MCP servers as toolsets, `FallbackModel` for provider failover, and durable-execution\n  integrations; check the current docs for which engines are supported.","example":"### A typed refund agent with dependencies and validation\nRequires `pip install pydantic-ai` (or `pydantic-ai-slim[openai]`) and a provider key. The deps object\nstands in for a database client.\n\n```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\nfrom pydantic_ai import Agent, ModelRetry, RunContext\n\n@dataclass\nclass Deps:\n    customer_id: str\n    orders: dict[str, dict]            # in production: a DB or API client\n\nclass RefundDecision(BaseModel):\n    order_id: str\n    eligible: bool\n    amount_usd: float = Field(ge=0)\n    reason: str\n\nagent = Agent(\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\", deps_type=Deps, output_type=RefundDecision,\n              instructions=\"Orders under 30 days old and not refunded are eligible for a full refund.\")\n\n@agent.tool\nasync def get_order(ctx: RunContext[Deps], order_id: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Fetch one of the current customer's orders by its ID.\"\"\"\n    order = ctx.deps.orders.get(order_id)\n    if order is None or order[\"customer_id\"] != ctx.deps.customer_id:\n        raise ModelRetry(f\"No order {order_id} for this customer. Ask the user to check the ID.\")\n    return order\n\n@agent.output_validator\nasync def within_total(ctx: RunContext[Deps], out: RefundDecision) -> RefundDecision:\n    order = ctx.deps.orders.get(out.order_id)\n    if order and out.amount_usd > order[\"total_usd\"]:\n        raise ModelRetry(\"Refund amount cannot exceed the order total.\")\n    return out\n\ndeps = Deps(\"c1\", {\"A1\": {\"customer_id\": \"c1\", \"total_usd\": 80.0, \"days_ago\": 12, \"refunded\": False}})\nresult = agent.run_sync(\"Can I get my money back for order A1?\", deps=deps)\nprint(result.output)        # RefundDecision(order_id='A1', eligible=True, amount_usd=80.0, ...)\nprint(result.usage())\n```\n\n- **Three layers of contract:** `Field(ge=0)` in the type, ownership in the tool, cross-field logic in the\n  validator. Each failure becomes a retry, not an exception in your handler.\n- **Ownership check uses deps,** so the model cannot read another customer's order by guessing IDs.\n\n### Production shape: tests without the network, budgets and stored history\nTests run against `TestModel`; production runs cap requests and persist history as JSON.\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom pydantic_ai import models\nfrom pydantic_ai.exceptions import UsageLimitExceeded\nfrom pydantic_ai.messages import ModelMessagesTypeAdapter\nfrom pydantic_ai.models.test import TestModel\nfrom pydantic_ai.usage import UsageLimits\n\nmodels.ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False             # any real model call in tests fails loudly\n\ndef test_agent_returns_refund_decision():\n    with agent.override(model=TestModel(call_tools=[])):   # skip tools, generate a schema-valid output\n        result = agent.run_sync(\"refund A1?\", deps=deps)\n    assert isinstance(result.output, RefundDecision)\n\nasync def handle(msg: str, deps: Deps, history_json: bytes | None):\n    history = ModelMessagesTypeAdapter.validate_json(history_json) if history_json else None\n    try:\n        r = await agent.run(msg, deps=deps, message_history=history,\n                            usage_limits=UsageLimits(request_limit=8))\n    except UsageLimitExceeded:\n        return None, history_json                # fail closed; log and route to a human\n    return r.output, r.all_messages_json()      # store bytes keyed by conversation ID\n```\n\n| Concern | Mechanism | What it buys |\n|---|---|---|\n| Unit tests | `TestModel`, `agent.override` | deterministic, free, no network |\n| Runaway loops | `UsageLimits(request_limit=8)` | a hard stop with a typed exception |\n| Conversations | `message_history`, `all_messages_json()` | stateless service, history in your DB |\n| Bad outputs | `output_type` plus validators | downstream code never sees an invalid object |","patterns":["**Inject everything through deps.** Put DB pools, HTTP clients, the authenticated user and feature flags in a `Deps` dataclass built per request. Tools then scope queries to `ctx.deps.user`, and tests pass fakes. Monitor tool latency per dependency, because slow clients dominate agent latency.","**Make the output type the API contract.** Downstream services consume `result.output` directly, so version that Pydantic model like an API schema. Track retry counts from validation failures per model; a rise after a model upgrade is an early regression signal.","**Instrument with OpenTelemetry.** Call `logfire.configure()` and `logfire.instrument_pydantic_ai()`, or export to another OTel backend, so each run shows model requests, tool calls, retries and token usage. Alert on runs that hit retry limits.","**Budget every run.** Pass `UsageLimits` (requests and tokens), set `retries` per tool, and catch `UsageLimitExceeded` and `UnexpectedModelBehavior` to fail closed. Record `result.usage()` per run for cost dashboards.","**Evaluate with datasets.** Keep a `pydantic_evals` `Dataset` of `Case` objects (input, expected output, evaluators) in the repo and run it in CI on prompt, model or tool changes. Report pass rate and cost per case.","**Fail over providers deliberately.** Use `FallbackModel` with two providers for availability, but run the eval set against both, because a fallback model that breaks your output type is not a fallback."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most agent bugs are contract bugs: a tool got the wrong argument type,\n  the output missed a field, a dependency was a global. Pydantic AI makes deps, tool arguments and outputs\n  typed and validated, and feeds validation errors back to the model to retry.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The agent module of a FastAPI or worker service, tools that\n  receive a `RunContext` with your DB and HTTP clients, an `output_type` your downstream code relies on,\n  and tests that swap in `TestModel`.\n- **What you control.** Model string, instructions, `deps_type`, tools and their retries,\n  `output_type`, output validators, usage limits and message history.\n- **What you can skip.** Graph DSLs for simple agents. Pydantic Graph exists for explicit state\n  machines, but most agents need only `Agent` plus plain Python control flow.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Extraction agents feeding systems.** Invoices, contracts or tickets become validated models that\n  downstream code writes to a database without parsing text.\n- **Customer-facing assistants inside FastAPI services.** Deps carry the user and clients per request,\n  and typed outputs drive UI components.\n- **Decision agents with audit needs.** Typed decisions with reasons and validator checks give\n  reviewers a consistent record to audit.\n- **Multi-provider products.** One agent definition runs on several providers, with `FallbackModel` for\n  availability and evals per provider.","mini_project":"**Typed support-decision agent with tests and an eval (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an agent that returns a validated `Decision` for 20 support messages, with tests that never\n  call a real model.\n- **Steps:** define `Deps` with a fake order store and the current user; add two tools with ownership\n  checks; set an `output_type` and one output validator; write `TestModel` unit tests; build a\n  20-case `pydantic_evals` dataset; run it on two models with `UsageLimits`.\n- **Done when:** unit tests pass offline, and a table shows eval pass rate, retry rate and cost per case\n  for both models."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    C[\"Caller: run with deps\"] --> M[\"Model call\"]\n    M -->|\"tool call\"| V1{\"Args valid?\"}\n    V1 -->|\"no\"| RT[\"Retry message\"]\n    V1 -->|\"yes\"| T[\"Tool with RunContext deps\"]\n    T -->|\"ModelRetry\"| RT\n    T -->|\"result\"| M\n    RT --> M\n    M -->|\"final output\"| V2{\"Output type and validators pass?\"}\n    V2 -->|\"no\"| RT\n    V2 -->|\"yes\"| OUT[\"result.output typed\"]","caption":"Follow how validation failures loop back to the model instead of surfacing as crashes."},"interview":[{"q":"Why does Pydantic AI pass dependencies through RunContext instead of letting tools import clients?","a":"- **Testability:** tools become functions of their inputs, so tests pass fakes without patching\n  globals.\n- **Scoping:** each request builds its own deps with the authenticated user, so tools enforce ownership\n  from code.\n- **Typing:** `RunContext[Deps]` lets type checkers verify every tool uses the deps correctly."},{"q":"What happens when a model returns output that fails your output type?","a":"- **The validation error is sent back** to the model as a retry prompt describing what failed.\n- **Output validators** can raise `ModelRetry` for checks that need data, such as amounts versus totals.\n- **Retries are bounded;** past the limit the run raises `UnexpectedModelBehavior`, which you handle by\n  failing closed."},{"q":"How would you test a Pydantic AI agent in CI?","a":"- **Unit level:** `TestModel` or `FunctionModel` via `agent.override`, with real requests disabled, to\n  test tools, deps wiring and output handling deterministically.\n- **Eval level:** a `pydantic_evals` dataset run against the real model on prompt or model changes, with\n  pass rate and cost reported.\n- **Keep them separate** so unit tests stay fast and free."},{"q":"When would you not choose Pydantic AI?","a":"- **When the hard part is retrieval,** LlamaIndex's data layer does more for you.\n- **When you need multi-day pauses and checkpoints as a first-class graph,** LangGraph is more mature.\n- **When you want a full computer-use harness** with file and shell tools, a vendor harness such as the\n  Claude Agent SDK fits better."},{"q":"How do you bound cost for a Pydantic AI agent in production?","a":"- **`UsageLimits`** on requests and tokens per run, caught as `UsageLimitExceeded`.\n- **`retries`** per tool and output, so validation loops end.\n- **Measure** `result.usage()` per run and cost per successful task, and compare models on an eval set."}],"tradeoffs":["**Explicit types vs speed of prototyping.** Defining deps and output models takes minutes up front and saves hours of debugging later. Flips for throwaway notebooks where a raw API call is faster.","**Thin framework vs batteries.** No built-in retrieval stack, memory store or multi-agent runtime; you compose them (LlamaIndex retrievers as tools, your DB for memory). Flips to a heavier framework when you want those prebuilt.","**Retries on validation vs cost and latency.** Each retry is another model call; strict types with a weak model can double cost. Flips toward looser types or a stronger model when retry rate is high.","**Plain Python control flow vs graphs.** Most flows stay readable as functions calling agents; durable pauses need Pydantic Graph persistence or a durable-execution integration, where LangGraph is more established."],"pitfalls":["**Copying pre-1.0 examples.** Symptom: `result_type` or `result.data` errors. Fix: use `output_type` and `result.output`, and pin the version your examples target.","**Trusting IDs from tool arguments.** Symptom: the agent fetches another user's records. Fix: tools check ownership against `ctx.deps`, set from your auth layer.","**Unbounded `ModelRetry` loops.** Symptom: slow runs that end in `UnexpectedModelBehavior`. Fix: make retry messages specific and actionable, set `retries`, and log which tool or validator retried.","**Tests that silently call real models.** Symptom: slow, flaky, billed CI. Fix: set `models.ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False` and override with `TestModel` or `FunctionModel`."],"when":"- **Use when** your team writes typed Python (FastAPI, Pydantic), the agent's output feeds other code,\n  and you want provider flexibility with testable tools.\n- **Avoid when** you need a prebuilt multi-agent collaboration runtime or heavy retrieval scaffolding\n  out of the box; look at CrewAI, LangGraph or LlamaIndex.\n- **Pairs well with** LlamaIndex retrievers as tools, Logfire or Langfuse for traces, and a durable\n  engine for long-running runs.\n- **Measure** validation retry rate, usage-limit hits, tool error rate, eval pass rate and cost per run.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes `Deps`, tools and output models, adds validators that raise `ModelRetry` with clear messages, and keeps tests on `TestModel` with real-model evals separate.","architect":"Treats output types as versioned contracts between the agent and downstream services, and picks where durability and memory live outside the framework.","fde":"Adapts the deps to the customer's systems and shows them validated, typed outputs their existing code can consume without parsing free text."},"check":{"q":"A Pydantic AI agent extracts invoice data into an `Invoice` model. After switching to a cheaper\nmodel, latency doubled and some runs fail with `UnexpectedModelBehavior`. What is happening and what do\nyou do?","a":"The cheaper model is producing outputs that fail validation (wrong types, missing fields, or your\nvalidator's checks), so each run spends extra requests on retries, and some exceed the retry limit.\nConfirm it in traces by counting retries per run and which field failed. Options: improve field\ndescriptions and instructions so the schema is easier to satisfy, relax types that are stricter than\nthe business needs, raise `retries` only if the success rate justifies the cost, or keep the stronger\nmodel for this agent. Decide on cost per successful extraction from an eval set, not on per-call price."},"quiz":[{"q":"In Pydantic AI, what does raising ModelRetry inside a tool do?","options":["It crashes the run immediately with a stack trace","It sends the message back to the model as a retry prompt, bounded by the configured retries","It retries the HTTP request to the model provider","It switches to a fallback model"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`ModelRetry` turns a tool or validator failure into feedback for the model. Past the retry limit the run raises `UnexpectedModelBehavior`."},{"q":"Where should a tool get the authenticated user's ID from?","options":["From a tool argument the model fills in","From the system prompt","From ctx.deps, built per request by your application","From the previous message in the history"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Deps are set by your code and never by the model, so ownership checks based on them cannot be talked around by the model or by injected text."},{"q":"Which setup keeps unit tests deterministic and free?","options":["Running against the real model with temperature 0","Overriding the agent's model with TestModel and disabling real model requests","Caching real responses in a global variable","Setting UsageLimits to one request"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`TestModel` generates schema-valid responses without network calls, and `ALLOW_MODEL_REQUESTS = False` makes accidental real calls fail."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/crewai","sources":["CrewAI documentation (Agents, Tasks, Crews, Flows, Tools)","CrewAI documentation — Memory and Processes"],"why":"- **Some work decomposes like a team.** Research, then analyse, then write, then review: each step has a\n  different focus and toolset. Role-specific agents keep each prompt short and focused.\n- **Hand-wiring several agents is repetitive.** Passing one agent's output as context to the next,\n  collecting outputs and counting tokens is boilerplate the crew handles.\n- **Fully autonomous crews are hard to control.** Flows were added so that routing, state and\n  branching are ordinary Python, with crews used only where open-ended reasoning helps.\n- **Teams want a readable spec.** YAML files for agents and tasks let non-engineers review roles and\n  task wording separately from code.","how":"### Agents, tasks, crews\n| Object | Key fields | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Agent` | `role`, `goal`, `backstory`, `tools`, `llm`, `allow_delegation`, `max_iter` | role, goal and backstory become the agent's system prompt |\n| `Task` | `description`, `expected_output`, `agent`, `context`, `output_pydantic`, `human_input` | `expected_output` is the stopping target; `context` passes earlier task outputs |\n| `Crew` | `agents`, `tasks`, `process`, `memory`, `verbose`, `max_rpm` | `kickoff(inputs={...})` fills `{placeholders}` in descriptions |\n| `CrewOutput` | `.raw`, `.pydantic`, `.json_dict`, `.tasks_output`, `.token_usage` | log `token_usage` per run |\n\n### Processes\n- **`Process.sequential`:** tasks run in order. A task without `context` receives earlier output\n  automatically; set `context` to choose exactly which task outputs it sees. Predictable and cheapest.\n- **`Process.hierarchical`:** a manager (set `manager_llm` or `manager_agent`) plans, delegates and\n  validates. More flexible, more calls, harder to predict.\n- **Delegation:** with `allow_delegation=True` an agent can hand subtasks or questions to coworkers via\n  built-in tools. Recent versions default it to off, but older examples enable it; keep it off unless\n  you measured a benefit, because it multiplies calls.\n\n### Tools and models\n- **Tools:** decorate a function with `@tool(\"Name\")` from `crewai.tools` (docstring is the description)\n  or subclass `BaseTool` with an `args_schema`. The `crewai-tools` package adds search, scraping and\n  file tools.\n- **Models:** `LLM(model=\"provider/model\", temperature=...)` per agent, so a cheap model can research\n  while a stronger one writes. Provider routing has used LiteLLM; check the current docs for supported\n  providers and native integrations.\n\n### Flows\n1. **Subclass `Flow[State]`** with a Pydantic state model, so every step reads and writes typed fields.\n2. **`@start()`** marks entry steps; **`@listen(step)`** runs after a step; **`@router(step)`** returns\n   a label, and `@listen(\"label\")` steps run for that branch. `or_` and `and_` combine triggers.\n3. **Call crews inside steps** where open-ended work is needed; plain Python handles the rest.\n4. **`flow.kickoff(inputs={...})`** runs it; persistence decorators can save state between runs\n   (check the current docs for `@persist` and backends).\n\n### Project scaffold\n- **`crewai create crew <name>`** generates `agents.yaml`, `tasks.yaml` and a class using `@CrewBase`,\n  `@agent`, `@task` and `@crew` decorators from `crewai.project`, plus a `crewai run` entry point.","example":"### A two-agent research crew with a typed result\nRequires `pip install crewai` and a provider key. The tool returns canned data so the example is\ndeterministic about inputs.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom crewai import LLM, Agent, Crew, Process, Task\nfrom crewai.tools import tool\n\n@tool(\"Fetch competitor pricing\")\ndef fetch_pricing(company: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the public price points for a company name.\"\"\"\n    return {\"Acme\": \"Starter $29/mo, Pro $99/mo\", \"Globex\": \"Team $49/user/mo\"}.get(company, \"no data\")\n\nclass Brief(BaseModel):\n    summary: str\n    price_points: list[str]\n    recommendation: str\n\nllm = LLM(model=\"openai/gpt-4o-mini\", temperature=0.2)\nresearcher = Agent(role=\"Pricing researcher\", goal=\"Collect accurate competitor price points\",\n                   backstory=\"You only report prices returned by your tool.\", tools=[fetch_pricing],\n                   llm=llm, allow_delegation=False, max_iter=5)\nanalyst = Agent(role=\"Pricing analyst\", goal=\"Turn price data into one clear recommendation\",\n                backstory=\"You write short, sourced briefs.\", llm=llm, allow_delegation=False)\n\nresearch = Task(description=\"Collect pricing for {companies}, calling the tool once per company.\",\n                expected_output=\"Each company with its price points\", agent=researcher)\nbrief = Task(description=\"Write a pricing brief for our {product}.\",\n             expected_output=\"Summary, price points and one recommendation\",\n             agent=analyst, context=[research], output_pydantic=Brief)\n\ncrew = Crew(agents=[researcher, analyst], tasks=[research, brief], process=Process.sequential)\nout = crew.kickoff(inputs={\"companies\": \"Acme, Globex\", \"product\": \"analytics add-on\"})\nprint(out.pydantic)          # Brief(...)\nprint(out.token_usage)       # log this per run\n```\n\n- **Why delegation is off:** with two fixed tasks there is nothing to delegate, and enabling it adds\n  coworker-question calls.\n- **`output_pydantic`** makes the last task's result a typed object for the code that consumes it.\n\n### A Flow that routes in code and calls a crew only when needed\nRouting is deterministic Python in the Flow; the billing crew runs only on billing tickets.\n\n```python\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel\nfrom crewai.flow.flow import Flow, listen, router, start\n\nclass TicketState(BaseModel):\n    text: str = \"\"\n    category: str = \"\"\n    reply: str = \"\"\n\nclass SupportFlow(Flow[TicketState]):\n    @start()\n    def classify(self):\n        t = self.state.text.lower()\n        self.state.category = \"billing\" if (\"charge\" in t or \"invoice\" in t) else \"general\"\n\n    @router(classify)\n    def route(self):\n        return self.state.category\n\n    @listen(\"billing\")\n    def run_billing_crew(self):\n        self.state.reply = billing_crew.kickoff(inputs={\"ticket\": self.state.text}).raw  # a Crew built as above\n\n    @listen(\"general\")\n    def general_reply(self):\n        self.state.reply = \"Thanks, routed to the general queue.\"\n\nflow = SupportFlow()\nflow.kickoff(inputs={\"text\": \"I was charged twice on my last invoice\"})\nprint(flow.state.category, \"->\", flow.state.reply)\n```\n\n| Approach for 1,000 tickets/day | Model calls per ticket (illustrative) | Predictability |\n|---|---|---|\n| Hierarchical crew decides everything | manager plus several agent calls on every ticket | low |\n| Flow routes, crew on billing only (say 30%) | zero for general tickets, a few for billing | high |","patterns":["**Flows outside, crews inside.** Put intake, routing, retries and persistence in a `Flow` with typed state, and call crews only for open-ended steps. Monitor share of runs that reach a crew and cost per branch, because that is where spend concentrates.","**Prefer sequential, disable delegation by default.** Start with `Process.sequential` and `allow_delegation=False`; switch to hierarchical only if an eval shows better outcomes. Track calls and tokens per run from `token_usage` before and after.","**Bound iterations and rate.** Set `max_iter` per agent and `max_rpm` on the crew or agent to avoid loops and provider rate-limit errors. Alert on runs that end because the iteration cap was hit.","**Typed outputs and review points.** Use `output_pydantic` on tasks consumed by code, and `human_input=True` (or an approval step in the Flow) before anything with side effects. Count rejected drafts per task to find weak prompts.","**Trace and control telemetry.** Connect an observability integration (the docs list several, including OpenTelemetry-based ones) to see each agent's calls and tool use. CrewAI sends anonymous usage telemetry by default; the docs describe disabling it (for example `OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true`; check the current variable).","**Treat role text as prompts under eval.** Keep `agents.yaml` and `tasks.yaml` in version control and run a fixed eval set on every edit, since a reworded backstory changes behaviour as much as code."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** It is the fastest way to stand up a \"team\" of specialised agents for\n  research, content or analysis pipelines, and it is common in job posts and demos, so you should know\n  where it shines and where it costs you control.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `Agent`, `Task` and `Crew` objects (often from YAML configs), tools\n  built with `@tool` or `BaseTool`, `crew.kickoff(inputs=...)` in a job, and `Flow` classes that route,\n  hold state and call crews.\n- **What you control.** Roles, goals and backstories (all prompts), task descriptions and\n  `expected_output`, task order and `context`, process type, delegation, tools, LLM per agent, and\n  iteration and rate limits.\n- **What you can skip.** Agent \"personalities\" beyond what improves outputs. Backstory text is prompt\n  tokens on every call; keep what measurably helps.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Research and report pipelines.** Researcher, analyst and writer agents produce weekly market,\n  competitor or literature briefs with typed sections.\n- **Content operations.** Drafting, editing and compliance-review agents prepare marketing or docs\n  content, with a human approval step before publishing.\n- **Sales and account prep.** A crew gathers company news, CRM notes and product fit into a meeting\n  brief before each call.\n- **Ticket workflows with selective crews.** A Flow triages tickets in code and runs a specialist crew\n  only for complex categories.","mini_project":"**Sequential research crew inside a routing Flow (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a Flow that routes 20 mixed requests in code and runs a two-agent crew only for research\n  requests, returning a typed brief.\n- **Steps:** build a researcher with one mock tool and a writer with `output_pydantic`; keep delegation\n  off and set `max_iter`; write the Flow with a router; run all 20 requests; record `token_usage`, crew\n  invocations and validation failures; then try hierarchical mode on the research subset.\n- **Done when:** a table compares cost, calls and output validity for sequential versus hierarchical,\n  and only research requests invoked the crew."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    IN[\"Ticket input\"] --> S[\"Flow start: classify\"]\n    S --> RT{\"Router\"}\n    RT -->|\"billing\"| CR[\"Billing crew\"]\n    RT -->|\"general\"| G[\"Plain Python reply\"]\n    CR --> T1[\"Task 1: researcher with tools\"]\n    T1 -->|\"context\"| T2[\"Task 2: writer\"]\n    T2 --> OUT[\"CrewOutput: raw, pydantic, token_usage\"]\n    OUT --> ST[(\"Flow state\")]\n    G --> ST","caption":"Read it top-down: a Flow owns state and routing in code, and only the branch that needs open-ended work\ncalls a crew whose tasks pass context forward."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between a crew and a flow in CrewAI?","a":"- **A crew** is a set of role-based agents executing tasks, sequentially or under a manager, with the\n  framework running each agent's loop.\n- **A flow** is event-driven Python with typed state, start, listen and router steps, where you decide\n  control flow and may call crews inside steps.\n- **Production use** usually puts the flow outside and crews inside where open-ended work helps."},{"q":"When does a role-based multi-agent design beat a single agent with tools?","a":"- **When subtasks need different context or tools** that would bloat one prompt, such as a researcher\n  with search tools and a writer with style rules.\n- **When intermediate outputs benefit from review** by a separately prompted agent.\n- **Otherwise a single agent is cheaper;** prove the gain with an eval rather than assuming it."},{"q":"Your crew's token usage tripled after a small change. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Diff the config:** was delegation enabled, the process switched to hierarchical, or a backstory or\n  task description lengthened?\n- **Read traces:** look for coworker questions, repeated tool calls and iteration-cap hits.\n- **Fix and measure:** revert or bound the change and confirm with `token_usage` on the same inputs."},{"q":"How do you make a CrewAI pipeline safe to run with side effects?","a":"- **Keep side effects in Flow steps or tools** with explicit approval (`human_input=True` or an\n  approval step), not inside free-form agent reasoning.\n- **Type the output** that triggers the action with `output_pydantic` and validate it in code.\n- **Scope tool credentials** to the minimum and log every call."},{"q":"How would you compare CrewAI with LangGraph for a client?","a":"- **CrewAI:** faster to express role-based pipelines, readable YAML specs, less control over prompts and\n  state transitions.\n- **LangGraph:** explicit state graphs, checkpointers and interrupts, more code but more control and\n  easier durable human-in-the-loop.\n- **Decide by** how much of the process is fixed versus open-ended, and how long runs must pause."}],"tradeoffs":["**Quick multi-agent setup vs control.** Roles and tasks get a pipeline running in an afternoon, but the framework writes the prompts and loop around your text. Flips to LangGraph or a hand-rolled loop when you need exact control of every call.","**Specialised agents vs token cost.** Each role adds a system prompt and calls; a single agent with good tools is often cheaper for the same quality. Flips when evals show roles genuinely help.","**Hierarchical flexibility vs predictability.** A manager agent handles varied tasks but adds calls and variance. Sequential plus Flow routing is the usual production compromise.","**Built-in memory vs your own store.** `memory=True` is convenient but its storage and embedding defaults may not fit your data policy; production teams often manage memory in their own database."],"pitfalls":["**Vague `expected_output`.** Symptom: agents ramble, loop or stop early. Fix: state format, length and what \"done\" means; use `output_pydantic` when code consumes it.","**Delegation on by accident.** Symptom: token usage several times higher than expected, with agents asking each other questions. Fix: set `allow_delegation=False` explicitly and check traces.","**Over-long backstories.** Symptom: high input tokens on every call with no quality gain. Fix: trim to the behaviours that matter and confirm with the eval set.","**Letting a crew do deterministic work.** Symptom: an LLM \"classifies\" tickets that a keyword rule or a single small call would handle. Fix: move it into Flow code."],"when":"- **Use when** the work naturally splits into roles (research, analyse, write, review), outputs are\n  documents or reports, and you want a readable spec quickly.\n- **Use Flows when** you need deterministic routing, typed state and selective use of crews.\n- **Avoid when** latency budgets are tight, the task is a single tool loop, or you need fine-grained\n  control of prompts and state; prefer a vendor SDK, Pydantic AI or LangGraph.\n- **Measure** cost and calls per run, task success on an eval set, iteration-cap hits and human\n  rejection rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes agents, tasks, tools and Flows, sets `max_iter`, delegation and output types, and reads traces to cut wasted calls.","architect":"Decides where crews are justified versus deterministic Flow steps, sets memory and telemetry policy, and compares cost per outcome with single-agent designs.","fde":"Prototypes a customer's multi-step content or research process as a crew, then hardens it with Flows, typed outputs and review steps before handover."},"check":{"q":"A CrewAI hierarchical crew producing weekly market reports costs five times what the team\nbudgeted, and output quality varies week to week. What do you change?","a":"Look at traces and `token_usage` first: hierarchical managers and delegation multiply calls, and\nlong backstories inflate every request. Move to `Process.sequential` with explicit task order and\n`context`, set `allow_delegation=False`, cap `max_iter`, and trim role text. Put fixed steps (data\ncollection, formatting) in Flow code or tools rather than agents. Make the final task's\n`expected_output` precise and use `output_pydantic`, so variation shows up as validation failures.\nCompare cost and quality on a fixed set of past weeks before and after each change."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does setting allow_delegation=False on an agent prevent?","options":["The agent from using any tools","The agent from handing subtasks or questions to other agents in the crew","The crew from running tasks sequentially","The agent from receiving context from earlier tasks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Delegation adds coworker tools that let an agent ask or assign work to others. Disabling it keeps the call count predictable; context passing between tasks is unaffected."},{"q":"In a CrewAI Flow, how does a router choose the next step?","options":["The LLM picks from all steps on every transition","Steps run in definition order","The router method returns a label, and steps decorated with @listen for that label run","The crew's manager agent decides"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Routers are ordinary Python that return a string; listeners subscribed to that string run next, which keeps routing deterministic."},{"q":"Which change most directly reduces cost in a crew that runs on every ticket?","options":["Adding a longer backstory to each agent","Switching to Process.hierarchical","Routing in Flow code so the crew runs only for tickets that need it","Enabling delegation so agents share work"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The cheapest model call is the one you do not make. Deterministic routing in the Flow sends only the tickets that need open-ended work to the crew."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/autogen-ag2","sources":["Microsoft AutoGen documentation (AgentChat, Core, Extensions)","AG2 documentation","Wu et al., 2023 — AutoGen: Enabling Next-Gen LLM Applications via Multi-Agent Conversation"],"why":"- **Some problems improve with critique.** A writer plus a reviewer, or a coder plus a test runner,\n  catches errors one pass misses. Conversation is the simplest way to express that loop.\n- **Code execution is a natural tool.** Letting one agent write Python and another run it in a sandbox\n  solves maths, data and file tasks that pure text cannot, and AutoGen made that a first-class pattern.\n- **Group roles need a coordinator.** With several agents, someone must pick the next speaker; group\n  chat managers and selector teams do that with a model or rules.\n- **The original design hit limits.** Synchronous, tightly coupled agents were hard to scale and\n  observe, which drove Microsoft's asynchronous, event-driven 0.4 rewrite and, separately, the AG2 fork.","how":"### Which AutoGen is which\n| Line | Package and import | API style | Status (check current docs) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| AutoGen 0.2 (original, 2023) | `pyautogen`, `import autogen` | `ConversableAgent`, `initiate_chat` | superseded |\n| AG2 (community fork, late 2024) | `ag2` (also published under older names), `import autogen` | 0.2-compatible, extended | actively developed |\n| Microsoft AutoGen 0.4+ (early 2025 rewrite) | `autogen-agentchat`, `autogen-core`, `autogen-ext` | async, event-driven, layered | Microsoft has announced Agent Framework as its successor |\n\n- **Read imports first.** `from autogen import ...` is AG2 or 0.2 style; `from autogen_agentchat ...` is\n  Microsoft 0.4+. Mixing tutorials from both lines is the most common source of confusion.\n\n### Microsoft AutoGen 0.4+ (AgentChat layer)\n- **Model clients** such as `OpenAIChatCompletionClient` (from `autogen_ext.models.openai`) are passed to\n  agents, so models are swappable.\n- **`AssistantAgent(name, model_client=..., tools=[...], system_message=...)`** calls tools; with\n  `reflect_on_tool_use=True` it summarises tool results in natural language.\n- **Teams:** `RoundRobinGroupChat` (fixed turn order), `SelectorGroupChat` (a model picks the next\n  speaker), `Swarm` (agents hand off explicitly), and Magentic-One style orchestration.\n- **Termination conditions** compose with `|` and `&`: `TextMentionTermination(\"APPROVE\") |\n  MaxMessageTermination(8)`.\n- **Run and stream:** `await team.run(task=...)` or `team.run_stream(...)` with `Console` for output;\n  `save_state()` and `load_state()` persist agents and teams.\n- **Core layer** (`autogen-core`) is an actor-style runtime for event-driven agents, including\n  distributed runtimes, for teams building their own patterns.\n\n### AG2 (0.2-style API)\n- **`ConversableAgent`** is the base; `AssistantAgent` is an LLM agent and `UserProxyAgent` stands in for\n  a human and can execute code.\n- **`initiate_chat(recipient, message=...)`** runs a two-agent conversation until\n  `is_termination_msg`, `max_consecutive_auto_reply` or `max_turns` stops it.\n- **`human_input_mode`** (`\"ALWAYS\"`, `\"TERMINATE\"`, `\"NEVER\"`) sets when a human is asked.\n- **Tools** are registered with `register_function(f, caller=..., executor=..., description=...)`: one\n  agent proposes the call, another executes it.\n- **`GroupChat` and `GroupChatManager`** run multi-agent chats with `max_round` and a speaker selection\n  method.","example":"### Investigator and reviewer team (Microsoft AutoGen 0.4+ AgentChat)\nRequires `pip install autogen-agentchat \"autogen-ext[openai]\"` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`. The tool returns\ncanned metrics so you can watch the critique loop.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom autogen_agentchat.agents import AssistantAgent\nfrom autogen_agentchat.conditions import MaxMessageTermination, TextMentionTermination\nfrom autogen_agentchat.teams import RoundRobinGroupChat\nfrom autogen_agentchat.ui import Console\nfrom autogen_ext.models.openai import OpenAIChatCompletionClient\n\nasync def get_error_rate(service: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the 5xx error rate for a service over the last hour.\"\"\"\n    return {\"checkout\": \"4.2% (baseline 0.3%)\", \"search\": \"0.1%\"}.get(service, \"unknown service\")\n\nasync def main():\n    client = OpenAIChatCompletionClient(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\")\n    investigator = AssistantAgent(\n        \"investigator\", model_client=client, tools=[get_error_rate], reflect_on_tool_use=True,\n        system_message=\"Diagnose incidents using tools. Propose one cause, citing tool data.\")\n    reviewer = AssistantAgent(\n        \"reviewer\", model_client=client,\n        system_message=\"Challenge claims without tool evidence. Say APPROVE only when supported.\")\n    stop = TextMentionTermination(\"APPROVE\") | MaxMessageTermination(8)   # always add a hard cap\n    team = RoundRobinGroupChat([investigator, reviewer], termination_condition=stop)\n    await Console(team.run_stream(task=\"Some users cannot check out. Diagnose.\"))\n    await client.close()\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n```\n\n- **The hard cap matters:** without `MaxMessageTermination`, a reviewer that never says APPROVE keeps\n  the loop, and the bill, running.\n- **State:** `await team.save_state()` returns a serialisable dict you can store and later\n  `load_state()` to resume.\n\n### Code-writing assistant with a sandboxed executor (AG2)\nThe classic two-agent pattern: the assistant writes code, the user proxy runs it in Docker and reports\nthe output back.\n\n```python\nimport os\nfrom autogen import AssistantAgent, UserProxyAgent\n\nllm_config = {\"config_list\": [{\"model\": \"gpt-4o-mini\", \"api_key\": os.environ[\"OPENAI_API_KEY\"]}]}\nassistant = AssistantAgent(\"assistant\", llm_config=llm_config)   # default prompt ends with TERMINATE\nexecutor = UserProxyAgent(\n    \"executor\",\n    human_input_mode=\"NEVER\",\n    max_consecutive_auto_reply=5,                                  # hard cap on auto replies\n    is_termination_msg=lambda m: \"TERMINATE\" in (m.get(\"content\") or \"\"),\n    code_execution_config={\"work_dir\": \"scratch\", \"use_docker\": True},  # run code in a container\n)\nexecutor.initiate_chat(assistant, message=\"Compute the median of [3, 9, 4, 12, 7] in Python and print it.\")\n```\n\n| Question | Microsoft AutoGen 0.4+ | AG2 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Programming model | async teams over an event-driven core | synchronous-style chats, 0.2-compatible |\n| Termination | composable condition objects | `is_termination_msg`, `max_consecutive_auto_reply`, `max_turns` |\n| Code execution | `CodeExecutorAgent` with Docker or local executors | `UserProxyAgent` with `code_execution_config` |\n| Best fit | new builds needing async and distribution | existing 0.2 code and its tutorials |\n| Watch | successor Agent Framework direction | package-name overlap with older releases |","patterns":["**Always pair a semantic stop with a hard cap.** Combine a text or handoff condition with `MaxMessageTermination` (or `max_turns` and `max_consecutive_auto_reply` in AG2), and alert on runs that end by the cap, because they usually mean the agents disagreed or looped.","**Execute code only in containers.** Use Docker-based executors with a scratch work directory, no credentials and restricted network; never local execution on a host with secrets. Monitor execution time, exit codes and blocked network calls.","**Persist team state between requests.** Save with `save_state()` after each run and reload by conversation ID, so a service can stay stateless and resume. Version the stored state, since formats can change across releases.","**Trace message flow.** Use the built-in OpenTelemetry support (check the current docs for setup) or log every message with sender, tokens and tool calls. Count messages and tokens per agent; one chatty agent is the usual cost driver.","**Pin the line and version.** Pin `autogen-agentchat` and `autogen-ext` together, or pin `ag2`, and check imports in CI so a dependency resolver cannot pull the other line. Review the Microsoft Agent Framework migration guidance before starting new long-lived projects."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** It popularised the \"agents talk to each other\" pattern: a coder and a\n  code executor, a writer and a critic, a group chat with a manager. You will meet it in research code,\n  prototypes and interviews, so know both lines and their limits.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** `AssistantAgent` and teams such as `RoundRobinGroupChat` or\n  `SelectorGroupChat` in Microsoft AutoGen; `ConversableAgent`, `UserProxyAgent` and `GroupChat` in AG2;\n  Docker-based code executors in both.\n- **What you control.** Agent system messages, tools, which agents participate, speaker selection,\n  termination conditions, code-execution sandboxing and the model client.\n- **What you can skip.** The lineage trivia beyond what you need to pick the right package and read old\n  examples correctly; the next section gives exactly that.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Data-analysis assistants.** An assistant writes pandas code, an executor runs it in Docker, and the\n  pair iterates until the numbers and chart are produced.\n- **Writer and critic loops.** Drafting specs, emails or reports with a reviewer agent that checks them\n  against explicit criteria before a human sees them.\n- **Research prototypes.** Teams explore multi-agent strategies (debate, planner-executor, Magentic-One\n  style orchestration) before hardening the winner.\n- **Incident investigation.** An investigator with read-only tools and a reviewer that rejects claims\n  without evidence, ending in an approved diagnosis.","mini_project":"**Writer-critic team with measured termination (1-2 hours).**\n- **Goal:** show how termination design changes cost and outcome in a two-agent team.\n- **Steps:** build a writer and a critic with AutoGen AgentChat (or AG2); give the critic three\n  concrete approval criteria; run 10 tasks with only a keyword stop (with a high safety cap), then with\n  keyword plus `MaxMessageTermination(6)`; log messages, tokens and termination reason per run.\n- **Done when:** a table compares messages, tokens and approval rate for both settings, and you can\n  name one task where the critic never approved and why."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    T[\"Task\"] --> TEAM[\"RoundRobinGroupChat\"]\n    TEAM --> A[\"Investigator agent\"]\n    A -->|\"tool call\"| TL[\"Tools or code executor\"]\n    TL -->|\"result\"| A\n    A -->|\"message\"| R[\"Reviewer agent\"]\n    R -->|\"message\"| TC{\"Termination condition\"}\n    TC -->|\"APPROVE or max messages\"| OUT[\"TaskResult messages\"]\n    TC -->|\"continue\"| A","caption":"Follow a critic loop: the team alternates speakers, tools run on the investigator's turn, and the\ntermination condition (checked after every message, simplified here to the reviewer's turn), not the\nmodel, decides when the conversation ends."},"interview":[{"q":"Explain the difference between AutoGen 0.2, AG2 and AutoGen 0.4.","a":"- **AutoGen 0.2** introduced conversable agents and two-agent and group chats with synchronous-style\n  APIs.\n- **AG2** is the community fork that keeps and extends that API under the `autogen` import.\n- **AutoGen 0.4** is Microsoft's async, event-driven rewrite with Core, AgentChat and Extensions\n  layers; Microsoft has since announced Agent Framework as the successor, so check its status."},{"q":"How do termination conditions work, and why are they critical?","a":"- **Conditions decide when a conversation ends:** a keyword, a handoff, a message count, a token budget,\n  composable with `|` and `&` in AutoGen 0.4.\n- **They are critical** because agents talking to agents have no natural end; a missing hard cap is the\n  main cause of runaway cost.\n- **Log which condition fired** to find prompts that never converge."},{"q":"How would you safely let an agent execute code it writes?","a":"- **Run it in a container** via a Docker executor with a throwaway work directory.\n- **Remove credentials and restrict network egress,** so generated code cannot reach production systems.\n- **Cap execution time and turns,** and log code plus outputs for review."},{"q":"When is a conversational multi-agent design the wrong choice?","a":"- **When the workflow is known:** fixed steps belong in code or a state machine, which is cheaper and\n  testable.\n- **When runs must pause for approvals for days:** you need durable checkpoints and interrupts.\n- **When latency matters:** every agent turn is a model call, so a group chat is slower than one agent\n  with tools."},{"q":"How do you choose speaker selection in a group chat?","a":"- **Round-robin** for fixed alternation like writer and critic: cheapest and predictable.\n- **Model-based selection** (`SelectorGroupChat`, or AG2's `\"auto\"`) when the right next expert depends\n  on content, accepting an extra call per turn.\n- **Explicit handoffs** (`Swarm`) when agents know who should act next, which is easier to test."}],"tradeoffs":["**Conversation as the abstraction vs explicit control.** Group chats are quick to express and good for critique and brainstorming, but control flow hides in who speaks next. Flips to LangGraph or a Flow when steps and transitions must be auditable.","**Model-selected speakers vs cost and predictability.** `SelectorGroupChat` adapts to the task, but each selection is a model call and selection errors stall progress. Round-robin or explicit handoffs are cheaper and testable.","**Code execution power vs risk.** Running generated code solves real tasks, but the sandbox becomes your security boundary. Flips to predefined tools when the code would touch production data.","**Ecosystem split vs stability.** Two active lines plus an announced successor mean tutorials and APIs diverge. Choose the line whose roadmap you trust for the system's lifetime."],"pitfalls":["**Mixing AG2 and AutoGen 0.4 examples.** Symptom: import errors or classes with unexpected arguments. Fix: check whether code imports `autogen` or `autogen_agentchat`, and follow that line's docs only.","**Relying on \"TERMINATE\" alone.** Symptom: endless polite exchanges (\"Thank you!\", \"You're welcome!\"). Fix: add message caps and make termination criteria concrete in system messages.","**Local code execution in production.** Symptom: generated code reads files or environment variables it should not. Fix: Docker executors, a throwaway work directory, no secrets in the environment.","**Too many agents in one group chat.** Symptom: rising tokens per task and wrong speaker picks. Fix: start with two or three agents and add one only when an eval shows it helps."],"when":"- **Use when** the problem benefits from agents critiquing each other or from generated code executed\n  in a sandbox, especially in research, data analysis and prototyping.\n- **Use Microsoft AutoGen 0.4+ when** you want async, event-driven teams and are comfortable tracking its\n  transition to Agent Framework; use AG2 when you maintain 0.2-style code.\n- **Avoid when** you need deterministic, auditable workflows, durable approval pauses, or tight latency;\n  choose LangGraph, a vendor SDK or Pydantic AI.\n- **Measure** messages and tokens per task, termination reason mix, task success on an eval set and\n  sandbox violations.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds teams with clear termination conditions and caps, wires tools and Docker executors, and reads message traces to cut wasted turns.","architect":"Chooses between the AutoGen lines (or their successor) for the system's lifetime, sets the sandbox boundary for code execution, and decides where conversation-based control is acceptable.","fde":"Uses quick group-chat prototypes to show a customer what multi-agent critique can do, then explains what must be hardened (caps, sandboxing, state) before production."},"check":{"q":"A two-agent AutoGen team (writer and critic) sometimes runs for 40 messages and costs ten times\nthe median. What is going on, and how do you fix it?","a":"The termination condition depends on the critic saying a keyword, and it never does: the critic's\ncriteria are vague, the writer cannot satisfy them, or the pair drifts into pleasantries. Add a hard cap\nwith `MaxMessageTermination` (or `max_turns` in AG2) combined with the semantic condition, and make the\ncritic's approval criteria concrete and checkable. Log the termination reason per run, alert when the\ncap triggers, and review those transcripts to fix the prompts. If critique rarely changes the output,\nmeasure whether the critic is worth its cost at all."},"quiz":[{"q":"You see `from autogen_agentchat.teams import RoundRobinGroupChat` in a codebase. Which line is it?","options":["AG2, the community fork","Microsoft AutoGen 0.4 or later, the AgentChat layer","AutoGen 0.2","CrewAI"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The `autogen_agentchat` package belongs to Microsoft's rewritten AutoGen. AG2 and AutoGen 0.2 use `import autogen` with `ConversableAgent`-style classes."},{"q":"Why combine TextMentionTermination with MaxMessageTermination?","options":["Because TextMentionTermination only works with one agent","Because the keyword may never appear, and a message cap guarantees the conversation ends","Because MaxMessageTermination is required to stream output","Because it reduces tokens per message"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Semantic conditions depend on the model; a hard cap bounds cost and latency when the agents never converge."},{"q":"What is the safest default for running code that an AutoGen or AG2 agent generates?","options":["Run it locally in the service process for speed","Ask the model to promise the code is safe","Execute it in a Docker-based executor with no credentials and restricted network","Disable termination conditions so errors can be fixed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The sandbox is the security boundary for generated code; prompts are not. A container without secrets or egress limits what any generated code can reach."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/google-agent-development-kit","sources":["Google Agent Development Kit documentation","google-adk Python package reference","Google Cloud Vertex AI Agent Engine documentation"],"why":"- **Agents need both reasoning and control flow.** Pure LLM routing is flexible but unpredictable; pure\n  code pipelines are predictable but brittle. ADK puts LLM agents and deterministic workflow agents in the\n  same tree, so you choose per step.\n- **Plumbing dominates early agent work.** Session storage, event history, streaming, tool schemas from\n  Python functions and a local test UI are each a week of glue code. ADK ships them as defaults.\n- **Multi-agent systems need a shared contract.** Every agent in ADK emits the same `Event` stream and\n  reads the same session state, so a sub-agent, a tool and a workflow step compose without adapters.\n- **Shipping is the hard part.** `adk deploy` targets Cloud Run and Vertex AI Agent Engine, and `adk eval`\n  replays test cases, so the path from notebook to a managed, evaluated service is short.","how":"### Core abstractions\n| Abstraction | What it is | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|\n| `LlmAgent` (alias `Agent`) | model + instruction + tools + optional `sub_agents` | the reasoning unit; decides which tool or sub-agent to call |\n| `SequentialAgent`, `ParallelAgent`, `LoopAgent` | workflow agents that run children in a fixed pattern, no LLM | deterministic control flow around LLM steps |\n| Tools | Python functions (wrapped as `FunctionTool`), `AgentTool`, built-ins such as `google_search`, `MCPToolset` | the agent's actions; schemas come from type hints and docstrings |\n| `Runner` | executes an agent for one user turn and yields `Event`s | separates the agent definition from execution |\n| `SessionService` | stores sessions: events plus a `state` dict | conversation persistence and shared scratchpad |\n| `MemoryService` | long-term, cross-session recall | memory beyond one conversation |\n| Callbacks | `before_model_callback`, `before_tool_callback`, `after_tool_callback` and others | guardrails, caching, logging, policy |\n\n### The mental model\n1. **A tree of agents.** The root agent receives the user turn. An `LlmAgent` can delegate to a child in\n   `sub_agents` (transfer) or call another agent as a tool (`AgentTool`), which returns control afterwards.\n2. **Everything is an event.** Model responses, tool calls, tool results and state changes are appended to\n   the session as `Event` objects, because one ordered log makes replay, tracing and eval possible.\n3. **State is the shared blackboard.** `session.state` is a dict visible to every agent. Setting\n   `output_key=\"draft\"` on an `LlmAgent` saves its final text into `state[\"draft\"]`, and later instructions\n   can reference it with `{draft}` templating.\n4. **State prefixes set scope.** No prefix is session scope, `user:` persists across a user's sessions,\n   `app:` is shared by all users, and `temp:` lives for one invocation only.\n5. **Services are swappable.** `InMemorySessionService` for tests, `DatabaseSessionService` for your own\n   SQL database, `VertexAiSessionService` on Google Cloud; agent code does not change.\n\n### When to pick ADK\n| Pick ADK when | Pick something else when |\n|---|---|\n| You deploy on Google Cloud or use Gemini heavily | You want a provider-neutral graph with explicit checkpointing: LangGraph |\n| You want workflow agents and LLM agents in one tree | You want the thinnest SDK over one provider: OpenAI Agents SDK or Claude Agent SDK |\n| You want a built-in dev UI and eval runner | Your team is TypeScript-first: Mastra or the Vercel AI SDK |\n| You plan to expose agents over A2A | You need type-validated outputs above all: Pydantic AI |","example":"### A minimal tool-calling agent\nRequires `pip install google-adk` and a Gemini API key (`GOOGLE_API_KEY`) or Vertex AI credentials. The\nmodel id is illustrative; use one your account supports.\n\n```python\nimport asyncio\nfrom google.adk.agents import Agent\nfrom google.adk.runners import Runner\nfrom google.adk.sessions import InMemorySessionService\nfrom google.genai import types\n\ndef get_order_status(order_id: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Look up the shipping status of an order by its id.\"\"\"\n    fake_db = {\"A100\": \"shipped\", \"A101\": \"processing\"}\n    return {\"order_id\": order_id, \"status\": fake_db.get(order_id, \"not_found\")}\n\nroot_agent = Agent(\n    name=\"support_agent\",\n    model=\"gemini-2.0-flash\",\n    instruction=\"Answer order questions. Always call get_order_status before answering.\",\n    tools=[get_order_status],\n)\n\nasync def main():\n    sessions = InMemorySessionService()\n    await sessions.create_session(app_name=\"support\", user_id=\"u1\", session_id=\"s1\")\n    runner = Runner(agent=root_agent, app_name=\"support\", session_service=sessions)\n    msg = types.Content(role=\"user\", parts=[types.Part(text=\"Where is order A100?\")])\n    async for event in runner.run_async(user_id=\"u1\", session_id=\"s1\", new_message=msg):\n        if event.is_final_response():\n            print(event.content.parts[0].text)\n\nasyncio.run(main())\n# In early releases create_session was synchronous; check the current docs for your version.\n```\n\n### A research pipeline with workflow agents and shared state\nTwo LLM agents run in a fixed order; the first writes to state, the second reads it through templating.\n\n```python\nfrom google.adk.agents import LlmAgent, SequentialAgent\n\nresearcher = LlmAgent(\n    name=\"researcher\", model=\"gemini-2.0-flash\",\n    instruction=\"List 5 key facts about the user's topic as bullets.\",\n    output_key=\"facts\",                      # final text saved to state[\"facts\"]\n)\nwriter = LlmAgent(\n    name=\"writer\", model=\"gemini-2.0-flash\",\n    instruction=\"Write a 120-word brief using only these facts:\\n{facts}\",\n    output_key=\"brief\",\n)\nroot_agent = SequentialAgent(name=\"brief_pipeline\", sub_agents=[researcher, writer])\n# Run it with the same Runner as above, or locally with `adk web` from the parent directory.\n```\n\n| Choice | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `SequentialAgent` instead of one big prompt | each step is small, testable and traced separately |\n| `output_key` instead of parsing chat history | the writer reads exactly one variable, not the whole transcript |\n| Swap writer for a `LoopAgent` with a critic | adds a bounded revise loop when quality needs it |","patterns":["**Persist sessions in a real database.** Replace `InMemorySessionService` with `DatabaseSessionService(db_url=...)` (any SQLAlchemy URL) or `VertexAiSessionService`, because in-memory sessions vanish on every restart or replica switch. Monitor session table growth and prune old events.","**Gate tools with callbacks.** Use `before_tool_callback` to check arguments and user permissions and return a dict to short-circuit the call; use `before_model_callback` for input guardrails. Monitor how often callbacks block, by tool.","**Deploy with the CLI, then pin versions.** `adk deploy cloud_run` or `adk deploy agent_engine` packages the agent; pin `google-adk` in requirements because the API is still evolving. Check the current docs for deploy flags. Monitor cold-start latency and error rate per revision.","**Evaluate trajectories, not only answers.** Record test cases from the dev UI into eval sets and run `adk eval` in CI; ADK scores tool-call trajectory and final response against references. Block merges when the trajectory score drops.","**Trace every invocation.** ADK emits OpenTelemetry spans; export them to Cloud Trace or a vendor such as Langfuse or LangSmith. Monitor tokens per invocation and tool latency.","**Use non-Gemini models deliberately.** Wrap other providers with `LiteLlm(model=\"...\")` from `google.adk.models.lite_llm`, but test each one, because tool-calling quality varies by model."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** ADK gives you the pieces every production agent needs in one package:\n  a tool-calling loop, deterministic orchestration (sequential, parallel, loop), session state, memory,\n  callbacks for guardrails, and an eval runner, without writing the plumbing yourself.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `google-adk` Python package (a Java SDK and other languages also\n  exist), the `adk` CLI (`adk web`, `adk run`, `adk eval`, `adk deploy`), and Google Cloud deployment\n  targets. It is model-agnostic through a LiteLLM wrapper, though Gemini is the first-class path.\n- **What you control.** Agent instructions and tools, how agents are composed into a tree, which session\n  and memory services persist state, and which callbacks gate model and tool calls.\n- **What you can skip.** Vertex-specific services if you are not on Google Cloud; the core agent, runner\n  and in-memory or database session services run anywhere.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Customer support agents on Google Cloud.** An LLM router delegates to billing, shipping and returns\n  sub-agents, with sessions in a database and deployment on Cloud Run.\n- **Research and report pipelines.** `SequentialAgent` and `ParallelAgent` fan out searches, then a writer\n  agent composes from shared state.\n- **Enterprise agents exposed over A2A.** A team publishes an ADK agent that other teams' agents call\n  through the Agent2Agent protocol.\n- **Agents over internal tools via MCP.** `MCPToolset` connects an ADK agent to existing MCP servers\n  without rewriting tools.","mini_project":"**Build and evaluate a three-step ADK pipeline (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** an ADK agent that triages a support ticket, looks up an order with a tool, and drafts a reply,\n  with an eval set that proves the order is always looked up first.\n- **Steps:** write a `get_order_status` tool; build a `SequentialAgent` of triage, lookup and writer agents\n  passing data via `output_key`; add a `before_tool_callback` that blocks unknown order ids; run it in\n  `adk web`, save 5 sessions as eval cases, and run `adk eval`.\n- **Done when:** all 5 eval cases pass on trajectory and response, a blocked-id case shows the callback\n  firing, and sessions survive a restart using `DatabaseSessionService` with SQLite."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User message\"] --> R[\"Runner.run_async\"]\n    R --> S[(\"SessionService: events + state\")]\n    R --> A[\"Root LlmAgent\"]\n    A -->|\"tool call\"| T[\"FunctionTool / MCPToolset\"]\n    A -->|\"transfer\"| W[\"SequentialAgent\"]\n    W --> C1[\"Researcher LlmAgent\"]\n    W --> C2[\"Writer LlmAgent\"]\n    C1 -->|\"output_key\"| S\n    T -->|\"result event\"| A\n    A --> E[\"Event stream to caller\"]\n    E -->|\"final response\"| U","caption":"Follow one user turn: the Runner loads the session, the root agent delegates, and every step lands as an\nevent in the session."},"interview":[{"q":"What is the difference between an LlmAgent and a workflow agent in ADK?","a":"- **LlmAgent reasons.** It uses a model, an instruction and tools, and decides at run time which tool to\n  call or which sub-agent to transfer to.\n- **Workflow agents orchestrate.** `SequentialAgent`, `ParallelAgent` and `LoopAgent` run children in a\n  fixed pattern with no model call, so the control flow is deterministic and testable."},{"q":"How does state flow between agents in an ADK pipeline?","a":"- **Through session state.** Every agent shares `session.state`; `output_key` writes an agent's final text\n  to a key, and later instructions reference it with `{key}` templating.\n- **Scopes matter.** Unprefixed keys are per session, `user:` and `app:` persist more widely, and `temp:`\n  lasts one invocation, so pick the scope that matches the data's lifetime."},{"q":"Your ADK agent works in adk web but loses context in production. What do you check?","a":"- **The session service.** In-memory sessions are per process; with several replicas or after a restart\n  the session is gone. Use `DatabaseSessionService` or `VertexAiSessionService`.\n- **Session ids.** Confirm the client sends the same `user_id` and `session_id` each turn, and that state\n  writes go through events rather than direct mutation."},{"q":"When would you not choose ADK for an agent project?","a":"- **Off Google Cloud with heavy durability needs.** If you need checkpoint and resume across long human\n  approvals on your own infrastructure, LangGraph or a durable workflow engine is more explicit.\n- **TypeScript-first teams or one-provider apps.** Mastra or a provider SDK is simpler when you do not need\n  ADK's multi-agent tree, dev UI or Google deploy targets."},{"q":"How do you add a guardrail that blocks a dangerous tool call?","a":"- **Use `before_tool_callback`.** Inspect the tool name, arguments and context state; returning a dict\n  skips the real call and uses that dict as the tool result.\n- **Log and evaluate it.** Record blocked calls as events and add eval cases that should be blocked, so a\n  prompt change cannot silently remove the guardrail."}],"tradeoffs":["**Batteries included vs lock-in pull.** Sessions, memory, evals and deploy are built in, but the smoothest paths (Agent Engine, Vertex memory) are Google Cloud services; off-cloud you assemble more yourself.","**Agent tree vs explicit graph.** LLM transfer between sub-agents is quick to write but harder to reason about than LangGraph's explicit edges; prefer workflow agents when the order is known.","**Young, fast-moving API vs stability.** New features land often, which also means breaking changes between minor versions; pin and read release notes.","**Gemini-first vs model-agnostic.** Other models work through LiteLLM, but built-in tools such as `google_search` are Gemini-only, and they have had limits on mixing with other tools in one agent (wrapping them in an `AgentTool` is the usual workaround); check the docs for your version."],"pitfalls":["**Keeping the in-memory session service in production.** Symptom: users lose context after a deploy or when load balancing switches replicas. Fix: a database or Vertex session service from day one.","**Letting LLM transfer do deterministic routing.** Symptom: the root agent sometimes skips a required step. Fix: put required steps in a `SequentialAgent` and keep LLM routing for genuinely open choices.","**Weak tool docstrings and untyped arguments.** ADK builds tool schemas from signatures and docstrings, so vague ones cause wrong or missing calls. Fix: type every argument and describe when to use the tool.","**Mutating state outside events.** Writing to a fetched session object directly is not persisted. Fix: use `output_key`, `tool_context.state` or callback context, which record state deltas in events.","**Parallel branches writing the same key.** `ParallelAgent` children share one session state, so two branches with the same `output_key` overwrite each other. Fix: give each branch its own key and merge them in a later step."],"when":"- **Use when:** you build on Google Cloud or Gemini, want sequential, parallel and loop orchestration\n  mixed with LLM agents, or need A2A exposure and a managed runtime.\n- **Avoid when:** you need provider-neutral durable checkpoints with interrupts and resume; LangGraph or a\n  workflow engine fits better.\n- **Measure:** task completion on an eval set, trajectory match rate, tokens and latency per invocation,\n  and tool error rate.\n- **Interviews:** explain the agent tree, the event log, state scopes and why workflow agents exist.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds agents as ADK trees, writes typed tools and callbacks, and runs `adk web` and `adk eval` locally before every change.","architect":"Decides session and memory services, the split between workflow and LLM agents, and whether to run on Agent Engine, Cloud Run or self-managed infrastructure.","fde":"Prototypes a customer agent in the ADK dev UI within a day, then hands over an eval set and a deployment plan that fits the customer's cloud."},"check":{"q":"Your ADK support agent must always verify identity, then look up the account, then answer, but it\nsometimes answers without verifying. How do you restructure it, and how do you prove the fix works?","a":"Move the fixed steps out of LLM routing: a `SequentialAgent` whose first child is a verification\nagent (or a `before_tool_callback` that blocks account tools until `state[\"verified\"]` is true), followed\nby the lookup and answer agents, which pass data through `output_key` state. Keep LLM choice only where\nthe path is genuinely open. Prove it with an eval set that includes unverified users and check the\ntrajectory score in `adk eval`: no account tool call may appear before verification. Persist sessions in\na database so the verified flag survives replicas."},"quiz":[{"q":"What does setting output_key on an LlmAgent do?","options":["It forces the model to return JSON with that key","It saves the agent's final response text into session state under that key","It names the tool the agent must call last","It sets the key used to encrypt the session"],"answer":1,"explanation":"`output_key` writes the agent's final text into `session.state`, which is how a later agent in a `SequentialAgent` reads the result through `{key}` templating without parsing the chat history."},{"q":"Which ADK component should run steps that must always happen in the same order?","options":["An LlmAgent with a strict instruction","The MemoryService","A SequentialAgent","A before_model_callback"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Workflow agents such as `SequentialAgent` run their children in a fixed order without asking a model, so required steps cannot be skipped the way they can when an LLM decides whether to transfer."},{"q":"Why is InMemorySessionService a problem in a multi-replica deployment?","options":["It does not support tool calls","It only works with Gemini models","It cannot stream events","Each process keeps its own sessions, so another replica or a restart loses the conversation"],"answer":3,"explanation":"In-memory sessions live inside one process. Load balancing or a restart sends the next turn somewhere without that history, so production uses a database or Vertex AI session service."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/dspy","sources":["DSPy documentation","Khattab et al., 2023 — DSPy: Compiling Declarative Language Model Calls into Self-Improving Pipelines","Opsahl-Ong et al., 2024 — Optimizing Instructions and Demonstrations for Multi-Stage Language Model Programs"],"why":"- **Prompts are brittle parameters.** A hand-tuned prompt is tied to one model and one wording; changing\n  the model or adding a step means re-tuning by trial and error with no record of why.\n- **Multi-step pipelines are hard to tune by hand.** In a 3-step agent, improving step 1's prompt changes\n  what steps 2 and 3 see; DSPy optimizes steps together against the end-to-end metric.\n- **Few-shot demos are expensive to curate.** Optimizers bootstrap demos by running the program on training\n  inputs and keeping traces that pass the metric, so you need inputs and a metric more than labelled traces.\n- **Quality must be measurable.** DSPy forces you to write a metric and a dev set first, which is the same\n  discipline agent evals require anyway.","how":"### Core abstractions\n| Abstraction | What it is | Analogy |\n|---|---|---|\n| `dspy.LM` + `dspy.configure` | the model client and the global default model | a database connection |\n| `dspy.Signature` | declared inputs, outputs and a task docstring, e.g. `\"question -> answer\"` | a function type signature |\n| Modules (`Predict`, `ChainOfThought`, `ReAct`, your own `dspy.Module`) | strategies that turn a signature into prompts and parse outputs | layers in a neural network |\n| `dspy.Example` | one training or dev example; `.with_inputs(...)` marks input fields | a labelled row |\n| Metric | `def metric(example, pred, trace=None) -> float or bool` | a loss function |\n| Optimizers (formerly teleprompters) | `BootstrapFewShot`, `MIPROv2` and others (recent releases add `GEPA` and `SIMBA`) that compile a program | an optimizer and training loop |\n| `dspy.Evaluate` | runs a program over a dev set and scores it | a test harness |\n\n### The workflow\n1. **Declare.** Write signatures for each step; types (including `Literal` and Pydantic models) become\n   output constraints the adapter parses.\n2. **Compose.** Build a `dspy.Module` whose `forward` calls sub-modules, like a PyTorch model.\n3. **Measure.** Write a metric and run `dspy.Evaluate` on a dev set to get a baseline score.\n4. **Compile.** Pass the program, metric and a train set to an optimizer. `BootstrapFewShot` collects demos\n   that pass the metric; `MIPROv2` also proposes and searches instructions.\n5. **Save and load.** `compiled.save(\"program.json\")` stores the learned instructions and demos;\n   `program.load(...)` restores them in production, so serving does not re-run optimization. The JSON\n   holds state, not code: build the same program class first, then call `load` on it.\n\n### When to pick DSPy\n| Pick DSPy when | Pick something else when |\n|---|---|\n| You have a metric and 20+ examples and want measurable gains | You have no eval set yet: build one first |\n| You switch models often and want to re-tune cheaply | You need a full agent runtime with sessions and deploy: ADK, LangGraph, Mastra |\n| A pipeline has several LLM steps that interact | One structured call is enough: Instructor or provider structured outputs |\n| You want small models to match bigger ones on a narrow task | Your team needs prompts humans can read and edit freely |","example":"### A tool-using agent, a metric and an optimizer\nRequires `pip install dspy` and an API key for the provider; the model string is illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport dspy\n\ndspy.configure(lm=dspy.LM(\"openai/gpt-4o-mini\"))\n\ndef lookup_refund_policy(product: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the refund window for a product category.\"\"\"\n    return {\"laptop\": \"30 days\", \"headphones\": \"14 days\"}.get(product.lower(), \"unknown\")\n\nclass SupportAnswer(dspy.Signature):\n    \"\"\"Answer a customer's refund question using the policy tool.\"\"\"\n    question: str = dspy.InputField()\n    answer: str = dspy.OutputField(desc=\"one sentence that states the refund window\")\n\nagent = dspy.ReAct(SupportAnswer, tools=[lookup_refund_policy], max_iters=4)\n\ntrainset = [\n    dspy.Example(question=\"Can I return my laptop after 3 weeks?\", answer=\"30 days\").with_inputs(\"question\"),\n    dspy.Example(question=\"Refund window for headphones?\", answer=\"14 days\").with_inputs(\"question\"),\n    # ...use 20+ real examples in practice\n]\n\ndef metric(example, pred, trace=None):\n    return example.answer in pred.answer          # did the answer state the right window?\n\nevaluate = dspy.Evaluate(devset=trainset, metric=metric, num_threads=4, display_progress=True)\nprint(evaluate(agent))                              # baseline (DSPy 3.x returns a result with .score)\n\ncompiled = dspy.BootstrapFewShot(metric=metric, max_bootstrapped_demos=4).compile(agent, trainset=trainset)\nprint(evaluate(compiled))                           # use a separate devset in real work\ncompiled.save(\"support_agent.json\")\ndspy.inspect_history(n=1)                           # see the exact prompt DSPy sent\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: moving a ticket router to a cheaper model\nA team routes tickets into 6 queues with a hand-tuned prompt on a large model and wants a small model.\nNumbers are illustrative of the process, not a benchmark.\n\n| Step | Action | Dev-set accuracy |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Hand prompt, large model | 91% |\n| 2 | Same prompt, small model | 78% |\n| 3 | `dspy.Predict` with a `Literal` output field, small model | 80% |\n| 4 | `MIPROv2(metric=metric, auto=\"light\")` on 200 train examples | 89% |\n| 5 | Ship small model with the compiled program; re-run optimization when the queue set changes | stays monitored |\n\n- **What made it work:** a clean metric (exact queue match), separate train and dev sets, and saving the\n  compiled program as a versioned artifact.\n- **What it cost:** one optimization run is many LLM calls; budget it like a training job, and check the\n  current docs for the `auto` presets and their call counts.","patterns":["**Optimize offline, serve the compiled artifact.** Run optimizers in a notebook or CI job, save with `program.save(...)`, version the JSON next to code, and `load` it at startup. Monitor the dev-set score recorded with each artifact version.","**Hold out a dev set and gate releases.** Use `dspy.Evaluate` in CI on a held-out set; block a new compiled program if it scores below the current one. Track per-slice scores so a gain on one category does not hide a regression on another.","**Use a cheap model for bootstrapping, a strong one as teacher when needed.** Optimizers accept settings for a teacher or prompt model (check the current docs per optimizer), so demos can come from a stronger model while the student serves cheaply. Monitor optimization cost in tokens.","**Trace DSPy calls.** Use `dspy.inspect_history` during development and an integration such as MLflow's DSPy autologging or OpenTelemetry instrumentation in production. Monitor tokens per module call.","**Cache and thread evaluations.** DSPy caches LM calls by default, which makes re-runs cheap but can hide model drift; disable or clear the cache when measuring a new model version. Use `num_threads` to speed evaluation within rate limits."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** An agent is a pipeline of LLM calls (route, retrieve, reason, call tools,\n  answer). DSPy lets you tune each call against an eval set instead of editing prompts by feel, and re-tune\n  when you switch models.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `dspy` Python package: `dspy.LM` for models (LiteLLM-style\n  provider strings), `dspy.Signature`, modules like `dspy.Predict`, `dspy.ChainOfThought` and `dspy.ReAct`,\n  `dspy.Evaluate`, and optimizers such as `dspy.BootstrapFewShot` and `dspy.MIPROv2`.\n- **What you control.** The signatures (inputs, outputs, docstring task description), the program's\n  structure, the metric, the training examples and the optimizer budget.\n- **What you can skip.** The research on bootstrapping internals. Know that optimizers search over\n  instructions and demos using your metric, and that more budget costs more LLM calls.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Ticket and intent routers.** Compile a classifier step for a cheaper model and prove accuracy on a\n  held-out set.\n- **RAG query rewriting.** Optimize a rewriter against downstream retrieval recall instead of guessing at\n  prompt wording.\n- **Extraction agents.** Typed signatures with Pydantic outputs, tuned for field-level accuracy on real\n  documents.\n- **Model migrations.** Re-compile an agent's prompts when moving to a new or smaller model and compare\n  dev-set scores.","mini_project":"**Compile a support router and prove the gain (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a DSPy module that routes support messages into 5 queues, optimized for a small model, with a\n  measured improvement over the baseline.\n- **Steps:** collect or write 80 labelled messages and split 50 train and 30 dev; write a signature with a\n  `Literal` output; score `dspy.Predict` with `dspy.Evaluate`; compile with `BootstrapFewShot` and then\n  `MIPROv2` on a light budget; save the best program and load it in a tiny FastAPI endpoint.\n- **Done when:** you have a table of baseline and compiled dev scores with optimization cost, the endpoint\n  loads the saved JSON, and you can show one failing trace and explain it."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    S[\"Signatures\"] --> P[\"Program: dspy.Module\"]\n    T[(\"Train set: dspy.Example\")] --> O[\"Optimizer: MIPROv2\"]\n    P --> O\n    O -->|\"run and trace\"| L[\"LM calls\"]\n    L --> M[\"Metric scores traces\"]\n    M -->|\"keep best demos and instructions\"| O\n    O --> C[\"Compiled program\"]\n    C --> E[\"dspy.Evaluate on dev set\"]\n    C --> J[(\"program.json\")]\n    J --> PR[\"Load in production agent\"]","caption":"Read it as a training loop: the optimizer runs the program on examples, scores traces with the metric,\nand keeps the best instructions and demos."},"interview":[{"q":"What problem do DSPy signatures and optimizers solve compared with prompt templates?","a":"- **Signatures separate what from how.** You declare inputs, outputs and the task; modules generate the\n  prompt and parse the result, so the program is not tied to one wording.\n- **Optimizers tune against a metric.** Instead of editing prompts by feel, an optimizer searches\n  instructions and demos that maximize your metric on examples, and can be re-run for a new model."},{"q":"How would you use DSPy inside a production agent built with another framework?","a":"- **Wrap a step, not the whole agent.** Compile a DSPy module for a measurable step such as routing or\n  query rewriting, and call it from a LangGraph node or an ADK tool.\n- **Ship the artifact.** Load the saved program at startup, log its version, and keep the runtime's own\n  sessions, tracing and approvals."},{"q":"Your optimized program regressed after a model upgrade. How do you handle it?","a":"- **Measure first.** Run `dspy.Evaluate` on the held-out set with the new model and the existing compiled\n  program, with caching disabled so results reflect the new model.\n- **Re-compile if needed.** If the score drops, re-run the optimizer for the new model and keep both\n  artifacts versioned, so rollback is a config change."},{"q":"When is DSPy the wrong tool?","a":"- **No metric or no data.** Without a dev set and a metric, optimizers have nothing to optimize, and DSPy\n  becomes extra abstraction over one prompt.\n- **Runtime concerns dominate.** Sessions, approvals, deployment and streaming UI are not DSPy's job; pick\n  an agent framework and use DSPy only where measurable tuning pays."},{"q":"How do you design a metric for an agent step?","a":"- **Match the downstream goal.** For a router, exact label match; for a retriever query, recall@k of the\n  retrieved documents; for answers, a grounded LLM-judge with a rubric.\n- **Validate the metric.** Read passing and failing traces to confirm the metric agrees with human\n  judgment before trusting optimizer gains."}],"tradeoffs":["**Measured quality vs readable prompts.** Compiled prompts with bootstrapped demos score better but are harder for humans to edit; edits should go through signatures and re-compilation.","**Optimization gains vs cost.** Better optimizers make more LLM calls; on a small dev set the gain can be noise, so the gain is real only when it holds on a held-out set.","**Abstraction vs control.** DSPy owns prompt formatting through adapters, which is great until you need exact provider features; then a thin SDK call inside a custom module is simpler.","**Library, not runtime.** DSPy has no sessions, deployment or human-in-the-loop; pair it with a framework or service for those."],"pitfalls":["**Optimizing without a held-out set.** Symptom: big gains in the notebook, no gain in production. Fix: separate train and dev sets, ideally from real traffic.","**A metric that rewards the wrong thing.** Substring checks pass verbose answers that contain the right token. Fix: inspect failing and passing traces, and use an LLM-judge metric when exact match is too weak.","**Forgetting to load the compiled program.** Symptom: production behaves like the baseline. Fix: load the saved artifact at startup and log its version on every call.","**Re-optimizing after every model change blindly.** Fix: re-evaluate first; re-compile only when the score drops, and compare costs."],"when":"- **Use when:** an agent step has a clear metric and repeatable inputs (routing, extraction, query\n  rewriting, answer grounding) and you want gains you can prove.\n- **Avoid when:** you have no eval data, the task is a single obvious call, or the team needs hand-written\n  prompts for compliance review.\n- **Measure:** dev-set score before and after, optimization cost in calls and dollars, and latency added\n  by demos in the prompt.\n- **Interviews:** explain signatures, modules and optimizers as declare, compose, compile.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes signatures and metrics, runs `dspy.Evaluate` and an optimizer, and ships the saved program as a versioned artifact.","architect":"Decides where DSPy owns prompts (narrow, measurable steps) and where a framework runtime owns orchestration, and budgets optimization runs.","fde":"Uses a customer's labelled tickets to compile a router quickly and shows the before-and-after dev-set score as the evidence."},"check":{"q":"A DSPy-optimized query rewriter scored 15 points higher in the notebook but production retrieval\ndid not improve. What are the likely causes and how do you check each?","a":"First, the gain may be overfit: if the dev set overlapped the train set or was tiny, re-score on a\nheld-out set from real queries. Second, production may not load the compiled program; confirm the saved\nJSON is loaded and its version logged. Third, the metric may not reflect retrieval quality; a rewriter\njudged on string similarity can score well while retrieving worse, so use recall@k of the downstream\nretriever as the metric. Finally, check that cached LM calls during evaluation did not hide a model\nversion difference."},"quiz":[{"q":"In DSPy, what is the role of a signature?","options":["It authenticates calls to the model provider","It declares a step's inputs, outputs and task description, independent of the prompt wording","It stores the optimized few-shot demos","It defines which optimizer to use"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A signature such as \"question -> answer\" declares what a step does. Modules turn it into prompts and parsers, and optimizers later fill in instructions and demos for it."},{"q":"What does BootstrapFewShot need from you to improve a program?","options":["Fully labelled reasoning traces for every example","A fine-tuned model checkpoint","Training examples and a metric that says whether an output is good","A vector database of past prompts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"It runs the program on training inputs, keeps the traces that pass your metric, and uses them as demonstrations, so inputs and a metric are enough; full traces are not required."},{"q":"Why save and load a compiled DSPy program in production?","options":["Loading makes the model run faster on GPUs","The optimizer must run on every request to stay accurate","Saved programs encrypt the prompts","It restores the learned instructions and demos without re-running the expensive optimization"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Optimization makes many LLM calls. Saving the compiled state to JSON and loading it at startup gives production the tuned behaviour at normal per-call cost, and makes versions auditable."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/mastra","sources":["Mastra documentation","Vercel AI SDK documentation"],"why":"- **TypeScript teams need agent infrastructure too.** Most frameworks are Python-first; a Next.js or Node\n  team would otherwise run a second stack just for agents.\n- **The AI SDK is a model layer, not an agent platform.** It handles providers, tool calling and\n  streaming, but not memory threads, workflows, evals or a dev UI; Mastra adds those on top.\n- **Agents and workflows need one model.** Some steps need LLM choice, others need fixed order and\n  human approval. Mastra workflows can call agents as steps, and agents can call workflows as tools.\n- **Fast feedback matters.** `mastra dev` serves a playground to chat with agents, run workflows and\n  inspect traces locally, which shortens the prompt-and-tool iteration loop.","how":"### Core abstractions\n| Abstraction | What it is | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Agent` | name, instructions, model, tools, optional memory | the tool-calling loop; `generate()` for one result, `stream()` for tokens |\n| `createTool` | id, description, Zod `inputSchema` and `outputSchema`, `execute` | typed actions; schemas validate model arguments |\n| `Memory` (`@mastra/memory`) | thread history, working memory, optional semantic recall over a store | multi-turn and cross-session context |\n| Workflows (`createWorkflow`, `createStep`) | typed steps chained with `.then()`, branching and parallel, suspend and resume | deterministic orchestration and human-in-the-loop |\n| `Mastra` instance | registers agents, workflows, storage, logger and telemetry | one place to wire services; powers `mastra dev` and the server |\n| MCP support (`@mastra/mcp`) | MCP client and server helpers | reuse MCP tools or expose your tools |\n\n### The mental model\n1. **Agents decide, workflows enforce.** Put open-ended reasoning in an agent; put required order,\n   retries and approvals in a workflow, because a workflow step cannot be skipped by the model.\n2. **Schemas everywhere.** Tool inputs, tool outputs and workflow step inputs and outputs are Zod schemas,\n   so mismatches fail at the boundary instead of deep inside a prompt.\n3. **Memory is keyed by thread and resource.** A thread is one conversation; a resource is usually the\n   user. Memory reads recent messages and optional semantic matches from storage before each call.\n4. **Storage is pluggable.** LibSQL or SQLite for local work, Postgres for production; workflow snapshots\n   use storage too, which is what makes suspend and resume survive a restart.\n\n### When to pick Mastra\n| Pick Mastra when | Pick something else when |\n|---|---|\n| Your product is TypeScript or Next.js and you want agents in the same codebase | Your team and data stack are Python: LangGraph, ADK, Pydantic AI |\n| You want agents, memory, workflows and a playground together | You only need streaming chat with a few tools: the AI SDK alone |\n| You want typed tools and workflow steps end to end | You need a battle-tested durable engine for long jobs: Temporal or Inngest beside your agents |\n| You want MCP tools in a JS agent quickly | You need the largest ecosystem of integrations: LangChain or LlamaIndex |","example":"### A minimal agent with a typed tool\nRequires `npm i @mastra/core @ai-sdk/openai zod` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`; the model id is illustrative. The\n`execute: async ({ context })` form below is the 0.x signature; 1.x passes the validated input as the\nfirst argument instead, so match the docs for your installed version.\n\n```typescript\nimport { Agent } from \"@mastra/core/agent\";\nimport { createTool } from \"@mastra/core/tools\";\nimport { openai } from \"@ai-sdk/openai\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\n\nconst orderStatus = createTool({\n  id: \"order-status\",\n  description: \"Look up the shipping status of an order by id\",\n  inputSchema: z.object({ orderId: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ orderId: z.string(), status: z.string() }),\n  execute: async ({ context }) => {\n    const db: Record<string, string> = { A100: \"shipped\", A101: \"processing\" };\n    return { orderId: context.orderId, status: db[context.orderId] ?? \"not_found\" };\n  },\n});\n\nexport const supportAgent = new Agent({\n  name: \"support-agent\",\n  instructions: \"Answer order questions. Always call order-status before answering.\",\n  model: openai(\"gpt-4o-mini\"),\n  tools: { orderStatus },\n});\n\nconst res = await supportAgent.generate(\"Where is order A100?\");\nconsole.log(res.text);\n```\n\n### Production wiring: memory, storage and registration\nThis shows the shape teams use; option names for memory and run APIs have shifted between releases, so\nconfirm against the current docs before copying.\n\n```typescript\nimport { Mastra } from \"@mastra/core\";\nimport { Agent } from \"@mastra/core/agent\";\nimport { Memory } from \"@mastra/memory\";\nimport { LibSQLStore } from \"@mastra/libsql\";\nimport { openai } from \"@ai-sdk/openai\";\n\nconst storage = new LibSQLStore({ url: \"file:./mastra.db\" });   // Postgres adapter in production\n\nexport const assistant = new Agent({\n  name: \"assistant\",\n  instructions: \"You are a concise account assistant.\",\n  model: openai(\"gpt-4o-mini\"),\n  memory: new Memory({ storage, options: { lastMessages: 20 } }),\n});\n\nexport const mastra = new Mastra({ agents: { assistant }, storage });\n\n// Each call names the conversation (thread) and the user (resource) so memory is scoped correctly.\nawait assistant.generate(\"My plan is Pro.\", { memory: { thread: \"t-42\", resource: \"user-7\" } });\n```\n\n| Setting | Why |\n|---|---|\n| `lastMessages: 20` | caps history tokens per call instead of sending the whole thread |\n| thread and resource ids from your auth layer | prevents one user's memory leaking into another's |\n| storage shared by memory and workflows | suspended workflows and threads survive deploys |","patterns":["**Deploy the Mastra server or embed in your app.** `mastra build` produces a server you can run in Node or a container; alternatively import agents into Next.js route handlers. Check the current docs for deployer packages per platform. Monitor p95 latency per agent and cold starts on serverless.","**Use Postgres-backed storage in production.** Local LibSQL files do not survive ephemeral containers; a shared database keeps threads, working memory and workflow snapshots across replicas. Monitor storage size per resource and prune old threads.","**Put approvals in workflows with suspend and resume.** A step suspends with a payload, your UI shows it, and a resume call continues from the stored snapshot. Monitor suspended-run age so approvals do not rot.","**Turn on tracing.** Mastra emits OpenTelemetry-compatible traces for agent, tool and workflow spans; export them to Langfuse, LangSmith or any OTel backend. Monitor tokens and tool errors per span.","**Score outputs with evals.** Mastra ships eval and scorer utilities (check the current package name and API); run them in CI on a fixed prompt set and sample live traffic. Track score trends per release."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Many agent products are TypeScript web apps. Mastra gives them the agent\n  loop, typed tools, conversation memory, workflows that persist snapshots to storage and a dev playground without a Python\n  service on the side.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** The `@mastra/core` package (`Agent`, `createTool`, `Mastra`,\n  workflows), `@mastra/memory` plus a storage adapter such as `@mastra/libsql` or Postgres, model providers\n  from the AI SDK (`@ai-sdk/openai` and others), and the `mastra dev` server.\n- **What you control.** Agent instructions, model, tools and their Zod schemas, memory configuration,\n  workflow steps and their schemas, and where storage lives.\n- **What you can skip.** The AI SDK's low-level streaming protocol; Mastra wraps it. Read the AI SDK\n  lesson only when you need custom UI streaming.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **In-app copilots in TypeScript products.** A Next.js SaaS adds an agent that calls its own APIs as typed\n  tools, with per-user memory.\n- **Approval-gated operations.** Workflows draft refunds, emails or config changes and suspend for a human\n  before acting.\n- **Internal tools over MCP.** A JS agent connects to company MCP servers for tickets, docs and CRM.\n- **Multi-step content or data pipelines.** Typed workflow steps call agents for extraction and\n  summarization, with retries.","mini_project":"**Ship an approval-gated refund agent in TypeScript (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a Mastra agent that answers order questions and, for refunds, runs a workflow that suspends for\n  human approval before calling a fake refund API.\n- **Steps:** create a project with `npm create mastra@latest`; add an `order-status` tool and a refund\n  workflow with draft, approval (suspend) and execute steps; give the agent memory backed by LibSQL; test\n  it in `mastra dev`; export traces to a local OTel or Langfuse backend.\n- **Done when:** a refund request pauses in the playground until you resume it, a restart does not lose the\n  suspended run or the thread, and the trace shows each tool and step with token counts."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User message\"] --> A[\"Mastra Agent\"]\n    A --> M[(\"Memory: thread + resource\")]\n    A -->|\"AI SDK model call\"| L[\"LLM\"]\n    L -->|\"tool call\"| T[\"createTool: Zod validated\"]\n    T -->|\"result\"| L\n    A -->|\"workflow as tool\"| W[\"Workflow\"]\n    W --> S1[\"Step: draft\"]\n    S1 --> S2[\"Step: approval, suspend\"]\n    S2 -->|\"resume\"| S3[\"Step: send\"]\n    W --> DB[(\"Storage snapshots\")]\n    L --> R[\"Final answer\"]","caption":"Follow a request: the agent loads memory, calls typed tools in a loop, and hands fixed-order work to a\nworkflow that can pause for approval."},"interview":[{"q":"When do you use a Mastra agent and when a Mastra workflow?","a":"- **Agent for open choices.** An agent picks tools in a loop and suits tasks where the path depends on\n  the conversation, such as support questions.\n- **Workflow for required order.** A workflow runs typed steps in a fixed graph, with branching, parallel\n  steps and suspend for approvals, so required checks cannot be skipped by the model."},{"q":"How does Mastra relate to the Vercel AI SDK?","a":"- **The AI SDK is the model layer.** It provides providers, tool calling and streaming; Mastra agents use\n  AI SDK model objects such as `openai(\"...\")`.\n- **Mastra adds the platform.** Memory, workflows, evals, tracing, MCP helpers and a dev server sit on top,\n  so you would use the AI SDK alone for simple chat and Mastra when you need those."},{"q":"Your agent's token cost doubles over long conversations. How do you debug it?","a":"- **Inspect what memory injects.** Check how many recent messages and semantic recall results are added to\n  each call in the trace.\n- **Cap and summarize.** Lower `lastMessages`, move stable user facts into working memory, and enable\n  semantic recall only for the queries that need it; compare cost per turn before and after."},{"q":"How would you add human approval before an agent sends an email?","a":"- **Make sending a workflow step.** A draft step produces the email, an approval step suspends with the\n  draft as payload, and a send step runs only after resume with an approved flag.\n- **Persist and monitor.** Store snapshots in shared storage so approvals survive deploys, and alert when\n  a suspended run is older than your SLA."},{"q":"What would make you choose a Python framework instead of Mastra?","a":"- **Python-heavy surroundings.** If retrieval, evals, fine-tuning and data pipelines are in Python, a\n  Python framework avoids a cross-language boundary.\n- **Specific runtime needs.** LangGraph's explicit checkpointing or ADK's Google Cloud integration can\n  outweigh Mastra's TypeScript fit."}],"tradeoffs":["**One TypeScript stack vs ecosystem size.** Agents live with your app code and types, but the Python ecosystem has more retrieval, eval and research tooling.","**Integrated features vs churn.** Memory, workflows, evals and a playground in one package speed you up, but the API has changed quickly; pin versions and read migration guides.","**Built-in workflows vs a dedicated engine.** Mastra workflows cover approvals and multi-step jobs well; very long, high-volume or cross-service jobs may still belong in Temporal or Inngest.","**AI SDK foundation vs abstraction depth.** You get every AI SDK provider, but debugging sometimes means reading two layers of library code."],"pitfalls":["**Unbounded memory history.** Symptom: token cost per turn climbs with conversation length. Fix: set a `lastMessages` cap, use working memory for durable facts, and add semantic recall only when needed.","**Missing or shared resource ids.** Symptom: users see each other's context. Fix: derive thread and resource ids from authenticated identity on the server, never from the client body alone.","**Business rules hidden in instructions.** Symptom: the agent sometimes skips a required check. Fix: move the rule into a workflow step or tool validation.","**Copying examples across major versions.** Symptom: type errors on `execute` arguments or memory options. Fix: match examples to your installed version and pin it."],"when":"- **Use when:** your agent ships inside a TypeScript product, you want typed tools, memory and workflows\n  together, and you value a local playground.\n- **Avoid when:** your retrieval, eval and data tooling is Python, or a single AI SDK call already does the\n  job.\n- **Measure:** task success on an eval set, tokens per turn, tool error rate and suspended-workflow age.\n- **Interviews:** explain agent versus workflow, Zod-typed tools, thread and resource memory scoping.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds agents and workflows in TypeScript with Zod-typed tools, tests them in `mastra dev`, and wires memory storage and tracing.","architect":"Decides whether agents live in the web app or a separate Mastra server, which storage backs memory and workflows, and where a dedicated workflow engine is still needed.","fde":"Drops a Mastra agent into a customer's Next.js app, connects their APIs as typed tools or MCP servers, and demos it in the playground."},"check":{"q":"A Mastra assistant in a Next.js app forgets context after deploys and sometimes shows one user's\nplan details to another user. What is wrong and how do you fix both?","a":"Two separate faults. Forgetting after deploys means memory uses local or in-process storage on an\nephemeral container; move memory and workflow storage to a shared Postgres-backed store so threads\nsurvive restarts and replicas. Cross-user leakage means thread or resource ids are wrong: shared,\nconstant, or taken from the client. Derive the resource id from the authenticated user and the thread id\nfrom a server-issued conversation id, then add a test that two users with the same thread name cannot\nread each other's memory."},"quiz":[{"q":"What do Zod schemas on a Mastra tool provide?","options":["Faster model inference","A typed contract the model's arguments and the tool's result are validated against","Automatic retries on network errors","Encryption of tool inputs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The `inputSchema` becomes the tool's JSON schema for the model and validates its arguments; the `outputSchema` checks the result, so bad calls fail at the boundary with a clear error."},{"q":"In Mastra memory, what do thread and resource usually represent?","options":["The model and the provider","A workflow and its steps","One conversation and the user or entity that owns it","The vector index and its embedding model"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A thread is one conversation's history; a resource is typically the user, so working memory and recall can span that user's threads without mixing users."},{"q":"Why put an approval step in a workflow rather than in the agent's instructions?","options":["Workflows use a cheaper model","Instructions cannot mention approvals","Agents cannot call tools after an approval","A workflow step runs in a fixed order and can suspend and resume, so the model cannot skip it"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Instructions are suggestions the model may not follow. A suspended workflow step is enforced by code and stored in a snapshot, so the send step cannot run until someone resumes it."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/agent-memory-libraries","sources":["Mem0 documentation","Letta documentation","Packer et al., 2023 — MemGPT: Towards LLMs as Operating Systems","Chhikara et al., 2025 — Mem0: Building Production-Ready AI Agents with Scalable Long-Term Memory"],"why":"- **Context windows are not memory.** Long histories cost tokens on every call and degrade attention;\n  the agent needs a compact, retrievable store of facts and preferences.\n- **Plain RAG over chat logs is noisy.** Retrieving raw old messages returns contradictions (\"I live in\n  Pune\" then \"I moved to Delhi\"); memory layers extract facts and update or replace stale ones.\n- **Memory needs lifecycle operations.** Users ask to forget; facts change; tenants must not mix. Both\n  tools expose add, search, update and delete keyed by ids.\n- **Some agents must manage memory themselves.** The MemGPT idea, carried into Letta, treats the context\n  window like RAM and external storage like disk, with the agent calling tools to move data between them.","how":"### Mem0: a memory layer you call around your agent\n1. **Add.** After a turn, call `m.add(messages, user_id=...)`. An LLM extracts candidate facts and compares\n   them with existing memories, then adds, updates or deletes entries, because storing every message\n   would duplicate and contradict. Passing `infer=False` skips extraction and stores the messages as\n   given, which is cheaper but loses consolidation.\n2. **Search.** Before the next model call, `m.search(query, user_id=...)` returns the most relevant\n   memories, which you inject into the prompt.\n3. **Manage.** `get_all`, `update`, `delete` and `history` support inspection, corrections and\n   right-to-be-forgotten requests.\n4. **Configure.** `Memory.from_config(...)` picks the LLM, embedder and vector store (Qdrant, pgvector,\n   Chroma and others); an optional graph store adds entity relations. `MemoryClient` targets the hosted\n   platform instead.\n\n### Letta: stateful agents that edit their own memory\n| Concept | What it is | Why it exists |\n|---|---|---|\n| Memory blocks (core memory) | labelled text blocks such as `human` and `persona` always in context | the agent always sees key facts, and can edit them |\n| Archival memory | a vector-searchable store outside the context | long-term facts that do not fit in context |\n| Recall memory | searchable past conversation history | find earlier turns without keeping them in context |\n| Memory tools | built-in tools the agent calls to edit blocks and search or insert into archival memory | the model decides what to remember |\n| Server | agents, state and tools persist in a database behind an API | agents are long-lived objects, not prompts |\n\n### Choosing among memory options\n| Option | Model | Pick when |\n|---|---|---|\n| Mem0 | library or hosted layer; your framework stays in charge | you have an agent already and want per-user memory added |\n| Letta | the server owns the agent, its state and memory | you want long-lived agents that self-manage memory |\n| Framework memory (LangGraph stores, ADK MemoryService, Mastra memory) | built into your runtime | you want fewer moving parts and simple recall |\n| Zep or other memory services | managed memory with temporal knowledge graphs | you need time-aware facts and a managed service |\n| Your own table plus vector search | full control | memory needs are narrow and audit rules are strict |","example":"### Mem0 around a plain chat loop\nRequires `pip install mem0ai openai` and `OPENAI_API_KEY`; the default config uses OpenAI models and a\nlocal vector store. The search return shape has changed across versions, so check the current docs.\n\n```python\nfrom mem0 import Memory\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nmemory = Memory()                      # Memory.from_config({...}) to choose LLM, embedder, vector store\nllm = OpenAI()\n\ndef chat(user_id: str, message: str) -> str:\n    found = memory.search(message, user_id=user_id, limit=5)\n    hits = found[\"results\"] if isinstance(found, dict) else found\n    facts = \"\\n\".join(f\"- {h['memory']}\" for h in hits)\n    msgs = [\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": f\"Known facts about the user:\\n{facts or '- none'}\"},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": message},\n    ]\n    reply = llm.chat.completions.create(model=\"gpt-4o-mini\", messages=msgs).choices[0].message.content\n    memory.add(msgs[1:] + [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": reply}], user_id=user_id)\n    return reply\n\nprint(chat(\"u-7\", \"I'm vegetarian and I live in Pune.\"))\nprint(chat(\"u-7\", \"Suggest a dinner place near me.\"))   # retrieves both facts\nprint(memory.get_all(user_id=\"u-7\"))                    # inspect what was stored\n```\n\n### Letta: create a stateful agent with memory blocks\nRequires a running Letta server (self-hosted or cloud) and `pip install letta-client`. Model handles are\nillustrative; check the current docs for supported names.\n\n```python\nfrom letta_client import Letta\n\nclient = Letta(base_url=\"http://localhost:8283\")\nagent = client.agents.create(\n    model=\"openai/gpt-4o-mini\",\n    embedding=\"openai/text-embedding-3-small\",\n    memory_blocks=[\n        {\"label\": \"human\", \"value\": \"Name unknown. Preferences unknown.\"},\n        {\"label\": \"persona\", \"value\": \"A concise travel assistant that remembers preferences.\"},\n    ],\n)\nresp = client.agents.messages.create(\n    agent_id=agent.id,\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I'm Asha and I only fly window seats.\"}],\n)\nfor m in resp.messages:\n    print(m.message_type, getattr(m, \"content\", \"\"))   # includes the agent's memory-edit tool calls\n# The agent persists on the server: later calls with the same agent_id see the updated human block.\n```\n\n| Question | Mem0 | Letta |\n|---|---|---|\n| Who decides what to remember | an extraction step after each add | the agent, via memory tools |\n| Where the agent loop runs | your framework | the Letta server |\n| Integration effort | two calls around your loop | adopt Letta as the agent runtime |","patterns":["**Scope every memory by tenant and user.** Pass `user_id` (and `agent_id` or run ids where supported) derived from authenticated identity, and filter by tenant in the store, because a missing id becomes a global memory. Monitor queries without a user scope; there should be none.","**Retrieve a few, inject compactly.** Limit search to a handful of results and render them as short bullets in the system prompt. Monitor memory tokens per call and answer quality on an eval set.","**Support deletion and audit.** Wire user \"forget me\" requests to `delete` or `delete_all`, keep `history` for audit, and test that deleted facts never resurface. Monitor deletion latency.","**Add asynchronously when latency matters.** Memory extraction is an extra LLM call; run `add` after the response is sent (a background task or queue) so users do not wait. Monitor extraction failures.","**Self-host Letta with a real database.** Run the Letta server with Postgres, back it up like any stateful service, and version agent configurations. Monitor agent state size and tool errors per agent.","**Evaluate memory explicitly.** Build multi-session test scripts (state a fact, change it, ask later) and score whether the agent uses the latest fact. Track precision of injected memories."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Without memory, every session starts cold and users repeat themselves;\n  with naive memory (dump all history), cost and confusion grow every turn. These libraries extract,\n  store, retrieve and update what matters.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Mem0 as a Python or TypeScript library (`mem0ai` package) over a\n  vector store, or its hosted platform; Letta as a server (self-hosted or cloud) with a client SDK and an\n  agent development UI.\n- **What you control.** What gets stored (extraction prompts or memory tools), scoping ids (user, agent,\n  session), retrieval count, update and deletion policy, and which store backs it.\n- **What you can skip.** Building your own extraction and deduplication pipeline unless you have unusual\n  needs; the concepts are in the agent-memory lesson, and these tools implement them.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Personal assistants and copilots.** Remember preferences, names and ongoing projects across sessions\n  without resending history.\n- **Customer support agents.** Recall a customer's past issues and device details so they do not repeat\n  themselves.\n- **Long-lived companion or tutor agents.** Letta-style agents that maintain a model of the learner and\n  update it as they progress.\n- **Sales and account agents.** Keep account context, stakeholders and commitments per customer, scoped by\n  tenant.","mini_project":"**Add long-term memory to a chat agent and test it across sessions (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a CLI or small web agent that remembers user preferences across restarts with Mem0, scoped per\n  user, with deletion.\n- **Steps:** configure Mem0 with a local vector store; wrap your chat loop with `search` and `add`; run a\n  scripted 3-session scenario where a preference changes in session 2; add a `/forget` command calling\n  delete for that user; optionally repeat the scenario with a Letta agent and compare.\n- **Done when:** session 3 uses the updated preference, a second user never sees the first user's facts,\n  `/forget` leaves `get_all` empty, and you have a table of memory tokens per turn."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"User turn\"] --> AG[\"Your agent loop\"]\n    AG -->|\"search by user_id\"| M0[\"Mem0\"]\n    M0 --> VS[(\"Vector store\")]\n    AG --> LLM[\"LLM call with memories\"]\n    LLM -->|\"add after turn\"| M0\n    U --> LS[\"Letta server agent\"]\n    LS --> CB[\"Core memory blocks\"]\n    LS -->|\"memory tools\"| AR[(\"Archival + recall\")]\n    LS --> DB[(\"Agent state DB\")]","caption":"Compare the two shapes: Mem0 wraps your agent's calls, while Letta is the agent server and edits its own\nmemory through tools."},"interview":[{"q":"How does Mem0's approach to memory differ from Letta's?","a":"- **Mem0 is a layer.** Your agent calls `search` before and `add` after a turn; an extraction step decides\n  which facts to add, update or delete in a vector store.\n- **Letta is a runtime.** Agents live on a server with memory blocks in context and archival storage\n  outside it, and the agent itself calls tools to edit what it remembers."},{"q":"Why not just store every message in a vector database and retrieve it?","a":"- **Raw messages are noisy and contradictory.** Old and new statements conflict, and retrieval returns\n  fragments without knowing which is current.\n- **Memory layers consolidate.** Extracting facts and updating existing entries keeps memory small,\n  current and cheap to inject, with history kept for audit."},{"q":"A user asks your agent to forget everything about them. What must happen?","a":"- **Delete across stores.** Call the memory library's delete for that user, and also purge raw logs,\n  caches, traces and backups according to policy, because memory is not the only copy.\n- **Prove it.** Run a follow-up check that searches return nothing for that user and that the agent no\n  longer uses the facts."},{"q":"How do you evaluate whether long-term memory helps your agent?","a":"- **Multi-session scenarios.** Scripts that state facts, change them, and ask questions sessions later;\n  score whether the answer uses the latest correct fact.\n- **Track cost and precision.** Measure memory tokens per call and the share of injected memories that were\n  relevant, and compare task success with memory on and off."},{"q":"Where should memory scoping be enforced?","a":"- **On the server, from identity.** Derive user and tenant ids from authentication, never from model output\n  or client-supplied fields alone.\n- **In the store too.** Filter by tenant at query time and test cross-tenant isolation, because a single\n  missing id can leak memories across users."}],"tradeoffs":["**Extracted facts vs raw history.** Extraction keeps memory small and current, but it can drop nuance or store a wrong inference; keep raw logs elsewhere for audit.","**Layer vs runtime.** Mem0 slots into any framework; Letta gives richer self-managed memory but asks you to run its server as the agent runtime.","**Self-managed memory vs predictability.** Letting the agent edit its own memory is flexible, but the model may store irrelevant or sensitive details; constrain block sizes and review edits.","**Hosted vs self-hosted.** Hosted platforms cut operations work but place user facts with a vendor, which may conflict with data residency rules."],"pitfalls":["**Global memories from missing ids.** Symptom: one user's preference appears for another. Fix: require ids at the call site and add a test that fails without them.","**Storing secrets and sensitive data.** Users paste card numbers or health details, which extraction happily saves. Fix: redact PII before `add`, and set extraction instructions that exclude sensitive categories.","**Stale facts winning retrieval.** Symptom: the agent uses an old address. Fix: rely on the update path (not only add), inspect `history`, and add eval cases where facts change.","**Memory as a substitute for state.** Order ids and workflow progress belong in your database or workflow state, not in fuzzy semantic memory."],"when":"- **Use when:** users return across sessions and expect the agent to remember preferences, facts or\n  ongoing projects.\n- **Avoid when:** sessions are one-off, or the needed context is structured data you can query directly.\n- **Measure:** memory precision (injected memories that were relevant), memory tokens per call, stale-fact\n  error rate, and deletion correctness.\n- **Interviews:** contrast extraction-based memory with self-editing memory, and explain scoping and\n  deletion.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wraps the agent loop with memory search and add calls, scopes ids correctly, redacts PII, and writes multi-session tests.","architect":"Chooses between a memory layer, a stateful agent server and framework-native memory, and sets retention, residency and deletion policy.","fde":"Shows a customer an agent that remembers their preferences across sessions, and explains exactly what is stored and how to delete it."},"check":{"q":"After adding Mem0, your assistant sometimes recommends meat dishes to a user who said last month\nthey turned vegetarian, and costs rose 20%. What do you investigate and change?","a":"For the stale fact, inspect `get_all` and `history` for that user: if both \"eats meat\" and\n\"vegetarian\" exist, the update step did not replace the old fact, so improve extraction instructions,\ncheck the conflict handling of your version, and add an eval case where a preference changes. Also check\nretrieval: a limit too low or a poor query may miss the newer fact. For cost, memory extraction adds an\nLLM call per `add`; run it asynchronously, use a cheaper extraction model, and cap injected memories to a\nhandful of short bullets."},"quiz":[{"q":"In Mem0, what happens when you call add with a new conversation turn?","options":["The raw messages are appended to a log with no processing","An LLM extracts facts and the library adds, updates or deletes memories so they stay consistent","The vector store is rebuilt from scratch","The messages are sent to every user in the tenant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Mem0 runs an extraction and consolidation step, so a changed preference updates or replaces the old memory rather than leaving two contradictory facts side by side."},{"q":"What are Letta's core memory blocks?","options":["Encrypted backups of the agent database","GPU memory reserved for the model","Labelled text blocks kept in the agent's context that the agent can edit with tools","A list of tools the agent may call"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Core memory blocks such as human and persona are always in context, and the agent updates them with memory tools; larger, older data lives in archival and recall memory outside the context."},{"q":"What is the most important control to prevent memory leaking between users?","options":["Using a larger embedding model","Retrieving more memories per query","Running memory extraction synchronously","Scoping every add and search by user and tenant ids derived from authentication"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Memories are only as isolated as their ids. Without a user or tenant scope a search can return another user's facts, so ids must come from authenticated identity and be required at every call."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/code-execution-sandboxes-for-agents","sources":["E2B documentation","Modal documentation (Sandboxes)","Docker documentation and Docker SDK for Python","gVisor documentation","Firecracker documentation"],"why":"- **Generated code is untrusted code.** It comes from a model that read user input, web pages and tool\n  results, any of which can contain injected instructions.\n- **Plain containers share the host kernel.** A kernel exploit or misconfiguration (privileged mode,\n  mounted Docker socket) escapes to the host; microVMs (Firecracker) and user-space kernels (gVisor) add a\n  stronger boundary.\n- **Agents need state across steps.** Data analysis agents load a file, explore, plot and iterate, so a\n  sandbox must persist a session (files and a live interpreter) across tool calls.\n- **Cost and latency matter.** A sandbox per call must start fast; products keep warm pools and snapshot\n  templates so start-up is closer to a second than to a minute (illustrative order of magnitude).","how":"### The agent-side contract\n1. **The model emits code** as a tool call, for example `run_python(code=\"...\")`.\n2. **The tool gets or creates a sandbox** for this session, with limits and no secrets inside.\n3. **It runs the code** with a timeout and captures stdout, stderr, errors and files.\n4. **It returns a bounded result** (truncated output, error text, artifact references), because huge\n   outputs flood the context window.\n5. **It destroys or expires the sandbox** when the session ends or after an idle timeout.\n\n### The three options\n| Option | Isolation | Model | Strengths | Costs |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| E2B | Firecracker microVMs, hosted (self-host option exists) | SDK creates sandboxes; code-interpreter SDK runs Jupyter-style cells | fast start, stateful sessions, charts and files returned, built for agents | per-second vendor pricing, data leaves your cloud unless self-hosted |\n| Modal | gVisor-based containers on Modal's cloud | `modal.Sandbox.create(...)` from Python with an image, timeout, network controls | any image, GPUs, same platform as your other Modal jobs | vendor platform, sandboxes run in Modal's cloud |\n| Docker (self-managed) | Linux namespaces and cgroups on your host; add gVisor (`runsc`) or Kata for more | Docker SDK or a job runner starts a container per task | full control, runs on your infra, no vendor | you own hardening, pooling, cleanup and escape risk |\n\n### Hardening checklist for any option\n| Control | Why |\n|---|---|\n| No network, or an egress allowlist | blocks data exfiltration and internal service calls |\n| No secrets in the sandbox | injected code can read every environment variable and file |\n| CPU, memory, process and time limits | stops fork bombs, infinite loops and cost runaway |\n| Non-root user, read-only root filesystem, dropped capabilities | shrinks what an escape attempt can use |\n| Never mount the Docker socket or host paths | either one is effectively root on the host |\n| Fresh sandbox per user or tenant | no cross-user files or state |","example":"### A hardened Docker code tool you can run locally\nRequires Docker and `pip install docker`. Each call starts a fresh, offline, resource-capped container.\n\n```python\nimport docker\n\nclient = docker.from_env()\n\ndef run_python(code: str, timeout_s: int = 10) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Tool: run untrusted Python in an isolated container and return bounded output.\"\"\"\n    c = client.containers.run(\n        \"python:3.12-slim\", [\"python\", \"-c\", code],\n        detach=True, network_disabled=True, mem_limit=\"256m\", nano_cpus=500_000_000,\n        pids_limit=64, read_only=True, user=\"65534:65534\", cap_drop=[\"ALL\"],\n        security_opt=[\"no-new-privileges\"],\n    )\n    try:\n        status = c.wait(timeout=timeout_s)          # raises on timeout\n        out = c.logs(stdout=True, stderr=True).decode(errors=\"replace\")\n        return {\"exit_code\": status[\"StatusCode\"], \"output\": out[-4000:]}  # keep the tail only\n    except Exception as e:\n        return {\"exit_code\": None, \"output\": f\"timeout or error: {type(e).__name__}\"}\n    finally:\n        c.remove(force=True)                        # force kills a still-running container first\n\nprint(run_python(\"print(sum(range(10)))\"))\nprint(run_python(\"import urllib.request; urllib.request.urlopen('http://example.com')\"))  # fails: no network\nprint(run_python(\"while True: pass\", timeout_s=3))                                         # killed\n```\n\n### Hosted sandboxes: E2B and Modal for a data-analysis agent\nBoth snippets show the core calls; method names have changed between major SDK versions, so check the\ncurrent docs.\n\n```python\n# E2B: pip install e2b-code-interpreter, E2B_API_KEY set\nfrom e2b_code_interpreter import Sandbox\n\nsbx = Sandbox.create(timeout=300)              # older SDKs used Sandbox(); stateful for this session\nsbx.files.write(\"/home/user/sales.csv\", open(\"sales.csv\", \"rb\").read())\nex = sbx.run_code(\"import pandas as pd; df = pd.read_csv('/home/user/sales.csv'); df.describe()\")\nprint(ex.logs.stdout, ex.error, ex.results)    # results can include rich outputs such as charts\nex2 = sbx.run_code(\"df['revenue'].sum()\")      # same interpreter: df still exists\nsbx.kill()\n\n# Modal: pip install modal, modal token set\nimport modal\napp = modal.App.lookup(\"agent-sandboxes\", create_if_missing=True)\nsb = modal.Sandbox.create(app=app, image=modal.Image.debian_slim().pip_install(\"pandas\"),\n                          timeout=120, block_network=True)\np = sb.exec(\"python\", \"-c\", \"print(2 + 2)\")\nprint(p.stdout.read())\nsb.terminate()\n```\n\n| Need | Best fit |\n|---|---|\n| Stateful notebook-style analysis with charts | E2B code interpreter |\n| Custom images, GPUs, or jobs already on Modal | Modal Sandboxes |\n| Data must stay on your infrastructure | Docker with gVisor or Kata, or a self-hosted product |","patterns":["**One sandbox per session, killed on idle.** Map agent session id to sandbox id, reuse it across tool calls, and set an idle timeout so abandoned sessions stop billing. Monitor live sandbox count and average lifetime against active sessions.","**Block egress by default, allowlist when needed.** Start with no network; if the agent must install packages, pre-bake them into the image or template instead of opening the internet. Monitor blocked connection attempts as a prompt-injection signal.","**Bound everything that returns to the model.** Truncate stdout to a few thousand characters, return file references instead of contents, and summarize big tables. Monitor tokens added per code-tool call.","**Pre-build images or templates.** Bake common libraries into a custom image or E2B template so start-up stays fast and runs are reproducible. Monitor sandbox start latency p95.","**Add a stronger runtime for self-managed containers.** Run untrusted code under gVisor (`--runtime=runsc`) or Kata Containers on dedicated nodes, never on the host that holds agent secrets. Monitor node-level CPU and memory saturation.","**Log code and outcomes for audit and evals.** Store the executed code, exit code and truncated output with the trace, so you can replay failures and score code-tool success rate."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** A code-interpreter tool turns model output into executed programs.\n  Without isolation, one prompt injection can read secrets, call internal services or delete data on the\n  host running your agent.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** A `run_code` or `execute_python` tool whose implementation creates\n  a sandbox, writes files, runs code with limits, and returns stdout, stderr and artifacts to the model.\n- **What you control.** Isolation level (container, gVisor, microVM), network egress, CPU, memory and\n  time limits, mounted files, installed packages, sandbox lifetime and what output flows back.\n- **What you can skip.** Writing your own hypervisor integration. Pick a product or a hardened container\n  runtime; the threat model is covered in the sandboxing lesson.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Data-analysis agents.** Users upload CSVs; the agent writes pandas code, runs it in a sandbox and\n  returns charts and tables.\n- **Coding agents that run tests.** The agent edits a repository clone inside a sandbox and runs the test\n  suite before proposing a patch.\n- **Spreadsheet and file conversion assistants.** Generated scripts transform documents without touching\n  the host.\n- **Evaluation harnesses.** Execute model-generated code against unit tests safely at scale.","mini_project":"**Build a sandboxed code tool and attack it (2-3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** a `run_python` tool for a small tool-calling agent, backed by Docker (or E2B), that survives\n  hostile code.\n- **Steps:** implement the hardened Docker tool from this lesson; connect it to a tool-calling loop that\n  answers questions about a CSV; write 6 hostile tests (infinite loop, fork bomb, network call, reading env\n  vars, writing to `/`, a 10 MB print); add session reuse with an idle timeout.\n- **Done when:** the agent answers 3 CSV questions correctly, all 6 hostile tests are contained with a clear\n  error, no containers remain after the run, and returned output never exceeds your size cap."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    M[\"Model emits run_python call\"] --> T[\"Code tool in agent\"]\n    T --> P{\"Session sandbox exists?\"}\n    P -->|\"no\"| C[\"Create sandbox: limits, no secrets\"]\n    P -->|\"yes\"| R[\"Reuse sandbox\"]\n    C --> X[\"Execute with timeout\"]\n    R --> X\n    X --> O[\"stdout, stderr, files\"]\n    O --> B[\"Truncate and summarize\"]\n    B --> M\n    X -.->|\"egress blocked\"| N[\"Internal network\"]\n    T -->|\"session end or idle\"| K[\"Kill sandbox\"]","caption":"Follow the tool call: the agent never runs code itself; a sandbox with limits does, and only a bounded\nresult returns to the model."},"interview":[{"q":"Why is a plain Docker container not enough isolation for agent-generated code?","a":"- **It shares the host kernel.** A kernel vulnerability or misconfiguration (privileged mode, mounted\n  socket, broad capabilities) lets code escape to the host.\n- **Stronger options exist.** gVisor intercepts syscalls in user space and Firecracker or Kata microVMs\n  give each sandbox its own kernel, so escapes need a much harder exploit."},{"q":"How would you design the run_code tool for a data-analysis agent?","a":"- **Session-scoped sandbox.** Create a sandbox per agent session, upload the user's files, and reuse the\n  interpreter across calls so variables persist.\n- **Limits and bounded results.** Apply timeouts and resource caps, return truncated stdout, errors and\n  file references, and kill the sandbox on idle or session end."},{"q":"Your sandbox bill tripled last month. What do you look at?","a":"- **Lifetime versus activity.** Compare sandbox minutes with active session minutes; a gap means missing\n  idle timeouts or cleanup failures.\n- **Start-up and retries.** Check whether failed runs are retried in fresh sandboxes, and whether images\n  install packages at start-up instead of being pre-built."},{"q":"When would you choose self-managed sandboxes over E2B or Modal?","a":"- **Data residency and control.** When code and data must stay in your VPC or on-premises, or a customer\n  forbids third-party execution.\n- **Accepting the cost.** You then own gVisor or Kata setup, pooling, cleanup and patching, so it is worth\n  it only when the requirement is real."},{"q":"How do you stop a prompt-injected agent from exfiltrating data through the sandbox?","a":"- **No secrets and no egress.** Keep credentials outside, disable network or allowlist specific hosts, and\n  give the sandbox only the data the task needs.\n- **Detect and audit.** Log executed code and blocked connections, alert on egress attempts, and include\n  injection cases in your eval suite."}],"tradeoffs":["**Hosted vs self-managed.** E2B and Modal remove isolation and pooling work but send code and data to a vendor; Docker keeps data in-house but makes you responsible for escapes and cleanup.","**Stronger isolation vs speed and cost.** MicroVMs and gVisor add a boundary at some start-up and syscall overhead; plain containers are faster but rely on the shared kernel.","**Stateful sessions vs hygiene.** Reusing a sandbox keeps variables and files for iterative analysis, but long-lived sandboxes accumulate state and cost; cap lifetime.","**Network access vs safety.** Internet access lets agents fetch data and install packages but opens exfiltration paths; the allowlist is the compromise."],"pitfalls":["**Running generated code in the agent process.** `exec()` or a subprocess on the agent host exposes every secret and network route. Fix: always cross a sandbox boundary.","**Mounting the Docker socket for convenience.** Symptom: a container can start privileged containers. Fix: never mount it; use a separate job runner with its own credentials.","**Passing API keys into the sandbox.** Injected code prints or sends them. Fix: keep credentials in the agent's tools outside the sandbox, and hand the sandbox only data.","**Leaking sandboxes.** Symptom: bills climb with abandoned sessions. Fix: timeouts, `finally` cleanup and a reaper job that kills sandboxes past their lifetime."],"when":"- **Use when:** agents analyse data, generate charts, transform files, test code or run user-provided\n  scripts.\n- **Avoid when:** a fixed function tool can do the job; giving the model a general interpreter is far more\n  power than calculating a total needs.\n- **Measure:** code-tool success rate, sandbox start latency, sandbox minutes per session, blocked egress\n  attempts and tokens returned per call.\n- **Interviews:** explain why containers alone are a weak boundary, and walk through the hardening list.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the code tool with limits, timeouts, output truncation and cleanup, and tests it with hostile inputs such as infinite loops and network calls.","architect":"Chooses hosted microVMs, Modal or self-managed gVisor based on data residency, cost and isolation needs, and keeps secrets outside every sandbox.","fde":"Explains to a customer's security team where generated code runs, what it can reach, and how sandboxes are destroyed, with the hardening checklist as evidence."},"check":{"q":"Your data-analysis agent runs model-written Python with `subprocess` on the same server as the\nagent, which holds a database password in an environment variable. Security asks you to fix it this\nsprint. What do you change?","a":"Move execution across an isolation boundary: an E2B or Modal sandbox, or a self-managed container\nunder gVisor on separate nodes. Inside it, no secrets and no network by default, CPU, memory, process and\ntime limits, a non-root user and a read-only filesystem. Keep the database credential in a separate,\nnarrow query tool outside the sandbox and pass only result data in. Reuse one sandbox per session with an\nidle timeout, truncate output returned to the model, and log executed code for audit. Test with\ninjection cases that try to read env vars or call the network."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change most reduces risk when an agent runs generated code?","options":["Using a larger model that writes better code","Running the code in an isolated sandbox with no secrets and no network by default","Asking the model to promise not to call the network","Running the code as root so it never fails on permissions"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Generated code can be steered by injected instructions, so the boundary must be enforced by isolation, no secrets and blocked egress, not by the model's own judgment."},{"q":"Why do agent sandboxes often persist across several tool calls in one session?","options":["Persistent sandboxes are always cheaper than fresh ones","Sandboxes cannot be created more than once per user","Iterative analysis needs files and interpreter state such as loaded data frames to survive between calls","It removes the need for timeouts"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A data-analysis agent loads data in one call and explores it in the next. A session-scoped sandbox keeps that state, with an idle timeout so it does not live forever."},{"q":"What is the main reason to truncate sandbox output before returning it to the model?","options":["Models cannot read program output","Truncation encrypts the output","Sandboxes cannot return more than one line","Large outputs consume the context window and cost tokens without helping the next step"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Printing a whole data frame can add thousands of tokens to every later step. Returning a tail, an error summary or a file reference keeps the loop cheap and focused."}]},{"key":"agent-frameworks/end-to-end-agent-stack","sources":["OpenTelemetry documentation (GenAI semantic conventions)","LiteLLM documentation","pgvector documentation","Langfuse documentation","LangGraph documentation"],"why":"- **Framework demos hide production needs.** A notebook agent has no auth, no persistence, no cost limits\n  and no regression tests; each missing layer becomes an incident.\n- **Model access must be centralized.** Teams call several providers; without a gateway, keys, rate\n  limits, fallbacks and spend tracking are scattered across services.\n- **Agents are hard to debug without traces.** A wrong answer at step 9 can come from a retrieval miss at\n  step 2; only a trace tree linking every model, tool and retrieval call shows it.\n- **Prompt and model changes regress silently.** An eval gate in CI and online scoring catch quality\n  drops that unit tests miss.\n- **Agents are long-running and stateful.** Sessions, checkpoints and human approvals need durable\n  storage and sometimes a workflow engine, not process memory.","how":"### The reference stack\n| Layer | Job | Default pick | Alternatives and when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Framework | agent loop, tools, state, human-in-the-loop | LangGraph (Python) or Mastra (TypeScript) | ADK on Google Cloud, OpenAI Agents SDK or Claude Agent SDK for one provider, Pydantic AI for typed outputs |\n| LLM gateway | one API to many models, keys, budgets, fallbacks, caching | LiteLLM proxy | managed gateways from cloud or CDN vendors when you want no ops |\n| Vector store | retrieval for RAG and memory | Postgres with pgvector | Qdrant, Weaviate or a managed service at larger scale or heavy filtering |\n| Tracing | spans for every model, tool and retrieval call | OpenTelemetry SDK exporting to Langfuse | LangSmith if you live in LangChain, Phoenix, or your existing APM |\n| Evals | offline gates and online scoring | a dataset plus harness in CI, LLM judges with rubrics | framework-native eval runners (ADK, Mastra), vendor eval suites |\n| Deployment | serving, state, scaling | container on a managed platform plus Postgres for sessions | a workflow engine (Temporal, Inngest) for long or approval-heavy jobs, Kubernetes at scale |\n\n### How the pieces connect\n1. **Request in.** The API authenticates the user and creates or loads a session id and a trace id,\n   because every later layer keys off them.\n2. **Framework runs the loop.** It loads state from Postgres, calls the model through the gateway, and\n   calls tools.\n3. **Gateway routes the model call.** It applies per-team budgets, retries and fallbacks, and tags the\n   call with user and session metadata for cost reports.\n4. **Retrieval tool queries the vector store** with tenant filters, returning chunks with source ids.\n5. **Every step emits a span** into one trace, so the trace shows model calls, tokens, tool latency and\n   retrieved ids.\n6. **Evals read traces and datasets.** Offline evals run the agent in CI on a fixed set; online scorers\n   sample production traces.\n7. **State is checkpointed** after each step, so a crash or a human approval resumes instead of restarting.\n\n### Decision rules\n- **Fewest moving parts that meet the requirement.** Each extra vendor adds a contract, a bill and an\n  outage domain.\n- **Open standards at the seams.** OpenAI-compatible APIs at the gateway and OpenTelemetry for traces\n  let you swap vendors without rewriting agents.\n- **Put durability where waits are long.** Seconds-long agent runs fit a web service; hours-long runs with\n  approvals belong in a workflow engine or a framework with checkpoints.","example":"### The seams in code: gateway, retrieval and tracing around one agent step\nFramework-neutral on purpose: the OpenAI client points at a LiteLLM proxy, retrieval uses pgvector, and\nOpenTelemetry spans wrap each call. Endpoints, model alias and table are illustrative.\n\n```python\nimport psycopg\nfrom openai import OpenAI\nfrom opentelemetry import trace\n\ntracer = trace.get_tracer(\"support-agent\")\nllm = OpenAI(base_url=\"http://litellm-proxy:4000\", api_key=\"team-virtual-key\")  # gateway, not provider\ndb = psycopg.connect(\"postgresql://app@db/agent\")\n\ndef retrieve(tenant_id: str, query_vec: list[float], k: int = 5) -> list[tuple]:\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"tool.retrieve\") as span:\n        rows = db.execute(\n            \"SELECT id, content FROM chunks WHERE tenant_id = %s \"\n            \"ORDER BY embedding <=> %s::vector LIMIT %s\",\n            (tenant_id, str(query_vec), k),\n        ).fetchall()\n        span.set_attribute(\"retrieval.doc_ids\", [str(r[0]) for r in rows])  # OTel takes str/int lists only\n        return rows\n\ndef answer(session_id: str, tenant_id: str, question: str, query_vec: list[float]) -> str:\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.step\") as span:\n        span.set_attribute(\"session.id\", session_id)\n        docs = retrieve(tenant_id, query_vec)\n        context = \"\\n\".join(f\"[{d[0]}] {d[1]}\" for d in docs)\n        resp = llm.chat.completions.create(\n            model=\"support-default\",                       # alias defined in the gateway config\n            messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": f\"Answer only from:\\n{context}\"},\n                      {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}],\n            extra_body={\"metadata\": {\"session_id\": session_id, \"tenant\": tenant_id}},\n        )\n        span.set_attribute(\"gen_ai.usage.input_tokens\", resp.usage.prompt_tokens)   # GenAI semconv\n        span.set_attribute(\"gen_ai.usage.output_tokens\", resp.usage.completion_tokens)\n        return resp.choices[0].message.content\n# Configure an OTLP exporter to Langfuse or another backend at startup; check each tool's current docs\n# for the exact metadata fields the gateway records.\n```\n\n### Worked scenario: picking the stack for a support agent\nA B2B SaaS team of 4 engineers, Python backend, 30 tenants, about 20k agent conversations a month\n(illustrative numbers).\n\n| Decision | Choice | Reason | Revisit when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Framework | LangGraph | explicit graph, checkpoints for refund approvals | team moves to TypeScript |\n| Gateway | LiteLLM proxy | two providers, per-tenant budgets, fallback on outages | ops burden exceeds a managed gateway's cost |\n| Vector store | pgvector in the existing Postgres | 2M chunks, tenant filter in SQL, one backup story | p95 retrieval latency or filtered recall degrades |\n| Tracing | OTel to self-hosted Langfuse | customer data stays in their VPC | trace volume outgrows the instance |\n| Evals | 150-case dataset in CI plus 5% sampled online judging | blocks prompt and model regressions | new intents appear in traffic |\n| Deployment | containers plus Postgres checkpoints | runs last under a minute, approvals via checkpoints | approvals stretch to days across systems |","patterns":["**Propagate one trace and session id everywhere.** Create them at the API, pass them to the framework, the gateway metadata and tool calls, and log them with errors. Monitor the share of model calls with no parent trace; it should be zero.","**Route all model traffic through the gateway.** Use model aliases (for example `support-default`) so a model swap is a config change, with per-team virtual keys and budgets. Monitor spend per alias and fallback rate.","**Gate deploys on evals.** Run the eval dataset on every prompt, model or tool change and fail CI on a score drop beyond a threshold; sample live traces for online scoring. Track scores per release.","**Keep tenant isolation in every data layer.** Tenant filters in vector queries, tenant-scoped sessions and memory, and tenant tags on traces. Test cross-tenant retrieval in CI.","**Set budgets at three levels.** Max steps per run in the framework, `max_tokens` on every model call plus token and request rate limits per key at the gateway, and spend limits per key (LiteLLM virtual keys take a max budget). Alert on runs that hit the step cap.","**Checkpoint state outside the process.** Store sessions and checkpoints in Postgres (or the framework's durable store) so deploys, crashes and human approvals resume safely. Monitor resumed-run success."],"agent":"- **Why it matters for agents.** Most agent failures in production are not model failures: a missing\n  trace, an unpinned model, a lost session or an unevaluated prompt change. The stack decides whether you\n  can see, reproduce and fix them.\n- **Where it shows up in your stack.** Framework (LangGraph, ADK, Mastra, OpenAI Agents SDK, Pydantic AI),\n  gateway (LiteLLM proxy or a managed AI gateway), vector store (pgvector, Qdrant and others), tracing\n  (OpenTelemetry into Langfuse, LangSmith or Phoenix), evals (a harness in CI plus online scoring), and\n  runtime (containers, a database for state, a queue or workflow engine).\n- **What you control.** One choice per layer, the contracts between layers (ids, schemas, trace context),\n  and which layer owns retries, budgets and approvals.\n- **What you can skip.** Buying a platform for every layer on day one. Start with one service, Postgres\n  with pgvector, a gateway and traces, then add pieces when a measured need appears.","projects":{"use_cases":"- **Taking a prototype to production.** Map what the demo lacks (gateway, traces, evals, durable state)\n  and add each with a default choice.\n- **Architecture reviews before launch.** Use the six layers as a checklist and ask for a reason and a\n  revisit trigger per choice.\n- **Multi-team agent platforms.** A shared gateway, tracing backend and eval service let several teams\n  ship agents with common controls.\n- **Customer deployments in a VPC.** Swap managed layers for self-hosted ones (LiteLLM, Langfuse,\n  pgvector) to meet data residency.","mini_project":"**Assemble a minimal production stack around one agent (3 hours).**\n- **Goal:** run a small RAG support agent through a LiteLLM proxy, with pgvector retrieval, OpenTelemetry\n  traces in Langfuse, and an eval gate.\n- **Steps:** start Postgres with pgvector, a LiteLLM proxy with one model alias, and Langfuse locally;\n  load 50 document chunks with a tenant column; build the agent step from this lesson with spans for\n  retrieval and the model call; write 15 eval cases and a script that fails when accuracy drops below a\n  threshold.\n- **Done when:** one request shows a full trace with retrieval doc ids and token counts, the gateway logs\n  cost by session, swapping the model alias needs no code change, and a deliberately broken prompt makes\n  the eval script fail."},"exercise":null,"mastery_test":null,"diagram":{"code":"flowchart TD\n    U[\"Client\"] --> API[\"API: auth, session id, trace id\"]\n    API --> F[\"Agent framework loop\"]\n    F --> ST[(\"Postgres: sessions, checkpoints\")]\n    F --> G[\"LLM gateway\"]\n    G --> P[\"Model providers\"]\n    F --> T[\"Tools\"]\n    T --> V[(\"Vector store with tenant filter\")]\n    F -.->|\"OTel spans\"| O[\"Tracing backend\"]\n    G -.->|\"cost and tokens\"| O\n    O -.->|\"sampled traces\"| E[\"Online evals\"]\n    CI[\"CI eval suite\"] -.->|\"gate deploys\"| API","caption":"Follow one request through the layers; the dotted lines are the trace and eval paths that make the rest\ndebuggable."},"interview":[{"q":"Walk me through the production stack you would use for a new agent, layer by layer.","a":"- **Six layers.** Framework for the loop, gateway for models, vector store for retrieval, tracing, evals\n  and deployment with durable state.\n- **Defaults with reasons.** For example LangGraph, a LiteLLM proxy, pgvector, OpenTelemetry into Langfuse,\n  a CI eval suite, and containers with Postgres checkpoints, each with a stated revisit trigger."},{"q":"Why put an LLM gateway in front of providers instead of calling SDKs directly?","a":"- **Central control.** Keys, budgets, rate limits, retries and fallbacks live in one place, and model\n  aliases make swaps a config change.\n- **Visibility.** Every call is logged with cost and metadata such as session and tenant, which is how you\n  answer what a feature costs."},{"q":"Your agent's cost per conversation doubled after a release. How do you find out why?","a":"- **Compare traces.** Look at steps per run, tokens per call and which model alias each call used, before\n  and after the release.\n- **Check the usual causes.** A prompt that loops more, larger retrieved context, a fallback to a pricier\n  model, or broken prompt caching; fix and add a budget alert."},{"q":"When would you move from framework checkpoints to a workflow engine?","a":"- **Long waits and many systems.** When runs wait hours or days for approvals or external events and\n  span several services, a workflow engine gives stronger durability and visibility.\n- **Not before.** For runs under a few minutes, framework checkpoints in Postgres are simpler and enough."},{"q":"How do you keep tenants isolated across the stack?","a":"- **In every store.** Tenant filters on vector queries, tenant-scoped sessions and memory, and per-tenant\n  keys or metadata at the gateway.\n- **Tested, not assumed.** CI tests that try cross-tenant retrieval and session access, and traces tagged\n  by tenant so leaks are detectable."}],"tradeoffs":["**Best-of-breed vs integrated platform.** Separate tools for each layer give flexibility and avoid lock-in but cost integration work; an integrated platform ships faster until you outgrow it.","**Self-hosted vs managed.** Self-hosting the gateway and tracing keeps data in your VPC but adds on-call work; managed services flip that, and data residency usually decides.","**Postgres for everything vs specialized stores.** One database is simpler to operate and back up; specialized vector stores win at large scale or heavy filtered search.","**Workflow engine vs framework checkpoints.** Engines give strong durability and visibility for long jobs, at the cost of another system; framework checkpoints are enough for short runs."],"pitfalls":["**Calling providers directly from each service.** Symptom: no spend visibility, keys in many places, and an outage takes everything down. Fix: one gateway with aliases and fallbacks.","**Tracing only the model call.** Symptom: you see a bad answer but not the retrieval or tool step that caused it. Fix: spans for tools and retrieval, linked in one trace.","**Evals as a one-time spreadsheet.** Symptom: quality drifts after prompt tweaks. Fix: a versioned dataset run automatically in CI and grown from production failures.","**Picking every layer before the first user.** Symptom: weeks of integration, no feedback. Fix: start with the default stack, measure, and swap a layer only when a metric says so."],"when":"- **Use when:** you are taking an agent from prototype to production, or reviewing an architecture for\n  gaps before launch.\n- **Avoid when:** you are still testing whether the use case works; a notebook and a provider SDK are\n  enough until then.\n- **Measure:** task success on the eval set, cost per conversation, p95 latency per step, trace coverage\n  and incident time to root cause.\n- **Interviews:** draw the six layers, name a default for each with a reason, and say what would make you\n  change it.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Wires trace and session ids through the framework, gateway and tools, and keeps the eval suite running in CI.","architect":"Picks one option per layer with a written reason and a revisit trigger, and owns the seams: OpenAI-compatible APIs, OpenTelemetry and tenant isolation.","fde":"Adapts the reference stack to a customer's cloud, data residency and existing tools, and explains which layers can reuse what they already run."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports that the agent gave a wrong refund amount yesterday. With your stack, what do you\ndo in the first 30 minutes, and what stack gap would make it impossible?","a":"Find the conversation by session id, open its trace, and walk the spans: which documents retrieval\nreturned (by id, with tenant), what the model saw and produced through the gateway (model alias, version,\ntokens), and which tool returned the amount. That shows whether the fault was retrieval, the tool, or the\nmodel. Then add the case to the eval dataset and fix. The gaps that make this impossible: no trace linking\ntool and retrieval spans to the model call, no session id carried through layers, or calls made\ndirectly to providers with no gateway log of the exact model used."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main benefit of routing all model calls through an LLM gateway?","options":["It makes every model respond faster","Keys, budgets, fallbacks and cost logging are centralized, and model swaps become configuration","It removes the need for evals","It stores the agent's conversation state"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A gateway gives one place for credentials, limits, retries and spend tracking, and aliases decouple agent code from specific provider models."},{"q":"Why should every layer carry the same trace and session ids?","options":["Providers require them for billing","They compress the prompt","They replace authentication","So one trace links the model, tool and retrieval steps of a run and you can find the root cause"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A wrong answer often starts in retrieval or a tool. Shared ids let you open one trace and follow the run across the framework, gateway and stores."},{"q":"Which is a reasonable default vector store for a small team already running Postgres?","options":["A new specialized vector database cluster on day one","An in-memory list in the agent process","pgvector in the existing Postgres, with tenant filters in SQL","The LLM gateway's cache"],"answer":2,"explanation":"pgvector keeps vectors next to the rest of the data with one backup and access story; move to a specialized store when scale or filtered-recall metrics demand it."}]}]});
